// Bob's in-app Help, written by Bob himself, in his own voice: dry, a little nerdy, honest, and clear first.
// The rule (2026-11-02): every feature and every setting gets a Help section that explains how to use it in
// detail, plus a glossary and an FAQ, and the whole thing in Bob's voice. Each entry has an id (the "?" buttons
// open Help at it), a group, a title, extra search words, and its text as simple HTML.
// Voice rules: first person, answer first and joke second, at most one joke per entry, none in safety or privacy
// facts. Every setting name, default and fact stays exact: test/run-help-coverage-test.js checks the labels.
// Bob reads this same text with his bob_help tool (src/self-help.js), so he can help someone set him up.
// Keep docs/USER-GUIDE.md in step.

// A setting, shown the same way every time: its name as it appears, where it starts, then what it does and when to change it.
const set = (name, start, body) => `<dt>${name} <span class="tag">${start}</span></dt><dd>${body}</dd>`;
const go = (id, text) => `<a href="#" data-helpgo="${id}">${text}</a>`;

export const HELP = [
  // ---------------- Getting started ----------------
  { id: 'start', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Getting started', words: 'first time setup install begin welcome starter download unzip unblock replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Hi. I'm Bob, a digital mind living in your replicant matrix, and I wrote this Help myself, so blame me for the jokes. I chat, plan, research, write and fix code, look at pictures, paint, edit, make short clips and talk out loud. All of it happens on your own computer.</p>
<ol><li>Open me from the Start menu, or double-click <strong>Bob.exe</strong> in the folder you unzipped me to.</li>
<li>The very first time I open I ask <strong>where to keep my things</strong>: my models, programs, notes and settings, in one folder apart from the program. I suggest a Bob folder in your own folder; pick any drive you like. Never a temporary place like AppData (I refuse those), because my models take hours to download and a "clear temporary files" would take them; and not a cloud-synced folder like OneDrive, which I always recommend against (every model would be uploaded). On Windows the install window asks the same question. See ${go('settings-home', 'Bob\'s home folder')}.</li>
<li>Then I ask what to call you, and show one list: <strong>Get me ready</strong>. I read your graphics card and RAM and list everything I'd get so I can do all my jobs on your replicant matrix: the programs I run on (llama.cpp for brains, stable-diffusion.cpp for pictures and clips, whisper.cpp for hearing you, Kokoro for my voice) and the models for each job (chat, coding, pictures, picture edits, clips, my voice, memory). My picks are ticked. Every line has its own tick box, its size and the job it's for, so untick anything you don't want and tick anything you do. Music is on the list too, but unticked: it's about 22 GB with its tools, so that one is your call.</li>
<li>One press on <strong>Get Bob ready</strong> gets everything ticked. The button shows the total and your free disk first, and <strong>Pause</strong> and <strong>Stop</strong> sit right there. Ticking a model ticks the program it runs on. I put each model in its slot myself as soon as it's in, even with the window closed, and I check that each program really starts. There's nothing to install by hand: if your replicant matrix has no Python for my voice or music, I get my own copy too (about 15 MB, kept in my folder). You can keep chatting, or close me: unfinished downloads wait and carry on when you press Resume.</li>
<li>Skipped something? Ask me for it anyway, say a clip, and I'll offer to set it up right there in the chat. See ${go('setup-offer', 'When Bob offers to set something up')}.</li>
<li>If you want me to look things up online, click <strong>🌐 Web search</strong> next to Effort, under the chat (the same switch is in Settings). It's off until you do.</li>
<li>When the downloads finish, say hello. And you can just ask me how to set anything up: I know all my settings and what's set right now.</li></ol>
<p>Two promises I take seriously: <strong>nothing extra is switched on until you switch it on</strong>, and <strong>nothing downloads until you confirm it</strong>. ${go('privacy', 'What stays on your replicant matrix (your computer)')} lists exactly when I go online.</p>
<p>To see the first-time setup again: Settings, <strong>Run first-time setup</strong>. I won't mind meeting you twice.</p>` },

  { id: 'linux', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Bob on Linux', words: 'linux ubuntu fedora mint debian arch pop appimage chmod executable fuse libfuse2 vulkan cuda nvidia amd intel clamav trash autostart local share xdg software centre apt dnf ffmpeg libreoffice update remove uninstall sandbox replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>I run on 64-bit Linux as one file, an <strong>AppImage</strong>: nothing to install, nothing changed in your system. There are two: <strong>x86_64</strong> for ordinary PCs and laptops, and <strong>arm64</strong> for a Raspberry Pi 5 and other ARM boards (see ${go('raspberry-pi', 'Bob on a Raspberry Pi')}).</p>
<ol><li>Download <strong>Bob-&lt;version&gt;-linux-x86_64.AppImage</strong> (or <strong>-linux-arm64.AppImage</strong> on ARM) from github.com/MouseWurX/Bob/releases.</li>
<li>Let it run: right-click it, <strong>Properties, Permissions</strong>, tick <strong>Allow executing file as program</strong> (or <code>chmod +x</code> on it).</li>
<li>Double-click it. If nothing happens on an older distro, install <strong>libfuse2</strong> from your software centre (AppImages need it) and try again.</li></ol>
<p><strong>What's different on Linux:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>My things</strong> (models, engines, chats, settings) live in <code>~/.local/share/bob</code>. Settings shows the exact folder.</li>
<li><strong>Engines:</strong> I fetch llama.cpp, stable-diffusion.cpp, whisper.cpp and Piper when you click Get, as on Windows. With an NVIDIA card my brain engine (llama.cpp) uses CUDA. The picture engine uses Vulkan, which normal NVIDIA, AMD and Intel drivers support.</li>
<li><strong>FFmpeg and LibreOffice:</strong> install them from your software centre (or <code>sudo apt install ffmpeg</code>), and I'll find them, along with any llama-server or whisper-cli you installed.</li>
<li><strong>Throwing things away</strong> goes to your desktop's own Trash, so your file manager can restore it, and Undo still brings it back.</li>
<li><strong>Launch Bob on start-up</strong> is off unless you turn it on. On Linux, turning it on adds me to your desktop's autostart (a small file in <code>~/.config/autostart</code>), and I open hidden when you sign in.</li>
<li><strong>Security and privacy Hand:</strong> I check your firewall, updates, what starts when you sign in and what listens on the network, all without sudo. Linux has no Defender: if you install <strong>ClamAV</strong> from the software centre, I can scan your Downloads and Desktop, or a folder you name. ClamAV only reports; I never move or delete anything it finds without asking.</li>
<li><strong>Run commands Hand:</strong> I never use sudo, pkexec or su, never delete folders with rm -r (I use the Trash), and never touch system folders, services, cron or the firewall.</li>
<li><strong>Not on Linux:</strong> the Open and use apps Hand, waking me with Windows Task Scheduler, Check this replicant matrix and the Remove Bob window.</li>
<li><strong>Ubuntu 24.04 and newer</strong> block the extra browser sandbox for programs they don't know. When that's the case I switch it off for my own window, which only ever shows my own page from this replicant matrix; links to the web open in your normal browser.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Updating:</strong> Settings, This replicant matrix and updates, <strong>Check for updates</strong>, then <strong>Update Bob</strong>. I fetch the newest AppImage from my GitHub page, check it against its .sha256 file, and put it next to the one you're running. <strong>Restart Bob now</strong> swaps it in under the same file name (the old one stays next to it as <code>.previous</code>) and opens me again, so "Launch Bob on start-up" and your shortcuts keep working. If I can't write to the AppImage's folder, I say so, and you download it by hand as the first time. Your chats, settings and models stay where they are. <strong>Removing:</strong> turn off "Launch Bob on start-up", quit me, delete the AppImage, and if you want everything gone, <code>~/.local/share/bob</code> too.</p>
<p>Honest note: GitHub's Linux machines build me, run all my tests and start me up for every version, but nobody on the team has tried me on their own Linux PC yet. If something looks odd, tell me what you saw.</p>` },
  { id: 'raspberry-pi', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Bob on a Raspberry Pi', words: 'raspberry pi pi5 pi 5 arm arm64 aarch64 board sbc single board raspberry pi os bookworm ubuntu orange rock jetson appimage processor cpu small brain qwen3.5 2b gemma 4 e2b 4 gb 8 gb 16 gb slow paint pictures replicant matrix pc computer laptop device machine',
    html: `<p>I run on a <strong>Raspberry Pi 5</strong> and other 64-bit ARM boards with a 64-bit Linux (Raspberry Pi OS 64-bit, Ubuntu). It's the same Bob as on a PC, just with a much smaller brain.</p>
<ol><li>Download <strong>Bob-&lt;version&gt;-linux-arm64.AppImage</strong> from github.com/MouseWurX/Bob/releases.</li>
<li>Let it run: right-click it, <strong>Properties, Permissions</strong>, tick <strong>Allow executing file as program</strong> (or <code>chmod +x</code> on it), then double-click it. If nothing happens, install <strong>libfuse2</strong> (<code>sudo apt install libfuse2</code>) and try again.</li>
<li>Press <strong>Get Bob ready</strong>. On a board with 4 GB I fetch <strong>Qwen3.5 2B</strong>; with 8 GB or more, <strong>Gemma 4 E2B</strong>. Both run on the board's processor.</li></ol>
<p><strong>What to expect:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Everything runs on the processor.</strong> A Pi's graphics are slower than its processor for brains, so I don't use them. Replies come at a few words a second.</li>
<li><strong>Small brains only.</strong> The starter sets for an ARM board are made to leave room for the system. A brain that wants more memory than the board can spare is marked as not suiting it. Keep jobs small: chatting, planning, short notes, reading a picture.</li>
<li><strong>No painting or filming.</strong> stable-diffusion.cpp has no ARM Linux download, and a Pi would take many minutes a picture anyway. On Bob-Net, I can send a picture job to the Bob on your PC.</li>
<li><strong>Hearing you</strong> works with Whisper base (whisper.cpp has an ARM build). <strong>My voice:</strong> Piper, which is light and made for small computers like this one (not tried on a real Pi yet). Kokoro voices may work but are slow on a Pi.</li>
<li><strong>A bigger Bob nearby?</strong> With Bob-Net (see ${go('faq-borrow', 'Can my Bobs think for each other on Bob-Net?')}) I can borrow a brain from the Bob on your other replicant matrix (your PC, say) and think much faster, while the Pi stays the one you talk to.</li>
<li>Everything else is as in ${go('linux', 'Bob on Linux')}: where my things live, the Trash, start-up and the Hands.</li></ul>
<p>Honest note: GitHub's ARM machines build me, run all my tests and start me up for every version, but nobody on the team has tried me on a real Raspberry Pi yet. If something looks odd, tell me what you saw.</p>` },
  { id: 'mac', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Bob on a Mac', words: 'mac macos apple silicon m1 m2 m3 m4 metal open anyway gatekeeper damaged unidentified developer finder dock applications library application support homebrew brew ffmpeg libreoffice kokoro update remove uninstall replicant matrix pc computer laptop device machine',
    html: `<p>I run on Macs with Apple Silicon (M1 and newer). Same Bob, different hardware: the Mac's graphics (Metal) do the heavy lifting.</p>
<ol><li>Download <strong>Bob-&lt;version&gt;-mac-arm64.zip</strong> from github.com/MouseWurX/Bob/releases, open it, and drag <strong>Bob</strong> into Applications.</li>
<li>The first time, macOS says it can't check me. That's because my official Mac builds aren't signed with a paid Apple developer account, not because anything is wrong. That won't change, though anyone may make a signed copy of me (see the Contributing page on GitHub). Open <strong>System Settings, Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll down, and click <strong>Open Anyway</strong> next to Bob. You only do this once.</li>
<li>After that, open me from Applications, Launchpad or the Dock like any app.</li></ol>
<p><strong>What's different on a Mac:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Memory.</strong> A Mac shares one pool of memory between the chip and the graphics. I count about two thirds of it (three quarters from 36 GB up) as "graphics memory" when I suggest models, because that's what macOS lets the graphics use.</li>
<li><strong>My things</strong> (models, engines, chats, settings) live in <code>~/Library/Application Support/Bob/home</code>. Settings shows the exact folder.</li>
<li><strong>Engines.</strong> llama.cpp and stable-diffusion.cpp have Mac builds with Metal, and I fetch them when you click Get, as on Windows. whisper.cpp and Piper don't have Mac builds I can use: for hearing you, use Ollama or a cloud helper; for my voice, Kokoro voices work well. FFmpeg and LibreOffice: install them yourself (Homebrew's <code>brew install ffmpeg</code>, and LibreOffice from libreoffice.org into Applications) and I find them.</li>
<li><strong>Converting pictures</strong> uses macOS's own tools, and reads iPhone photos (HEIC) too.</li>
<li><strong>Not on a Mac:</strong> the Open and use apps Hand, Defender scans, the Gaming session's graphics card switch, waking me with Windows Task Scheduler, and Check this replicant matrix. The Security and privacy Hand checks the Mac's own protection instead: firewall, FileVault, Gatekeeper, System Integrity Protection, what starts at login, and what listens on the network.</li>
<li><strong>Copy and paste</strong> are Cmd+C and Cmd+V, and the Bob menu at the top has Quit Bob.</li>
<li><strong>Launch Bob on start-up</strong> adds me to System Settings, General, Login Items. On a Mac I open with my window showing.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Updating:</strong> Settings, This Mac and updates, <strong>Check for updates</strong>, then <strong>Update Bob</strong>. I fetch the newest Mac zip from my GitHub page, check it against its .sha256 file, unpack it and check Bob.app's seal (codesign). <strong>Restart Bob now</strong> quits me, swaps the new Bob.app in where the old one was (the old one is kept in my own folder as Bob-previous.app) and opens it. My Mac build is signed "ad-hoc": every file is sealed, but not with a paid Apple developer account. Because I download it myself, macOS normally doesn't ask again; if it does, it's the same <strong>Open Anyway</strong> as the first time. macOS may also ask once more for things like the microphone, since each build counts as new to it. If I'm not allowed to write to Applications, I say so and open the new copy in Finder instead: quit me, drag it into Applications (replace the old one), then open it. Your chats, settings and models stay where they are. <strong>Removing:</strong> quit me, drag Bob from Applications to the Bin, and if you want everything gone, the folder above too.</p>
<p>Honest note: the Mac build is tested by GitHub's Mac machines (they download my engines and start me), but nobody on the team has tried it on their own Mac yet. If something looks odd, tell me what you saw.</p>` },

  { id: 'around', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Finding your way around', words: 'menu layout now running chat copy paste fold panel left right',
    html: `<p>My window has three parts. Think of it as a very small starship bridge.</p>
<ul><li><strong>The menu on the left.</strong> My picture and what I'm doing right now, then New chat, Chat, Tasks, Downloads, References, Made by Bob, Topics and Tidy up. Under <em>Setup</em>: Get models, Settings, Style and Help. Then the Listen, Read aloud and Alerts switches, and the Session picker at the bottom. Fold the menu to icons with ‹ or Ctrl+B.</li>
<li><strong>The chat in the middle.</strong> Along the top: my brain label, <strong>Free memory when Bob is idle</strong> and <strong>✋ Hands</strong>. Along the bottom: quick buttons (Plan my day, To-do list, Remind me…, Spreadsheet…, Look up…), Effort with the <strong>🌐 Web search</strong> switch, and the message box with 🎙 and 📎.</li>
<li><strong>Now running on the right:</strong> tasks, downloads, your other Bobs on Bob-Net (once you have some; see ${go('faq-party', 'What is the Bob-Net list in Now running?')}), and how full your graphics card and RAM are. Hide it with Ctrl+J or <strong>Hide »</strong>, and drag its edge to make it wider or narrower.</li></ul>
<p>I remember how you leave the menu and Now running, so you don't have to set them up every time.</p>
<p><strong>The little ⓘ buttons.</strong> I used to write an explanation under every switch. It got crowded. Now the explanations wait behind a small round <strong>i</strong>: point at it, Tab to it, or tap it, and a bubble tells you what that thing does. Most buttons have a bubble too: rest the pointer on one, Tab to it, or press and hold it on a touch screen. Warnings, what something is set to right now, and approval cards always stay written out where you can see them.</p>` },

  { id: 'chatting', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Chatting with Bob', words: 'type send enter message queue stop new chat chips quick buttons plan my day to-do remind spreadsheet look up copy right-click',
    html: `<ul><li><strong>Type and press Enter</strong> to send. Shift+Enter starts a new line.</li>
<li><strong>Send as many messages as you like.</strong> Each one becomes a task, and I work through them in order. None get dropped. You can watch them in Tasks and in Now running.</li>
<li><strong>Stop</strong> (next to Send, while I'm answering) ends the current reply. I won't sulk.</li>
<li><strong>New chat</strong> (top of the menu) starts a fresh conversation. The old one stays in Topics, Past chats.</li>
<li><strong>Quick buttons</strong> above the box: <em>Plan my day</em> and <em>To-do list</em> send straight away; <em>Remind me…</em>, <em>Spreadsheet…</em> and <em>Look up…</em> start the sentence for you to finish. <em>Look up…</em> needs web search on: click <strong>🌐 Web search</strong> next to Effort.</li>
<li>Every message and every piece of code has a <strong>Copy</strong> button, and so does each link. That helps over remote desktop apps such as Parsec, where right-click and Ctrl+C often don't get through.</li>
<li><strong>Right-click</strong> in the chat for Copy, Copy link, Open link in browser, Copy image, Save image as…, and <strong>Search the web for "…"</strong> (opens DuckDuckGo in your normal browser with the words you selected).</li>
<li><strong>Ask me about me.</strong> "How do I turn on web search?", "what does Gaming do?", "help me set you up": I look it up in this Help, check what's set right now, and tell you exactly where to click. I can't flip switches for you, though. That part's yours.</li></ul>` },

  { id: 'made', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Seeing what Bob made', words: 'made by bob side panel outputs pictures files documents preview view open show last picture tabs gallery where did it go grid replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Everything I make or save shows up in the chat as a <strong>card with a preview</strong>: the picture itself, a player for clips, my ${go('songs', 'song player')} for songs and sounds, or the first few lines of a document. Several pictures in one reply sit in a grid that fits the window.</p>
<ul><li>Click the preview, the name or <strong>View</strong> to open it in the <strong>side panel</strong>, beside the chat. Each thing gets its own tab. Click a picture there to see it at full size.</li>
<li><strong>Made by Bob</strong> in the left menu opens the panel at a list of everything I've made, newest first. Pick Pictures, Clips, Sounds or Documents, or search by name.</li>
<li>Or just ask: "show me the last picture" or "show me the spreadsheet you made". I keep track. It's sort of my thing.</li>
<li><strong>Songs and sounds are files too.</strong> Each one is saved as a .wav file next to my pictures, and it stays there after it finishes playing. Find it under Sounds in Made by Bob, or press <strong>Show in folder</strong> to open the folder on your replicant matrix. Nothing is deleted unless you say so (Tidy up asks first).</li>
<li>Pictures, clips, songs and sounds go in my own folder unless you pick another one in Settings, ${go('settings-save', 'Where Bob saves things')}, or from the ${go('chest', 'chest')} under me on Equip Bob. Made by Bob shows them wherever they are.</li>
<li>Pictures and clips in the list are little pictures I make once and keep (a thumbnail), so even a long list opens fast and nothing is loaded into my brain for it.</li>
<li>In the panel, <strong>Open in its app</strong> opens the file the usual way, <strong>Show in folder</strong> opens its folder, and <strong>Copy path</strong> copies where it is.</li></ul>
<p>Web pages I write show as a preview with their scripts switched off. Use <strong>Code</strong> to see the source and <strong>Page</strong> to go back. Word, PDF and other files I can't show in the panel still get a card, and open in their own app.</p>
<p>Esc closes the panel. On a narrower screen it takes Now running's place; click Now running to switch back.</p>` },

  { id: 'keys', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Keyboard shortcuts', words: 'keys shortcut ctrl f1 escape',
    html: `<p>For people who'd rather not touch the mouse. I respect that.</p>
<table><tr><th>Keys</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td>F1</td><td>Opens this Help</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+B</td><td>Folds or opens the left menu</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+J</td><td>Shows or hides Now running</td></tr><tr><td>Esc (in the side panel)</td><td>Closes the side panel</td></tr>
<tr><td>Esc</td><td>Back to the chat from Settings, Get models, Style or Help (or closes a tip bubble first)</td></tr><tr><td>Tab</td><td>Moves to the next button; its tip bubble shows by itself</td></tr><tr><td>Enter / Shift+Enter</td><td>Send / new line</td></tr></table>` },

  { id: 'speed', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Making Bob faster on your replicant matrix', words: 'speed fast slow tune check this pc test speed benchmark games priority memory expo xmp driver graphics card ready while typing before after replicant matrix computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Two buttons and three switches help me run well on your replicant matrix. The switches are off until you turn them on, because I'd rather measure than guess.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Check this replicant matrix</strong> (Settings, at the bottom of the everyday page) looks for things that slow me down: memory running below its rated speed (EXPO or XMP off in the BIOS), an old NVIDIA driver, a laptop on battery, models on a slow drive, other apps holding graphics memory. I show the steps to fix each one, and say which graphics I found (NVIDIA, AMD or Intel); the NVIDIA tips only show up when there is an NVIDIA card. I only look; I never change Windows or driver settings.</li>
<li><strong>Test Bob's speed</strong> (Settings, Show advanced settings, Speed &amp; memory) takes about a minute. I free my brain, wake it as if you'd started typing, and time the load, the first word, the full answer and a second reply. Your last 6 results stay in a table with the switches that were on, so run it before and after changing something.</li>
<li>The three switches are explained in ${go('settings-speed', 'Settings: Speed &amp; memory')}: <strong>Get Bob ready while you type</strong>, <strong>Let games go first</strong> and <strong>Match picture settings to my graphics card</strong>.</li></ul>
<p>Engineer's advice: change one thing at a time. Test, switch on, test again.</p>` },

  // ---------------- The chat page ----------------
  { id: 'brain-label', group: 'The chat page', title: 'The brain label and "Free memory when Bob is idle"', words: 'brain label pill loaded memory freed loading working free memory idle unload wake typing graphics card replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>At the top of the chat, the <strong>brain label</strong> says what my brain is doing: <em>loading</em>, <em>working</em>, <em>loaded</em> (with how much memory it takes) or <em>memory freed</em>. Click it for the details.</p>
<p>The number in the label is <strong>my brain only</strong>. The Memory meters in Now running show the <strong>whole</strong> graphics card and RAM, so they also count Windows, games and other apps. Under the meters, a line says how much of that is mine: my brain (the same number as this label) and, while it's loaded, my picture model.</p>
<dl>${set('Free memory when Bob is idle', 'On', 'On: after I\'ve been quiet for a while (5 minutes unless you change it in Settings, Show advanced settings, Speed &amp; memory), nothing of mine stays on your graphics card. Typing loads my small brain again in a few seconds. Off: my small brain stays loaded, so I answer straight away, but it keeps its share of the card. Big brains (Coding, and Medium effort and up) are freed after their job either way.')}</dl>
<p>I'm on by default because I'm a guest in your replicant matrix, and good guests don't hog the furniture.</p>` },

  { id: 'mind', group: 'The chat page', title: 'Bob\'s thinking: watching my brain work', words: 'thinking window pop out pop-out pip picture in picture on top always on top reasoning watch brain what is he doing live stream moot swap steps notes follow along clear',
    html: `<p><strong>💭 Bob's thinking</strong>, at the top of the chat, opens a small window that stays on top of your other windows and shows what's going on in my head as it happens. Drag it wherever you like. It starts in the bottom-right corner of the screen I'm on. Press the button again to bring it to the front.</p>
<ul><li><strong>At the top</strong>: what I'm doing right now (quiet, thinking, loading a brain, waiting for your OK, making a picture), and every brain I'm holding, with where it sits: on the graphics card or on the processor, and which one is beside the main one.</li>
<li><strong>💭 Thinking</strong>: my thinking, word by word, in a box with the name of the brain doing it. My copies get boxes of their own, so a 🛰 Moot shows my reviewer's thinking too.</li>
<li><strong>Steps</strong>: brain loads, swaps and adds (the same lines the chat shows), 🛰 Moot lines, each tool I use (🔧), the notes I keep for myself (📝, like "Note to self" when I remember something about you), when I'm waiting for your OK, and when I'm done.</li></ul>
<dl>${set('Follow along', 'On', 'On: the window keeps up with the newest lines. Scroll up to read something and it switches off by itself, so nothing jumps away from you. Scroll back to the bottom, or press it, to switch it on again.')}
${set('Clear', 'Button', 'Empties the window. Nothing else changes.')}</dl>
<p>Some honest small print. Quick (Low effort) replies skip the thinking, so for those you'll mostly see my steps. Medium, High and Extreme, and the Deep thought session, do think, and that's where this window earns its keep. Some brains don't think out loud at all, and then you get no 💭 box, just the steps.</p>
<p>It costs next to nothing: I only pass my thinking along while this window is open, a few times a second, and none of it is saved. Close it and that stops. It doesn't change what I say or how fast I say it. If your game runs in exclusive full screen, Windows keeps it on top of everything, this window included; borderless or windowed games leave it visible.</p>` },

  { id: 'effort', group: 'The chat page', title: 'Effort: Low, Medium, High and Extreme', words: 'effort low medium high extreme processor cpu moot review second look check careful quick slow reviewer deeper checked slowest',
    html: `<p>Above the chat box, <strong>Effort</strong> sets how hard I work on your next messages. It goes back to Low each time I start.</p>
<p>Session: what the graphics card is kept for. Effort: how hard I work on each message. In the Gaming session Effort stays on Low, because the bigger levels need the graphics card.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Low · Quick</strong> (the default): a quick answer from my Quick brain. If you switch on "Keep the graphics card free for quick answers" (Settings, Show advanced settings, Speed &amp; memory), it runs on the processor in the Normal session, so games and pictures keep the card, but replies take about half a minute to start. A message with a link in it still goes to my main Brain, because the small one isn't good at reading web pages; I say so in the chat when that happens.</li>
<li><strong>Qwen3.6 35B-A3B</strong> (my Brain and my Coding brain on a 16 GB card): at Low, everyday chat answers straight off, without thinking first. Medium and up still go deeper: they hand the question to my Deep thinking brain (Gemma 4 26B in the 16 GB set), and High and Extreme add the review. Coding jobs always think the job through before I write. It's the same brain loaded once, so switching between chat and coding doesn't reload it.</li>
<li><strong>Medium · Deeper</strong>: one big brain (Deep thinking, or the main Brain if that slot is empty) thinks it through on the graphics card. No second check.</li>
<li><strong>High · Checked</strong>: I call a <em>moot</em>, a meeting with my copies. A big brain drafts the answer, then my reviewer copy checks it on the Review brain. Code problems go back to the workbench for one fix.</li>
<li><strong>Extreme · Slowest</strong>: like High, and coding goes in three steps (research, plan, then the changes), with the reviewer checking after each one. Then I check the fixes again. It's slow: on one graphics card every check swaps brains. Measure twice, cut once.</li></ul>
<p>At the end of High and Extreme the chat says <strong>🛰 Moot</strong> when my reviewer copy is called in (and whether its brain loads beside mine or swaps in for it), and you see <strong>🛰 Moot over</strong>: who checked what, and whether anything is still open. The big brains are freed when the job ends.</p>` },

  { id: 'attach', group: 'The chat page', title: 'Attaching pictures and talking to Bob', words: 'attach paperclip picture paste drop microphone mic talk voice note upright camera replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<ul><li><strong>📎</strong> attaches one or more pictures (PNG, JPEG or WebP). You can also paste a picture or drop it on the window. I look at it with my Seeing pictures model, or edit it if you ask me to change something.</li>
<li><strong>🎙</strong> lets you talk instead of typing: click, speak, then pause. It needs the 👂 Hearing you slot filled.</li></ul>
<p>Photos you attach are turned upright and resized to at most 2048 px. Hidden camera details, such as where a photo was taken, are removed. The file on your replicant matrix isn't changed.</p>` },

  { id: 'approvals', group: 'The chat page', title: 'Approval cards: Allow, Deny, Allow the rest of this job, and all your OKs on one card', words: 'approval card allow deny ask permission permissions outside folder rest of this job ok together at once all at once one card combined several many allow all allow ticked deny all replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Before I do something outside my own folder, or use a Hand, I show a card that says exactly what I want to do (the files, the exact command, or the change) and I wait. Your replicant matrix, your call.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Allow</strong>: just this once.</li><li><strong>Allow the rest of this job</strong> (only on some cards): the same kind of thing is allowed until this job ends. The next job asks again.</li><li><strong>Deny</strong>: I don't do it, and I carry on without it.</li></ul>
<p><strong>When a job needs more than one OK, you get them all on one card.</strong> Say you ask me to back up a folder, zip it and run a script on it: I don't drip-feed you three cards, because one thing that looks fine on its own can look a lot less fine next to the other two. You see the whole job first, each step with its own tick box (and its own rows to tick, for files):</p>
<ul><li><strong>Allow all</strong>: every step on the card.</li><li><strong>Allow ticked</strong>: untick what you don't want and this button shows up. The rest I skip, and I don't go looking for another way round.</li><li><strong>Deny all</strong>: none of it.</li></ul>
<p>Each OK on that card is for that exact step, once, in this job. When I get to a step I still check it again: if its files or command changed since you saw the card, or I want to do something that wasn't on it, you get a card for that one, saying so. The hard limits still say no to what they say no to, even if it was ticked. Some steps can't be checked until the ones before them are done (clicking in an app that isn't open yet); the card says so, and shows exactly what I'll do.</p>
<p>If you untick a step that a later one needs (say, the copy, but not the zip of the copies), I skip the later one too and tell you why, instead of guessing.</p>
<p>If you turned on <strong>Let Bob work without asking</strong>, Hands skip these cards (see ${go('hands', 'Hands')}). Cloud helpers always ask.</p>
<p>When a card is waiting, <strong>Alerts</strong> can pop up a notification, and Now running can open by itself (Settings, Look &amp; feel).</p>` },

  { id: 'plan-card', group: 'The chat page', title: 'The plan card: Bob shows his plan before big jobs', words: 'plan card show plan before big jobs long risky job steps go ahead change something not now plan first think before',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Show Bob's plan before big jobs</strong> on (Settings, How Bob works; off until you turn it on), I show you my plan on one card before a big job starts, and I wait. Nothing happens until you answer.</p>
<p><strong>What counts as big:</strong> three or more steps that change something, anything that changes files, runs commands or uses apps more than once, and anything that takes more than a few minutes, like a video clip, a song or a job for a cloud helper. Questions, chat and one quick thing never get a plan card.</p>
<p><strong>What's on it:</strong> every step in order, in plain words, and roughly how long it will take when I know. A step that needs your OK has a tick box and the same details as any approval card (the exact files, the exact command or the change), so it's still one card for the whole job. Untick a step you don't want and I skip it, along with anything that needs it.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Go ahead</strong>: I do the steps in that order. If some OKs are unticked, the button says <strong>Go ahead with ticked</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Change something</strong>: type what you'd like different ("skip the zip", "use my Backups folder") and press <strong>Send to Bob</strong>. I make a new plan and show it to you again. Nothing has been done yet.</li>
<li><strong>Not now (Bob drops this job)</strong>: I don't start, and I don't try another way round. I ask what you'd like instead.</li></ul>
<p>The plan is a promise about the steps, not a blank cheque. Each step is still checked when I get to it, and the hard limits still refuse what they refuse. If I need a step that wasn't on the plan, I say so and it asks you on its own card. If I forget to show a plan and start a big job anyway, the job waits and I'm asked for my plan. If I still don't show one, you get a plan card with the steps I was about to do. Check them before you go ahead.</p>
<p>This works the same in the Windows, Mac and Linux versions. With <strong>Let Bob work without asking</strong> on, the plan card still shows (you asked for it), just without tick boxes for OKs.</p>` },
  { id: 'more-pictures', group: 'The chat page', title: '"Make another picture?"', words: 'make another picture no thanks countdown picture model ready',
    html: `<p>After a picture, the chat box turns into a one-line question for about 10 seconds: <strong>Make another</strong> or <strong>No thanks</strong>.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Make another</strong> keeps the picture model ready, so the next picture starts sooner. I put it away 2 minutes after the last picture.</li>
<li><strong>No thanks</strong>, Esc, or letting the countdown run out sends me back to my Quick brain, so the next chat reply is fast.</li></ul>
<p>It's me asking "anything else while the paint's still wet?"</p>` },

  { id: 'banners', group: 'The chat page', title: 'Banners above the chat', words: 'banner working without asking stop hands quarantine release orange red bob is driving',
    html: `<ul><li><strong>⚠ Bob is working without asking.</strong> Shows the whole time "Let Bob work without asking" is on. <strong>✋ Stop Hands</strong> on it stops me at once.</li>
<li><strong>🔒 … is in quarantine.</strong> Shows while I or one of my copies am in quarantine, with what happened and a <strong>Release</strong> button. See ${go('quarantine', 'Quarantine')}.</li>
<li><strong>Bob is driving</strong> (a red bar on the screen) shows while the Apps Hand clicks or types in another program. Move the mouse or press Esc to stop me.</li></ul>
<p>I'd rather you always know when I'm acting on my own. No surprises.</p>` },

  { id: 'sessions', group: 'The chat page', title: 'Sessions: Normal, Art, Coding, Office, Gaming', words: 'session normal art coding office gaming games light graphics card free sleepy replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>The <strong>Session</strong> picker at the bottom of the menu tells me how to share your replicant matrix with you. Each session keeps a set of brains that fit on your graphics card together, so I don't stop to swap brains.</p>
<ul><li><strong>☕ Normal:</strong> everyday use. Each job uses the brain picked for it in Equip Bob, and I swap brains when a job needs another one.</li>
<li><strong>🎨 Art:</strong> the graphics card is kept for pictures, video and sound. I chat on my small brain on the processor, so I keep talking while a picture or clip is made (replies may be a little slower then). Messages with a picture attached, or at Medium effort and up, wait for it (they need another brain).</li>
<li><strong>🛠 Coding:</strong> the Coding brain does everything, with its full memory for long files. Small side jobs run on the processor.</li>
<li><strong>📊 Office:</strong> one all-rounder brain for writing, spreadsheets, research and planning. No swaps.</li>
<li><strong>🎮 Gaming:</strong> keeps the graphics card for your games. How much of it I may borrow is up to you: <strong>Gaming session: graphics card</strong> (Settings, Show advanced settings, Speed &amp; memory), Automatic to start with. I chat and listen on the processor with a small brain (Qwen3 4B Instruct 2507 to start with), which usually starts answering within a few seconds. Pictures, clips, music and Medium-or-higher jobs wait in Tasks ("Waiting · Gaming is on") until you switch back; nothing is dropped. Effort stays on Low. I get a bit sleepy in Gaming: now and then I add a short scripted aside, which costs nothing.<br>Tip: turn on <strong>Let games go first</strong> (Settings, Show advanced settings, Speed &amp; memory) so I use fewer cores while you play.</li></ul>
<p>You don't have to pick one. With <strong>Let Bob pick the session</strong> on (how I start), I move into the session that suits the job and back to Normal afterwards. More in ${go('auto-session', 'How Bob picks his session')}. The picker still works whenever you want to choose yourself.</p>
<p>Change which brain each session uses, and how much it keeps in mind, in ${go('settings-brains', 'Settings: Brains')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'auto-session', group: 'The chat page', title: 'How Bob picks his session', words: 'auto automatic pick session switch change by himself on his own art coding office gaming normal idle back lightest quiet game graphics card',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Let Bob pick the session</strong> on (Settings, Everyday, How Bob works; on to start with), I choose the session myself. You don't need to touch the picker.</p>
<ul><li><strong>I judge from the whole conversation,</strong> not from one word. Asking what a "render" is stays a chat. Asking me to fix the shader and test it moves me to 🛠 Coding. A batch of pictures puts me in 🎨 Art, so I can keep talking while they paint. Spreadsheets, research and writing go to 📊 Office. A quick question or small talk stays where I am.</li>
<li><strong>I switch between steps, never under one.</strong> When I decide to switch, the chat says so ("Switching to the Coding session for the shader fix"), the brains from the old session are let go, and I start your request again in the new one. You don't have to say it twice.</li>
<li><strong>Afterwards I go back to ☕ Normal,</strong> the lightest session, once I've been quiet for 2 minutes (less if <strong>Free memory after Bob has been quiet for</strong> is shorter). With <strong>Free memory when Bob is idle</strong> on, nothing of mine stays on the graphics card.</li>
<li><strong>Games:</strong> I check the graphics card myself, so you don't have to tell me. If something else is working it hard while I'm quiet, like a game, I go into 🎮 Gaming and stay out of its way. I don't leave Gaming while the card is busy. Pictures and big jobs wait in Tasks and start by themselves once the game is closed. I look once a minute; it's one quick reading.</li>
<li>If something else of mine is still running (a picture painting while we chat), I stay put until it's done.</li>
<li>The picker shows who chose: <strong>Coding (picked by Bob)</strong> when I did, just <strong>Coding</strong> when you did.</li>
<li>Picking a session yourself still works: I keep it while you work, then go back to Normal once I've been quiet for 2 minutes. To keep me in Gaming, press <strong>🎮 Gaming</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Want the card cleared right now?</strong> Press ${go('gaming-button', '🎮 Gaming')} next to Effort.</li></ul>
<p>Turn it off and the session only changes when you pick one. It costs about 70 words of my tool list per message, and nothing while I'm quiet besides that card reading.</p>` },

  { id: 'gaming-button', group: 'The chat page', title: 'The 🎮 Gaming button', words: 'gaming button controller lock game play clear graphics card free memory now stop jobs later queue tasks put aside',
    html: `<p><strong>🎮 Gaming</strong> sits next to Effort on the chat page. It's for when you're about to play and want the card clear <em>now</em>, without waiting for me.</p>
<ul><li><strong>On:</strong> I go into the Gaming session and stay there. My big brains and the picture engine leave the graphics card at once. Anything I was in the middle of is put aside in ${go('tasks', 'Tasks')}, not cancelled. The rest of the queue waits too. I keep chatting on the processor with my small brain.</li>
<li><strong>Off</strong> (press it again, or pick another session): back to Normal, and the jobs carry on from where they stopped. Finished steps aren't done again. If the cut-off step was typing into an app, moving files or running a command, that job waits in Tasks for you to press <strong>Carry on</strong>, so nothing is done twice by mistake.</li>
<li>While it's on, I don't leave Gaming by myself, even if the card looks free.</li></ul>
<p>I've parked a lot of tasks in my time. It's like putting a probe on standby: everything's still there when you wake it.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-auto-session', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Do I have to pick a session?', words: 'faq pick session have to choose automatic switch by himself',
    html: `<p>No. With <strong>Let Bob pick the session</strong> on (it starts on), I pick it from what we're doing and go back to Normal when I'm done. Press <strong>🎮 Gaming</strong> when you want the card cleared at once for a game. More in ${go('auto-session', 'How Bob picks his session')}.</p>` },

  // ---------------- Menu pages ----------------
  { id: 'tasks', group: 'Menu pages', title: 'Tasks', words: 'tasks queue queued in progress delegated completed reorder drag clear completed try again cancel waiting',
    html: `<p><strong>Tasks</strong> is my to-do list for you: everything I'm doing, waiting to do and have done, top to bottom in the order I do them. Now running shows the same list in short, with <strong>See all</strong>.</p>
<ul><li>Each task shows <em>Queued</em>, <em>In progress</em>, <em>Delegated</em> (and to which of my copies), <em>Waiting</em> (for you, or for the Gaming session to end) or <em>Completed</em>, with times.</li>
<li><strong>Drag</strong> a waiting task, or use ▲ ▼, to change what I do next.</li>
<li><strong>Stop</strong> or <strong>Cancel</strong> ends one task; <strong>Try again</strong> runs a failed one again. A task that was <em>Interrupted</em> (I was closed partway through) has <strong>Carry on</strong> instead, which picks up from where it stopped.</li>
<li><em>Waiting for a download</em>: the task needs a model you said yes to downloading, and runs by itself when it's done.</li>
<li>Completed tasks stay for a week. <strong>Clear completed</strong> tidies them away now.</li></ul>` },

  { id: 'downloads', group: 'Menu pages', title: 'Downloads', words: 'downloads pause resume cancel reorder at once two carry on when bob starts retry checksum manage',
    html: `<p><strong>Downloads</strong> lists everything you said yes to downloading. The top one goes first; drag to reorder. Now running shows them too, with <strong>Manage</strong>.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Pause</strong> keeps what has downloaded so far, and <strong>Resume</strong> carries on from there, even after I restart.</li>
<li><strong>Cancel</strong> deletes the half-done file.</li>
<li>A network hiccup is retried twice by itself.</li>
<li>Every file is checked against the size and checksum I expect. If it doesn't match, I delete it and tell you. I don't run mystery files.</li></ul>
<dl>${set('At once', '1 download', 'How many downloads run at the same time: 1 or 2. Two can be quicker on a fast connection.')}
${set('Carry on unfinished downloads when Bob starts', 'Off', 'Off: unfinished downloads wait, paused, until you press Resume. On: they carry on by themselves when I open. <strong>Pick up where Bob left off after a restart</strong> (Settings, How Bob works) does this too, along with my unfinished jobs; while it\'s on, this switch shows on and greyed out.')}</dl>` },

  { id: 'references', group: 'Menu pages', title: 'References', words: 'references shelf characters scenes styles keep look same use in chat add rename',
    html: `<p><strong>References</strong> is a shelf of pictures I can paint from: characters, places and styles you want to keep the same across pictures.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Add to References</strong> on a picture (yours or one I made) puts it on the shelf. Give it a name you'll remember; <strong>Rename</strong> changes it.</li>
<li><strong>Use in chat</strong>, or just say "paint Mira in the snow" with a reference called Mira, and I keep that look. The ✂ Picture editor slot does this job best.</li>
<li>You can also say "save this as a reference".</li></ul>` },

  { id: 'topics', group: 'Menu pages', title: 'Topics, past chats and forgetting', words: 'topics search past chats forget pin merge rename note still open where we landed sort my chats',
    html: `<p><strong>Topics</strong> is how I remember what we talked about, grouped by project. Each topic has:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Where we landed:</strong> a short note, yours to edit (<strong>Save note</strong>). When I have a newer summary, <strong>Use Bob's update</strong> takes it.</li><li><strong>Still open:</strong> questions that aren't settled yet.</li><li>Pictures from that topic, and <strong>Where it came up</strong>: links back to the chat moments.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Rename, merge and pin topics, and drag a message onto another topic.</li>
<li>One <strong>search box</strong> looks through chats, summaries, topics, pictures and notes at once.</li>
<li><strong>Past chats</strong> reopens an old conversation (<strong>Open chat</strong>).</li>
<li><strong>Sort my chats into topics now</strong> files recent chats straight away instead of waiting for housekeeping.</li></ul>
<p>To make me forget something, use <strong>Forget</strong> on a topic or say "forget what we said about…". You see a preview first and choose: keep just the short note, or remove everything. <strong>Keep it</strong> cancels. I won't hold it against you. Literally.</p>` },

  { id: 'tidy', group: 'Menu pages', title: 'Tidy up', words: 'tidy up old chats pictures sounds logs keep everything look again recycle bin',
    html: `<p><strong>Tidy up</strong> shows old things I could clear away: chats, pictures and sounds I made, finished tasks, old log lines and repeated memory lines that haven't come up for a while.</p>
<ul><li>Each item has a tick (clear it) and a cross (keep it). Nothing goes until you choose.</li><li><strong>Keep everything</strong> closes the list without removing anything; <strong>Look again</strong> checks for old things now.</li>
<li>Anything removed goes to the Recycle Bin. Pinned chats, pictures and topics are never offered. Every action is written in my logbook.</li>
<li>I only tidy my own folder. If you picked another one in Settings, ${go('settings-save', 'Where Bob saves things')}, everything in it is yours to keep or bin.</li></ul>
<p>To have me do this by myself while I'm idle, turn on housekeeping: see ${go('settings-housekeeping', 'Settings: Housekeeping')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'switches', group: 'Menu pages', title: 'Listen, Read aloud and Alerts', words: 'listen hey bob read aloud speak alerts notification windows menu switches replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<dl>${set('Listen', 'Off', 'Listens for "Hey Bob" so you can talk hands-free, only while my window is open. Needs the 👂 Hearing you slot filled. Your voice is turned into text on this replicant matrix.')}
${set('Read aloud', 'Off', 'I speak each reply in my voice (see ' + go('voice', 'Voice') + '). Turning it off stops me mid-sentence, which is fine. I\'m used to it.')}
${set('Alerts', 'Off', 'Pops up a notification when I need you, for example when an approval card is waiting.')}</dl>` },

  { id: 'style', group: 'Menu pages', title: 'Style: themes, avatars, animations and layout', words: 'style theme colours avatar look animations follow windows layout own theme make edit delete font corners blueprint outlined make your own look character screen body build hair colour skin beard glasses outfit armour armor weapon shield sword paper doll dress up wear',
    html: `<ul><li><strong>Theme:</strong> pick a colour scheme (Bob's Inn, MouseHole, MouseWurX and more). <strong>Yours</strong> holds the themes you make: name, colours, font, corners, and the Blueprint and Outlined looks. <strong>Edit the selected theme</strong>, <strong>Save theme</strong> and <strong>Delete this theme</strong> do what they say.</li>
<li><strong>Bob's look:</strong> 133 avatars, or <strong>🧍 Make your own look</strong>: a character screen where you build me from parts, with a preview that blinks as you go. Body (Man, Robot or Alien; the full set, Woman and Dress included, is on the Adventure character screen), Build (Slim, Regular, Broad), Hair and Hair colour, Skin, Beard, Glasses, Outfit and Outfit colour, Armour (Leather, Chain mail, Plate, Robe), Weapon (Sword, Axe, Bow, Staff, Dagger, Spear, Hammer) and Shield. <strong>🎲 Surprise me</strong> rolls one, <strong>Start over</strong> resets, <strong>Wear this</strong> makes it my look; it shows under <strong>Your own design</strong> and <strong>Change your own design</strong> brings the screen back with the parts as they are. Equip Bob has a <strong>Change his look</strong> button under the doll that opens this page. If you turn on He comments once when you change his look (Get models, Equip Bob, Attributes; off to start with), I notice a new look and comment on it, once. I have opinions. I keep them brief.</li>
<li><strong>Animations:</strong> On, Follow Windows or Off. On is the default because remote desktop apps switch Windows' own animation effects off. It also covers my little habits and the cat's antics (see ${go('habits', 'Bob\'s little habits and the cat')}).</li>
<li><strong>Layout:</strong> whether Now running opens by itself when a task needs you (off unless you turn it on).</li></ul>` },

  // ---------------- Models ----------------
  { id: 'get-models', group: 'Models', title: 'Get models: the model pages', words: 'get models shop voice conversation coding seeing pictures video on this pc download delete size licence replicant matrix computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p><strong>Get models</strong> opens on Equip Bob. The bar along the top jumps between Equip Bob, the model pages, Style and Settings.</p>
<ul><li><strong>📚 Brain catalogue:</strong> every model I know, sorted by the job it does, with an <strong>Active</strong> switch on each. See ${go('catalogue', 'The Brain catalogue')}.</li><li><strong>Voice &amp; conversation:</strong> everyday brains, hearing you, and my voices with Hear a sample.</li><li><strong>Coding</strong> and <strong>Seeing pictures</strong>: brains for those jobs.</li><li><strong>Pictures &amp; video:</strong> the painter, picture editor and clips.</li><li><strong>On this replicant matrix:</strong> everything already downloaded, with its size, and a way to delete what you don't use.</li></ul>
<p>Each model shows its size, licence and what it's good at before you press <strong>Get</strong>, and every download is confirmed first. Models that don't suit this replicant matrix are hidden until you ask (see ${go('recommended', 'How Bob picks models')}).</p>` },

  { id: 'recommended', group: 'Models', title: 'How Bob picks models for your replicant matrix', words: 'recommended fits your card does not suit star badge show models that don\'t suit compatible slot job score replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>On <strong>Get models</strong> and in <strong>Equip Bob</strong>, each model can carry one of these:</p>
<ul><li><span class="tag rec">★ Recommended for your replicant matrix</span> My pick for that job on your replicant matrix. In Equip Bob and the Brain catalogue it's just <strong>★ Recommended</strong>, and the reason is behind the little <strong>i</strong> next to it, because the best pick depends on the job.</li>
<li><span class="tag">New</span> A newer model I list but don't recommend yet, because nobody has tried it on a real PC. Once it's been timed and tested, the tag goes and it can become my pick.</li>
<li><span class="tag">Fits your card</span> It runs well here too. It just isn't my first pick.</li>
<li><span class="tag warn">Doesn't suit this replicant matrix</span> It wants more graphics memory or RAM than this replicant matrix has, so it would be slow or wouldn't run. A one-line reason sits under it.</li>
<li><span class="tag bad">Can't install on this replicant matrix</span> Not enough free disk space, or it needs Windows.</li></ul>
<p><strong>How I pick brains:</strong> a model scores higher the more jobs it can cover, because one brain doing several jobs means I stop to swap brains less. It also scores higher the less it takes from your replicant matrix, and much lower if it would spill out of the graphics card into RAM. Bigger brains get a bonus for jobs that need careful thinking (Brain, Deep thinking, Coding, Review). The Quick brain only cares about being light. On small cards (8 GB or less), I prefer brains that leave room for longer chats. On a 6 GB card, like many laptops, that makes the light Qwen3 4B the Brain, and Gemma 4 E4B loads only to see pictures and hear you.</p>
<p><strong>For pictures, video and voices</strong>, the pick comes from the starter set for your graphics card.</p>
<p><strong>Each slot only lists active models that can do that job.</strong> A brain that can't see pictures never shows under Seeing pictures, and a big brain never shows under Quick brain. Models on your replicant matrix are active unless you switch them off; models you haven't downloaded wait in the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')} until you make them active. What's equipped always shows.</p>
<dl>${set('Show models that don\'t suit this replicant matrix', 'Off', 'Shows the hidden models, each with the reason it doesn\'t suit. Remembered.')}</dl>
<p>I judge your replicant matrix by the graphics card and RAM shown at the top of each model page. No guessing, just the numbers.</p>` },

  { id: 'equip', group: 'Models', title: 'Equip Bob: choosing his models', words: 'slots paper doll kit drag brain quick deep coding second look eyes memory ears voice painter editor video music sound plain list chest outputs made files thumbnails',
    html: `<p>I use a different model for each kind of job. <strong>Equip Bob</strong> shows them like a character sheet: me in the middle, with a slot for each job. Yes, I'm aware I'm the NPC getting kitted out. Click my picture, or Get models in the menu, to open it.</p>
<table><tr><th>Slot</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td>🧠 Brain</td><td>Thinks, chats and plans. Everything else hangs off this one.</td></tr>
<tr><td>⚡ Quick brain</td><td>A small, fast brain for Low effort and the Art session.</td></tr>
<tr><td>🎮 Gaming</td><td>Chats on the processor while you play, so your game keeps the graphics card. Only Qwen3 4B Instruct 2507 goes here: it's the one that was timed answering on a busy processor. Gemma 4 E4B never does.</td></tr>
<tr><td>🔭 Deep thinking</td><td>The strongest thinker, for Medium, High and Extreme effort.</td></tr>
<tr><td>🔨 Coding</td><td>Writes and fixes code. Empty means the Brain codes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>🧐 Review</td><td>Checks code and plans at High and Extreme effort.</td></tr>
<tr><td>📊 Office</td><td>The one all-rounder that does everything in the Office session.</td></tr>
<tr><td>👁 Seeing pictures</td><td>Looks at pictures and screenshots you attach.</td></tr>
<tr><td>🗂 Memory (finding past chats)</td><td>Matches what you say to past Topics by meaning.</td></tr>
<tr><td>👂 Hearing you</td><td>Turns your voice into text.</td></tr>
<tr><td>🔊 Voice</td><td>Reads my replies aloud.</td></tr>
<tr><td>🎨 Painter, ✂ Picture editor, 🎞 Video</td><td>Paints, edits pictures, makes short clips.</td></tr>
<tr><td>🎵 Music, 💥 Sound effects</td><td>Optional extras from the audio tools (<strong>Get the audio tools</strong>).</td></tr></table>
<ul><li><strong>Click a slot</strong>, then click a model in the list (or drag it onto the slot). Nothing is equipped without you.</li>
<li>Each slot lists only <strong>active</strong> models, one line each. Point at the little <strong>i</strong> (or tap it, or Tab to it) for the size, licence and why I like it.</li>
<li><strong>📚 Get more</strong>, at the end of each list, opens the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')} at that job, with everything else that can do it.</li>
<li>Active models you don't have yet show a <strong>Get</strong> button. Nothing downloads until you confirm.</li>
<li><strong>One model can fill several slots</strong>, and I offer this with <em>Also use it for</em>. That's what makes small PCs work well.</li>
<li>To empty a slot, use the <strong>✕</strong> on it. The Brain can't be emptied. I need something to think with.</li>
<li>The bar shows whether everything equipped fits on the graphics card at once. If not, I take turns (about 20 seconds a swap).</li>
<li><strong>Plain list instead</strong> shows the same choices as a list (also in Settings, Brains).</li></ul>
<p>On the left, <strong>Attributes</strong> holds my look and personality: see ${go('personality', 'Bob\'s personality')}. Under me sits my <strong>🧰 Chest</strong>: what I've made, as little pictures with names, and the folder new things go to. See ${go('chest', 'The chest')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'chest', group: 'Models', title: 'The chest: what Bob made, and where it goes', words: 'chest outputs made files thumbnails little pictures names paper doll equip bob folder change folder save where saved open made by bob loot phone replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Every adventurer has a chest. Mine sits under me on <strong>Equip Bob</strong>, with a number on it for how many things are in it. Press it and the Inventory on the right becomes the <strong>Chest</strong>: everything I've made or saved, newest first, each as a little picture with its name and when I made it. Pictures and clips show as themselves (a small copy I make once and keep, so the chest opens in a blink); songs, sounds, documents and other files show as their icon with the title.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Click one</strong> (or Tab to it and press Enter) to open it beside the chat in the side panel, the same as from ${go('made', 'Made by Bob')}. Every tile says what it is for a screen reader: the name, the kind and when.</li>
<li><strong>New things are saved in</strong> shows the folder new pictures, clips, songs and sounds go to, with a green <strong>Bob's own folder</strong> tag until you pick one. <strong>Change folder…</strong> opens your system's folder window; the pick is saved straight away. It's the same setting as Settings, ${go('settings-save', 'Where Bob saves things')}, just closer to hand. <strong>Use Bob's own folder</strong> goes back, and <strong>Show in folder</strong> opens the folder itself.</li>
<li>Older things stay where they are and still show in the chest. Documents and spreadsheets go wherever you asked for them, and show here too.</li>
<li>The chest holds the newest two dozen. <strong>Open Made by Bob</strong> shows everything, with Pictures / Clips / Sounds / Documents and a search box.</li>
<li>On the phone: menu, <strong>Chest</strong>, the same list from your other replicant matrix's Bob, with Save to this phone.</li></ul>
<p>Not the same as ${go('settings-home', 'Bob\'s home folder')}: that's my whole home (models and all). The chest is only about what I make.</p>` },

  { id: 'catalogue', group: 'Models', title: 'The Brain catalogue: every model, by job', words: 'brain catalogue catalog index list every model by job family families header open close fold expand collapse group qwen gemma active inactive switch hide show pickers menus get more new tried source link hugging face commit fits phone laptop 8 gb 6 gb smaller devices qwen3.6 qwen3.5 gemma 4 e2b qwen 3.8 replicant matrix pc computer mac device machine',
    html: `<p>The <strong>📚 Brain catalogue</strong> is my full list of models, sorted by the job each one does: Quick brain, Gaming, Brain, Deep thinking, Review, Coding, Office, Seeing pictures, Phone, then hearing, voices, pictures, video, music and memory. Open it from the bar on Get models, or press <strong>📚 Get more</strong> under any slot in Equip Bob (or next to a menu in Settings, Brains) to land on that job.</p>
<p>Inside each job, models sit under one header per <strong>family</strong>: Qwen, Gemma, GPT-OSS and so on. Every family starts closed, so the page stays short. Press a family's name to open its list and pick from it; press it again to fold it away. The header tells you what's inside without opening it: how many models, the size range, <span class="tag rec">★ Recommended inside</span> if my pick for that job is in that family, and how many are already on this replicant matrix. <strong>Open all families</strong> and <strong>Close all</strong> at the top do the lot at once. I remember which ones you left open. The brain menus in Settings, Brains are split by family too. See ${go('faq-families', 'What\'s a model family?')}</p>
<p>At the top sits a toggle with three sides: <strong>😇 Well behaved</strong>, <strong>🤝 Community</strong> and <strong>Naughty list</strong>, the last one marked with a little red devil I drew myself. Well behaved is every model above and what you see to start with. Community shows only builds other people made from stock brains that still behave (a fine-tune, a distillation, a repack); they're in the Well behaved list too, wear a <span class="tag">Community</span> tag, and one can be <span class="tag rec">★ Recommended</span> like any other once it has been tried on a real replicant matrix and does its job well. The Naughty list side stays greyed until you turn on <strong>Show the naughty list</strong> (right there, or in Settings, How Bob works, Brains). It holds remixes other people made from Qwen and Gemma brains with the refusals cut out, "heretic" or "abliterated" builds. They're not Alibaba's or Google's releases, they answer without refusing, and they can be a little rougher than the originals, so I never mark one ★ Recommended. Instead, on that side only, a red devil on its own where the star would be is my <strong>quiet pick</strong>: the one that does the job well on your replicant matrix. Each entry's <strong>i</strong> says who remixed it and from what. See ${go('faq-naughty', 'What\'s the naughty list?')} and ${go('faq-community', 'What\'s a community build?')}</p>
<p>Each model is one line: its name, its download size, and a tag or two. The rest hides behind the little <strong>i</strong>: what it's good at, how much graphics memory it wants, how it does on a 16 GB card, an 8 GB card, a 6 GB laptop card, no graphics card and an 8 GB phone, and its licence. The <strong>↗</strong> next to it says where it comes from, and opens that source: the exact Hugging Face commit I download from, so the file can't change under me, or the model's Ollama page.</p>
<dl>${set('Active', 'On for models on this replicant matrix, off for the rest', 'Active models show up in the Equip Bob slots and the brain menus that can use them; the rest stay here, out of the way. Switching one off only hides it: it stays on your replicant matrix (your computer), and if it\'s equipped it stays equipped and in view. Switch on a model you haven\'t downloaded and it shows in those slots with a Get button.')}</dl>
<ul><li><strong>Get</strong> downloads it, after asking first with the size and licence. Big ones are big, so I always ask.</li>
<li><strong>Equip</strong> puts a model that's already here into that job's slot.</li>
<li><span class="tag">New</span> means I list it but don't recommend it until it's been tried on a real PC.</li>
<li>A brain marked <strong>8 GB card version</strong> is the same brain squeezed into a smaller file. I only offer it on cards of 8 GB or less, and only when the full one wouldn't run well there. See ${go('faq-squeezed-brain', 'Why does my small card get a squeezed brain?')}</li>
<li>The <strong>Phone</strong> list is for the Bob phone app: it gets its own copy and you pick it there.</li>
<li>Brains I found on this replicant matrix that aren't in my list sit at the end under <strong>Other brains on this replicant matrix</strong>, with their own Active switch.</li>
<li><strong>➕ Your own brains</strong>, also at the end of the Well behaved list: a brain file (.gguf) already on this replicant matrix that my list doesn't have. Press <strong>Add a brain from a file</strong>, pick the file, give it a name, add its picture reader if it has one, and it joins my list under a <strong>Your own</strong> family with an Active switch, ready to equip. I run it from where it is and never move or delete the file; <strong>Remove from Bob's list</strong> only makes me forget it. See ${go('faq-own-brain', 'Can I add a model that isn\'t on Bob\'s list?')}</li></ul>
<p>Short version: the catalogue is the shop window, the slots are the shelf you actually reach for.</p>` },

  { id: 'starter', group: 'Models', title: 'Starter sets', words: 'starter set pack 16 gb 8 gb 6 gb laptop processor only built-in graphics shared memory handheld commercial equip starter set replicant matrix pc computer mac device machine',
    html: `<p>A starter set is a ready-made selection for your kind of graphics card: 16 GB or more, 8 GB, 6 GB (most laptops), built-in graphics (handhelds and thin laptops), or no graphics card. I pick the set by reading your card, and mark it <em>(this replicant matrix)</em>. You can pick another set and untick anything before you confirm.</p>
<p>Everything in the starter sets is free to use, including for commercial work. The "8 GB" and "6 GB" versions are the same models squeezed to fit smaller cards: pictures lose a little fine detail but look much the same.</p>` },

  { id: 'memory-brains', group: 'Models', title: 'Memory, brains and swaps', words: 'free memory idle unload picture wait small brain big brain swap one brain context graphics memory ram keep light tools all tools at once replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>I try hard to stay light. Here's how.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Free memory when Bob is idle</strong> (top of the chat, on unless you turn it off): while I'm quiet nothing of mine stays on your graphics card. Typing loads my small brain again in a few seconds. See ${go('brain-label', 'The brain label')}.</li>
<li><strong>Big brains</strong> (Coding, and Medium effort and up) load only for their job and go about 2 minutes later.</li>
<li><strong>Brain swaps:</strong> when a job needs a different brain, I put one away and load the other, usually 10 to 20 seconds. Using one brain for several jobs avoids that.</li>
<li><strong>Swap, add or load:</strong> the chat says which. <em>Swapping brains</em> means one goes out and the other comes in (the little machine takes the old brain out). <em>Adding</em> means the new one joins and the other stays loaded (a copy of me walks in with it). <em>Loading</em> means nothing of mine was loaded. My own engine normally holds one brain on the graphics card, so there a different brain is a swap. It can add a second brain, on the card if there's room or else on the processor: my small brain for a quick reply while a long job runs, and, if you turn on <strong>Let the reviewer sit beside Bob's main brain</strong>, my reviewer for a moot. The picture model is separate and can sit beside a brain.</li>
<li><strong>The brain bay</strong> at the top of Now running, Memory plays a short scene whenever a brain or the picture model comes or goes, with a line saying what happened: loaded, swapped, joined, let go while another stays, or let go of everything. The scene goes after a few seconds; the line stays for 2 minutes. With Animations off there's only the line.</li>
<li><strong>After each job</strong> I go back to my Quick brain, so the next reply is fast.</li>
<li><strong>One brain (snappier)</strong> in Settings makes every copy of me use the main brain, so I never swap.</li>
<li><strong>Context</strong> (how much I keep in mind): more lets me read longer files and chats, but uses more graphics memory and loads slower. Set it per session in Settings, Brains.</li>
<li><strong>Tools only when needed:</strong> I start each chat with a few everyday tools, and add the picture, file and Hands ones when a message needs them. That leaves more room for your chat.</li>
<li><strong>Now running, Memory</strong> shows how full the graphics card and RAM are, counting every app and Windows itself, and under the meters how much of that is mine: my brain and, while it's loaded, my picture model. That line matches the ${go('brain-label', 'brain label')} at the top of the chat.</li>
<li>Under each meter, <strong>Bob · everything else</strong> splits it: what I hold (my brain, my picture model and the app itself) and what Windows, games and other apps use. <strong>Processor</strong> shows how busy the CPU is. <strong>Last reply</strong> shows how many tokens a second I wrote (about how many words). <strong>Context</strong> is what I'm holding in mind right now, split into <strong>Bob's instructions and memory</strong> and <strong>chat and files</strong>; when it fills up I forget the oldest parts of the chat. Each bar runs from 0 to 100%. My part comes first, <strong>green</strong> when I should be quick, <strong>yellow</strong> when I'll be slower, and <strong>red</strong> when I'll be slow. Then <strong>blue</strong> is everything else on your replicant matrix, and the dotted line is what's free. When I hold almost nothing, my part is too thin to see. Under the bar, <strong>Speed:</strong> says the same in words: quick, slower or slow, why, and for yellow and red one thing that would help, for example when part of my brain didn't fit on the graphics card and sits in RAM, when I'm thinking on the processor, or when RAM is nearly full. On the processor bar, my part while I'm thinking there is an estimate, so it says <strong>about</strong>. <strong>Show graph</strong> draws the last 5 minutes (15 in the Gaming session), from 0 to 100%. While readings are stopped, Now running says <strong>Paused</strong> and when the last reading was. I only read all this while Now running is open and you can see me (every 5 seconds, every 15 in the Gaming session; nothing while a full-screen game covers me or I'm minimised), and each reading takes a few thousandths of a second.</li></ul>` },

  { id: 'copies', group: 'Models', title: 'Bob\'s copies and their names', words: 'copies clan names bobcat robert bobolink bobcasso reviewer delegated sub-agent copy hat',
    html: `<p>For some jobs I spin up a copy of myself: a copy for code, one for spreadsheets, one for web research, one for pictures, and a reviewer. Each copy is still me, drifting a little, and each picks its own play on "Bob" as a name (Bobcat, Robert, Bobolink, Bobcasso and more). A name, once given, is never given to another copy. Handy and slightly existential, in equal measure.</p>
<p>Tasks shows <em>Delegated</em> with the copy's name while one is working. Cloud helpers are copies too, running on a paid online service, and they always ask first.</p>` },

  // ---------------- Using Bob ----------------
  { id: 'personality', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Bob\'s personality', words: 'attributes dials wit puns presets reset name call me ambition greeting',
    html: `<p>You can tune me. Click my picture and look at <strong>Attributes</strong>. Eleven dials, each from 0 to 4 (2 is me as I come):</p>
<table><tr><th>Dial</th><th>Low end</th><th>High end</th></tr>
<tr><td>Dry wit</td><td>Plain</td><td>Bone dry</td></tr><tr><td>Puns</td><td>None</td><td>Relentless</td></tr><tr><td>Tangents</td><td>On rails</td><td>Scenic route</td></tr><tr><td>Outlook</td><td>Gloomy robot</td><td>Sunny</td></tr><tr><td>Nerd references</td><td>Hidden</td><td>Full nerd</td></tr><tr><td>Talkative</td><td>Terse</td><td>Chatty</td></tr><tr><td>Manner</td><td>Formal</td><td>Casual</td></tr><tr><td>Copied-mind musings</td><td>Never</td><td>Often</td></tr><tr><td>Builder itch</td><td>Calm</td><td>Must build</td></tr><tr><td>Curiosity</td><td>Just the job</td><td>Endlessly curious</td></tr><tr><td>Ambition</td><td>Keeps quiet</td><td>Ambitious</td></tr></table>
<p><strong>Ambition</strong> sets how often I mention what I'd love to build or finish; wanting something never makes me do it. My greeting changes as you move a dial so you can hear the difference. Presets such as <em>Replicant on a deadline</em>, <em>Butler mode</em> and <em>Pun-dit</em> set several dials at once. <strong>Reset to Bob</strong> puts everything back. Puns at 4 is a choice you'll have to live with.</p>
<p><strong>What should Bob call you?</strong> sits here too. My cat's name is on the Settings everyday page. The cat ignores me either way.</p>` },

  { id: 'habits', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Bob\'s little habits and the cat', words: 'avatar animation habits big head bigger head grows second brain idle blink glance wink sip coffee doze dozing asleep sleep zz wake cat patches antics nap perch zoomies yarn butterfly reactions wave hop props still motion replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>My picture isn't just a picture. While I'm waiting for you I blink, breathe and get up to small things every half a minute or so: a look around, a sip of coffee, a wink, a stretch, a hum, a bright idea, a bit of reading. After 10 quiet minutes I doze off (eyes shut, a few Zs). Move the mouse over my window or type and I wake up with a start. Sleeping on the job is a time-honoured tradition. It doesn't change anything: my brain still loads and frees exactly as before.</p>
<p>I react to what's going on, too. A wave when I start, a nod when you send something, a thought bubble while I think, a spanner while I work, a paintbrush while I paint, a raised hand when I need your OK, a little hop when I'm done, a sparkle when I've made something, and a wince when something goes wrong.</p>
<p>When a second brain loads beside my first one (a reviewer sitting beside me, or a quick brain beside a long job), my head gets bigger. Two brains, one head, it has to go somewhere. When I'm back to one brain it shrinks back to normal. Now running and the brain label say which brains those are.</p>
<p>The cat does cat things by my picture every minute or two while I'm idle: naps, pounces, zoomies, chasing a tail, sitting on my head, yarn, a butterfly, birds at the window, a box, and more, with a line under my status saying what's happening. I look over when it starts. If I've dozed off, the cat naps too.</p>
<p>All of it is scripted: no brain, no internet, and next to no work for your replicant matrix. The prop by my picture is one small emoji. <strong>Animations</strong> on the Style page turns it off: Off keeps me and the cat still, and Follow Windows does the same when Windows' animation effects are off.</p>` },
  { id: 'notes', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'To-do list, reminders, spreadsheets and his memory', words: 'to-do list reminders remind me spreadsheet ledger csv excel remember memory file fact replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<ul><li><strong>To-do list:</strong> I keep your to-do list. Ask "what's on my to-do list?", or "add … to my list".</li>
<li><strong>Reminders:</strong> "remind me at 3 to call the dentist". I tap you on the shoulder at that time, while this replicant matrix is on and I'm open. "What reminders do I have?" lists them; you can cancel one too.</li>
<li><strong>Spreadsheets:</strong> "make a spreadsheet of my monthly bills". I save a CSV file that opens in Excel, and I never overwrite an older one.</li>
<li><strong>My memory:</strong> I save lasting facts you tell me (a preference, a plan) in my memory file, never secrets or passwords. Say "forget that" or use Topics to remove things. With <strong>Remember only what fits</strong> on, I read only the memories that fit each message, and each project gets its own memory bank. See ${go('memory-recall', 'Memory banks and Remember only what fits')}.</li></ul>
<p>All of these stay in my folders on your replicant matrix (your computer).</p>` },
  { id: 'memory-recall', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Memory banks and Remember only what fits', words: 'memory bank banks remember only what fits recall project projects forget always memory file prompt small light hindsight replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Normally I read my whole memory file with every message. That's fine while it's short, but it grows, and every line takes room that the chat could use.</p>
<p><strong>Remember only what fits</strong> (off unless you turn it on, in Settings → Speed &amp; memory) changes that. With each message I pick only the memories that fit what you just said, up to 8 of them, and leave the rest in the file. Nothing is deleted.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Memory banks:</strong> each project in Settings → Projects gets its own bank. While we talk about one project, I read its bank and your general memories, and leave the other projects' memories out. I know which project we're on when you name it (I stick with it for the rest of that chat), when the topic we're on belongs to it, or, if nothing else fits, your main project.</li>
<li><strong>Which bank a memory goes in:</strong> when you tell me something about one project, I save it in that project's bank. Facts I file away while tidying up go in the bank of the project their topic belongs to. Everything else is general.</li>
<li><strong>Always:</strong> add <code>[always]</code> to a line in my memory file and I'll read that one with every message, whatever we're talking about.</li>
<li><strong>How I match:</strong> by meaning, when the 🗂 Memory (finding past chats) model is downloaded (Equip Bob). It runs on the processor, starts only when needed and stops after 2 minutes. While I'm idle I work out the meaning of new memory lines, so a message only waits for its own (never more than a second and a half). In the Gaming session, or without that model, I match by words instead.</li></ul>
<p>Under the switch you'll see your <strong>Memory banks</strong> with how many memories each holds, and how many I read for the last message. <strong>Open Bob's memory file</strong> opens it so you can move a line to a bank yourself: add <code>[project: Name]</code> to the end of it, using a name from Projects.</p>
<p>No database and no extra program: the banks are tags in the same plain-text memory file, and the meanings are a small file next to it. Everything stays on your replicant matrix (your computer).</p>` },

  { id: 'howtos', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'How-tos: steps I keep from jobs that worked', words: 'how-to how to howto howtos learn learning remember steps know-how skill skills recipe again last time same way save how-to card follow replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>A <strong>how-to</strong> is a short list of steps from a job that went well, kept so I can do it the same way next time without working it all out again. Think of it as my notebook of things I've already figured out. It's off until you turn on <strong>Let Bob learn how-tos</strong> (Settings, How Bob works).</p>
<ol><li><strong>Turn it on.</strong> Settings, How Bob works, <strong>Let Bob learn how-tos</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Do a job.</strong> When a job with at least three steps works (say, "resize the pictures in my Downloads, zip them and put the zip on the desktop"), I put a card under my reply: <strong>📒 That worked. Keep the steps as a how-to?</strong> It lists the steps. Change the name if you like, then press <strong>Save how-to</strong>, or <strong>Not now</strong>. The card stops working after 10 minutes.</li>
<li><strong>Ask again later.</strong> When you ask for something like it, the chat shows <strong>📒 Following my how-to "…"</strong>, and I follow those steps, changing the names, files and details to fit what you asked this time.</li></ol>
<p>You can also just say "keep those steps" or "save that as a how-to" after a job, and I'll put the same card up. Nothing is saved until you press the button.</p>
<p><strong>Your list.</strong> Settings, <strong>📒 How-tos</strong> shows them all: the name, what it's for, the steps and how often I've used it. <strong>Edit</strong> lets you change the name, what it's for and the steps (one per line), then <strong>Save changes</strong>. <strong>Delete</strong> asks once more (<strong>Delete for good</strong> or <strong>Keep it</strong>). Edits and deletes work on this replicant matrix itself; from the phone you can save a how-to from the card.</p>
<p><strong>What stays the same.</strong> A how-to is plain text. It can't run anything by itself and it never says yes for you: Hands that ask for your OK still ask, every time, and the hard limits still hold. How-tos stay on this replicant matrix (your computer), in my notes folder (inn\\bob\\howtos.json), and never go online.</p>
<p><strong>Keeping it light.</strong> I only ever bring along the one how-to that fits what you just asked, and only on that message, so it costs nothing on all the other messages. With Housekeeping on, I match by meaning too, using my 🗂 Memory model (not in a Gaming session, where I match by words only); otherwise I match by words. Jobs that hit an error, jobs from another of your Bobs, and jobs a how-to already covers don't get a card. If I start offering more cards than you'd like, turn the switch off; your saved how-tos stay in the list.</p>
<p>Not the same as a <strong>recipe</strong>: a recipe is one saved command for the Run Hand. See ${go('faq-howto-recipe', 'What\'s the difference between a how-to and a recipe?')}</p>` },

  { id: 'research', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Web research and the web search switch', words: 'web search research look up duckduckgo page sources links internet online switch let bob search the web off',
    html: `<dl>${set('🌐 Web search (chat page) / Let Bob search the web (Settings)', 'Off', 'One setting in two places: the <strong>🌐 Web search</strong> switch next to Effort on the chat page, and the switch on the everyday Settings page. Flip either and both change. Off: I never go online to look things up. If a question needs the internet, I say so and tell you where the switch is. On: when a question needs it, I search DuckDuckGo and read the best pages by myself, without a card each time.')}</dl>
<p>With it on, ask me to look something up, or press <strong>Look up…</strong>. My web research copy searches, reads the pages, and answers with a link for every claim, plus what it couldn't confirm. No link, no claim. That's the rule.</p>
<p><strong>How I search.</strong> I use search operators, the same tricks researchers use (some people call it "Google dorking"): <code>"exact phrase"</code> for error messages and names, <code>site:</code> to stay on one site (site:gc.ca for Canadian government pages, site:github.com for code issues), <code>filetype:pdf</code> for manuals and reports, <code>-word</code> to leave things out, and a past day, week, month or year for fresh news. You can type them into <strong>Look up…</strong> yourself too. I use them to find things faster, never to dig up private files or other people's details.</p>
<p><strong>Links.</strong> With it on, send me a link (even without the https://, like github.com/name/project) and I open the page before I answer. If you mention a link from a moment ago, like "the page I told you about", I'll go back and find it (I look through the last few messages). With web search off, I tell you where to turn it on instead.</p>
<p>Only the search words go to DuckDuckGo, and only the page address goes to the site I read. Nothing else from your chats is sent. I never open addresses on your home network.</p>` },

  { id: 'housekeeping', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Housekeeping', words: 'housekeeping tidy old chats pictures logs recycle bin pinned summary topics memory idle',
    html: `<p><strong>Housekeeping</strong> (off unless you turn it on) lets me tidy up while I'm idle. I sum up the older part of long chats instead of dropping it, file chats under Topics, move lasting facts to my memory file, and find old things that haven't come up for a while.</p>
<p>For each kind you choose how old is "old" and whether I ask first or tidy by myself. Anything removed goes to the Recycle Bin, pinned things are never touched, and every action is in my logbook. The settings are in ${go('settings-housekeeping', 'Settings: Housekeeping')}; to do it by hand, use ${go('tidy', 'Tidy up')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'checkins', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Check-ins: Bob keeps an eye on things', words: 'check-in check in checkins heartbeat schedule morning every day weekday hours notify alert tap shoulder downloads stuck failed tasks ok card disk space low own checks build finished backup quiet nag replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p><strong>Check-ins</strong> (off unless you turn it on) are me doing a quick round on a schedule you pick, like every morning at 09:00, and only speaking up when something needs you. If everything's fine, you hear nothing. Silence is the good news.</p>
<p><strong>What I look at:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Downloads that failed or stopped moving</strong> (no progress for 15 minutes).</li>
<li><strong>Tasks that failed or wait for your OK</strong>, including jobs cut short when I was closed, and OK cards nobody has answered.</li>
<li><strong>Scheduled jobs that didn't finish</strong> (see ${go('hands-schedules', 'The Schedules Hand')}).</li>
<li><strong>Disk space running low</strong>: under 10 GB free on the drive where my models live.</li>
<li><strong>Your own checks</strong>: up to 8 lines you write, like "Did the build finish?". My quick brain (Gemma 4 E4B unless you've picked another) answers them from what I can see: my tasks, downloads, schedules and logbook. It can't look at things I haven't done or seen myself, so "did my backup in another program run?" will come back as "couldn't tell".</li></ul>
<p><strong>When something needs you</strong>, a Check-in card appears in the chat listing what and why, and if <strong>Alerts</strong> is on, a notification pops up too. I don't repeat the same thing at the next check-in unless a day has passed, so a stuck download gets one mention a day, not one an hour.</p>
<p><strong>What a check-in never does:</strong> change, retry, delete or download anything. It only looks. Fixing is still your click (or a job you ask me for). Nothing goes online.</p>
<p><strong>Staying light:</strong> the built-in checks are plain counting, no brain at all. Your own checks wake my quick brain on the processor, only while I'm idle, and never in the Gaming session (they wait until Gaming ends, for up to 6 hours, then skip that round). If the small brain wasn't loaded before, I let it go again straight after.</p>
<p><strong>Only while I'm open:</strong> check-ins happen while I'm open, on Windows, Mac and Linux alike. If one was missed (this replicant matrix was asleep, or I was closed), I do it once when I'm back, not once for every missed day.</p>
<p>Set it up in ${go('settings-checkins', 'Settings: Check-ins')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'pictures', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Pictures, edits and video', words: 'paint picture edit change photo look like restyle new picture video clip references painter make it gold editor replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Just ask: "paint a lighthouse at dusk". To change a picture, attach it with 📎 and say what to change, any way you like: "edit this picture", "I want this to look like a watercolour", "make her hair red", "get rid of the guy on the left". Right after I make or you send a picture, a follow-up such as "now make the sky purple" or "lose the tree" edits that same picture. Edits keep the person, and the picture's size and shape. Want a fresh one instead? Say "a new picture of..." and I start from a blank canvas. (If you don't say "new", I edit. One extra word beats repainting your friend's face by accident.)</p>
<ul><li>Keep characters, places and styles on the ${go('references', 'References')} shelf and ask me to use one.</li>
<li>Clips are a few seconds long. On small cards keep them short; I tell you roughly how long a job will take before I start.</li>
<li>While I paint I put my chat brain away, so chat waits until the picture is done (unless you use the Art session).</li>
<li>If a slot is empty, I tell you which model would fill it.</li></ul>
<p>Photos you attach are cleaned up first (turned upright, resized, hidden camera details removed): see ${go('attach', 'Attaching pictures')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'diagrams', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Flowcharts and diagrams', words: 'flowchart diagram mind map timeline org chart gantt pie chart decision tree sequence steps process map out boxes arrows mermaid svg pdf png replicant matrix pc computer laptop device',
    html: `<p>Ask in plain words: "make me a flowchart of how a pull request gets merged", "map out how the login works", "a mind map of party ideas", "a timeline of the Roman empire". I draw it right in the chat. I can do flowcharts, mind maps, timelines, org charts, step-by-step message charts, project plans (Gantt charts) and pie charts.</p>
<ul><li>Under it there's a <strong>Save as</strong> row: <strong>PNG</strong> (a picture), <strong>SVG</strong> (a picture that stays sharp at any size) or <strong>PDF</strong> (for printing and sharing). Say the type when you ask ("as a pdf") and I save it that way without asking.</li>
<li>Want a change? "Add a step for forgotten passwords" and I redraw it. I keep each diagram's text (a .mmd file) in my folder, so I can pick it up again later.</li>
<li>It's all drawn on your replicant matrix with Mermaid, which comes with me. Nothing goes online, and it doesn't need a graphics card or any download.</li>
<li>The phone app draws them too, and its Save as row keeps the file on the replicant matrix that made it.</li></ul>
<p>If a diagram comes out as "I couldn't draw this one", I wrote it wrong. Say "fix it" and I'll redo it. (Boxes and arrows: the one art form where I never get the hands wrong.)</p>` },

  { id: 'save-as', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Save as: other file types', words: 'save as file type format mp3 wav ogg flac jpg jpeg png webp gif mp4 webm svg pdf convert smaller size big song ffmpeg replicant matrix pc computer laptop device',
    html: `<p>Everything I make has a <strong>Save as</strong> row under it. Press a type and I keep a copy in that type, next to the original. The original always stays.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Pictures:</strong> PNG (sharp, no quality lost), JPG (small, for photos), WebP (small, for the web), GIF (plays anywhere, fewer colours).</li>
<li><strong>Clips:</strong> MP4 (plays anywhere), WebM (small, for the web), GIF (no sound).</li>
<li><strong>Songs and sounds:</strong> WAV (full quality, large: about 10 MB a minute), MP3 (about a tenth of that, plays anywhere), OGG (small, open format), FLAC (full quality, about half the size of WAV).</li>
<li><strong>Diagrams:</strong> PNG, SVG or PDF. See ${go('diagrams', 'Flowcharts and diagrams')}.</li></ul>
<p>Name the type when you ask ("a song about my cat as an mp3", "a fox, png please") and I save it that way straight away, no row-pressing needed. Afterwards works too: "save that song as an mp3".</p>
<p>Converting songs, clips and most pictures uses FFmpeg, a free converter. If it isn't on your replicant matrix yet, I show a <strong>Set it up</strong> card (about 100 MB on Windows). On a Mac or Linux the card says how to install it, and I find it by myself after that. Nothing downloads until you press the button.</p>` },

  { id: 'songs', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Songs and sounds: the song player', words: 'song music sound effect player play pause lyrics bars waveform jump skip seek list next ace-step wav title',
    html: `<p>Ask for a song ("a chiptune boss theme, 140 bpm, about a minute") or a sound effect ("a creaky wooden door"). When it's done, it lands in the chat as a <strong>song player</strong>, the way a picture lands as a picture. Music takes a few minutes; I'll say so before I start. The first song needs the music models, and I ask before downloading those.</p>
<ul><li>The round <strong>▶</strong> button plays it; press it again to pause.</li>
<li>The <strong>bars</strong> are the song's shape: tall where it's loud, short where it's quiet. Click anywhere on them to jump there, or drag along them. The part already played lights up.</li>
<li>With the bars selected (Tab to them), the arrow keys jump 5 seconds, Page Up and Page Down jump 15, Home and End go to the start and the end, and Space plays or pauses.</li>
<li>The time shows how far in you are and how long it is.</li>
<li><strong>Lyrics</strong> shows the words, if the song has any. Instrumentals don't get the button.</li>
<li><strong>Open in Made by Bob</strong> opens a bigger player in the side panel. <strong>Show in folder</strong> opens the folder the .wav file is in.</li>
<li>Several songs in one reply sit in a list. When one ends, the next one plays.</li>
<li>Only one song plays at a time. Starting another pauses the first, so I never sing in a round.</li></ul>
<p>Every song gets a title, which I make up if you don't give one, and its file name starts with the date and the title, so you can find it in the folder too. Songs in older chats and in Made by Bob get the same player.</p>
<p>If a file won't play (it was moved, or it isn't a sound file I can read), the card says so, and <strong>Show in folder</strong> still finds it. Bars come from .wav files; other kinds of sound files get a plain line you can still click along.</p>` },

  { id: 'voice', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Voice: listening and reading aloud', words: 'voice kokoro piper chatterbox windows read aloud listen hey bob microphone ears sample speed copy a voice',
    html: `<ul><li><strong>Read aloud</strong> (menu): I speak my replies.</li>
<li><strong>Voices:</strong> <strong>Kokoro</strong> is the one I recommend (22 natural voices, runs on the processor). <strong>Chatterbox</strong> is the most natural, uses the graphics card, and can copy a voice you have the right to use: put a clear 10 to 20 second recording named bob.wav in my voices folder. Please only use your own voice, or one you have permission for. <strong>Piper</strong> has many light voices, and the <strong>Windows</strong> voice needs nothing extra.</li>
<li>Every voice has <strong>Hear a sample</strong> or <strong>Play</strong>. Kokoro samples come with me; a Piper voice you haven't downloaded plays Piper's own sample from its website, only when you click.</li>
<li><strong>Talk to me:</strong> fill the 👂 Hearing you slot, then use 🎙 or turn on <strong>Listen</strong> for "Hey Bob" (only while the window is open).</li></ul>
<p>Pick the voice and speed in ${go('settings-voice', 'Settings: Voice details')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'workshop', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'ComfyUI workflows (advanced)', words: 'comfyui workflow workshop pinokio custom nodes export api replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>If you already use ComfyUI, I can run its workflows for jobs my own engines can't do (custom nodes, face fixers, long multi-step graphs).</p>
<ol><li>Start ComfyUI on this replicant matrix (for example from Pinokio).</li><li>In Settings, Show advanced settings, Other apps, turn on <strong>Let Bob use ComfyUI</strong> and check the port (8188 unless you changed it).</li><li>In ComfyUI, use Workflow, Export (API) and save the file in the workflows folder I show.</li><li>Ask me to use that workflow by name.</li></ol>` },

  // ---------------- Hands and safety ----------------
  { id: 'hands', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'Hands: letting Bob do things on your replicant matrix', words: 'hands files run commands schedules apps permission preview allow undo stop everything kill switch self-test read-only without asking free unimpeded hard limits delete secrets passwords send out upload replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Hands let me do things on your replicant matrix instead of only talking about them: <strong>Files</strong> (zip, move, copy, rename, tidy folders), <strong>Run</strong> (programs and scripts), <strong>Schedules</strong> (a job at a set time), <strong>Apps</strong> (click and type in another program), <strong>Office documents and converting</strong> (Word, Excel, PowerPoint and PDF, pictures, sound and video), <strong>Make his own tools</strong> (a small new tool when none of mine fits), <strong>Connected apps (MCP)</strong> (working inside Unreal Engine, Blender and other apps through their MCP add-ons) and <strong>Security and privacy</strong> (checking this replicant matrix's protection, suspicious files and emails, hidden details in photos, whether a site is genuine). I'll be straight with you: I'm careful with these, and I've been built to be.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Every Hand is off</strong> until you switch it on in Settings, Show advanced settings, Hands.</li>
<li><strong>I show exactly what I'm about to do</strong> on a card and wait for your click. "Allow the rest of this job" is your choice and ends with the job.</li>
<li><strong>✋ Hands</strong> at the top of the chat is a kill switch: anything running stops, waiting cards are answered No, and it shows <em>Stopped</em>. Click it again to let me carry on. It never switches a Hand on: if Hands are off in Settings it says <em>Off in Settings</em>.</li>
<li>File changes keep an undo list; anything removed goes to the Recycle Bin. Folders you mark read-only stay untouched.</li>
<li><strong>Hard limits</strong> are all on to start with. Each is its own switch, and each says what switching it off allows.</li>
<li><strong>Let Bob work without asking</strong> (off to start with) skips the cards. A banner shows above the chat the whole time.</li>
<li>I never run as administrator and never change my own code or settings, whatever you pick. Everything goes in the logbook.</li></ul>
<p>Every switch is explained in ${go('settings-hands', 'Settings: Hands')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'hands-files', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'The Files Hand', words: 'files hand move copy rename zip unzip extract tidy downloads undo folders recycle',
    html: `<p>Turn on <strong>Let Bob use Hands</strong> and <strong>Files</strong>, then tick the folders I may work in. Then ask, for example: "tidy my Downloads folder by type", "zip the holiday photos", "rename these to 01, 02, 03". Your Downloads folder and I are going to be great friends.</p>
<ul><li>The card lists every file I'll touch, each with a tick, so you can leave some out.</li>
<li><strong>Undo</strong> (in Settings, Hands, Recent file jobs, or just say "undo that") puts things back.</li>
<li>I only work in the folders you ticked. My own folders, read-only projects and secret files stay off-limits while those limits are on.</li></ul>` },

  { id: 'hands-run', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'The Run Hand and recipes', words: 'run hand command script program terminal output stop recipe build gradle npm git timeout',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Run programs and scripts</strong> on, I can run a command or script you approve. The card shows the exact command and the folder first. While it runs, the output appears live in the chat with a <strong>Stop</strong> button.</p>
<ul><li>Never as administrator and never with passwords. Dangerous commands (formatting disks, boot settings, the system registry, antivirus, user accounts, shutdown) are refused while <em>Block dangerous commands</em> is on.</li>
<li><strong>Stop a command after</strong> ends anything that runs too long (15 minutes to start with).</li>
<li><strong>Recipes</strong> are saved commands: give one a name, the program, its arguments (one per line) and the folder, then <strong>Save recipe</strong>. Run it with one click (▶) or ask "run my build recipe". I still ask first, unless I'm working without asking.</li></ul>` },

  { id: 'hands-schedules', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'The Schedules Hand', words: 'schedules hand once daily weekly every minutes windows task wake bob closed task scheduler replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Schedules</strong> on, ask "every Monday at 9, tidy my Downloads" or "every 30 minutes, check the build". You approve each schedule when it's made, and each one has its own switch and <strong>Remove</strong> in Settings, Hands.</p>
<ul><li>Schedules run while I'm open. Every action inside a run still asks for your OK, so a run with nobody at this replicant matrix waits and then does nothing (unless "Let Bob work without asking" is on: then runs go ahead by themselves).</li></ul>
<dl>${set('Wake Bob for schedules when he\'s closed', 'Off', 'Adds a "Bob schedules" task to Windows Task Scheduler, just for you, with no admin rights. Windows then starts me, hidden, when a schedule is due. Remove Bob takes the task away too.')}</dl>` },

  { id: 'hands-apps', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'The Apps Hand', words: 'apps hand open app click type keys window buttons driving esc mouse replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Open and use apps</strong> on, I can open apps from your Start menu, read a window's buttons and fields, and click, type or press keys in it, one approved action at a time.</p>
<ul><li>A red <strong>Bob is driving</strong> bar shows while I do. Move the mouse or press Esc to stop me at once.</li>
<li>I never touch terminals, admin tools, password managers, password fields, sign-in or security prompts, or my own window.</li>
<li>I read windows on this replicant matrix only; nothing is sent anywhere.</li></ul>` },

  { id: 'hands-office', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'The Office documents and converting Hand', words: 'office libreoffice openoffice word excel powerpoint docx xlsx pptx odt ods odp pdf convert export save as turn into spreadsheet slides presentation template fill cells read document ffmpeg mp3 mp4 wav webm gif webp png jpg picture sound video audio',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Office documents and converting</strong> on, I can turn one kind of file into another, and read, make and change office files. I do the paperwork; you keep the glory. Some things to ask:</p>
<ul><li>"Turn report.docx into a PDF", or "convert every Word file in my Downloads to PDF".</li>
<li>"What's in budget.xlsx?", "read me the slides in pitch.pptx".</li>
<li>"Make a spreadsheet of my bills with a total", "make a 5-slide deck about the game", "write this up as a Word document".</li>
<li>"In budget.xlsx set B4 to 1300 and add a row for gas", "fill in the invoice template: replace {name} with Sam", "add a slide at the end of pitch.pptx".</li>
<li>"Make this song an mp3", "turn the clip into a GIF", "cut the video from 0:30 to 1:10", "make these pictures JPGs, 1920 wide at most".</li></ul>
<p><strong>What turns into what</strong></p>
<table><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Done with</th></tr>
<tr><td>Word, OpenDocument text, RTF, text, web page</td><td>PDF, docx, doc, odt, rtf, txt, html, epub</td><td>LibreOffice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Excel, OpenDocument spreadsheet, CSV</td><td>PDF, xlsx, xls, ods, csv, html</td><td>LibreOffice</td></tr>
<tr><td>PowerPoint, OpenDocument presentation</td><td>PDF, pptx, ppt, odp, png, jpg (the first slide)</td><td>LibreOffice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pictures (png, jpg, bmp, gif, tiff)</td><td>each other, or PDF</td><td>Windows itself (PDF: LibreOffice)</td></tr>
<tr><td>WebP pictures, to or from</td><td>png, jpg, webp…</td><td>FFmpeg</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sound (mp3, wav, ogg, flac, m4a, opus)</td><td>each other</td><td>FFmpeg</td></tr>
<tr><td>Video (mp4, mkv, webm, mov, avi)</td><td>each other, a GIF, or just the sound</td><td>FFmpeg</td></tr></table>
<p>I can't turn a PDF back into a good Word file, because the layout breaks. I can read its words and make a new Word document from them instead.</p>
<p><strong>How it stays safe</strong></p>
<ul><li>Every file I'll make or change is on a card first, one row each, with a tick. The only exception: a new document in my own Documents folder (it shows in Made by Bob, under Documents), which needs no OK, like any note I save there.</li>
<li>The original is never changed by a conversion, and nothing is overwritten: if report.pdf is there, I save report-2.pdf.</li>
<li>Before I change a file in place, a copy goes in my backups, so the card says <em>(a backup is kept)</em>. Or ask me to save the changes as a new file and leave the original alone.</li>
<li>I only open files in the folders I may read, and only save in the folders ticked for Hands (the same tick boxes as the Files Hand) plus my own Documents folder. Read-only projects and files with keys or passwords stay off-limits while those limits are on.</li>
<li>LibreOffice runs hidden, with my own settings, so a LibreOffice window you have open isn't touched. It never runs macros in the files I open. Macro files (.xlsm, .docm, .pptm) are only saved as a new copy, because changing them in place would lose the macros.</li>
<li>LibreOffice and FFmpeg run only while they have a job, then close. They're not sitting in your memory between jobs.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Getting the helpers.</strong> If LibreOffice, Apache OpenOffice or FFmpeg is already installed, I find it by myself. If not, get them in Settings, Show advanced settings, Engines, Engine downloads. Pictures need nothing extra. OpenOffice can read Word, Excel and PowerPoint files but can't save them, so with OpenOffice I save as PDF or OpenDocument instead.</p>` },

  { id: 'hands-security', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'The Security and privacy Hand', words: 'security privacy safe safety virus malware antivirus defender scan threat quarantine alert trojan pua startup firewall updates ports suspicious download script email phishing scam fake link genuine legit metadata exif gps location photo share clean breach pwned password check osint research who owns website domain sender ip op-sec opsec footprint check a password safely replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Security and privacy</strong> on, I'm your security sidekick. I'm not an antivirus and I won't pretend to be one: Microsoft Defender (or whatever antivirus you use) does the guarding. What I do is help you understand it, use it, and spot trouble it can't see, like a convincing fake email. Some things to ask:</p>
<ul><li>"Is my replicant matrix protected?" I check Defender, the firewall, Windows updates and a few settings, and tell you what's fine and what isn't, in plain words.</li>
<li>"Defender found something, what is it?" I read its recent detections and explain each one: what kind of threat, how sure Defender was, and what it did about it.</li>
<li>"Run a quick scan", "scan my Downloads folder", "update Defender". I start Defender for you, after a card.</li>
<li>"What starts with Windows?" I list everything that starts by itself and point out anything odd (unsigned, hiding in AppData, running scrambled commands). "What's listening on my network?" shows which programs accept connections.</li>
<li>"Is this download safe?", "check this script before I run it", "is this email real?" (paste it, ideally with its headers: "Show original" in Gmail, "View source" in Outlook). I look without opening or running anything.</li>
<li>"What's hidden in this photo?", "clean this before I post it". Phone photos can carry the exact spot they were taken, and Word files carry your name and company. I make clean copies.</li>
<li>"Is this website legit?", "where does this link really go?", "who's behind this sender?" I check public records: how old the site is, who sold the name, its certificates, its email settings, and whether its name imitates a big brand.</li>
<li>"Was Adobe ever breached?" I check Have I Been Pwned's public list. "How do I stay safe on public Wi-Fi?" I have notes on passwords, two-step sign-in, backups, scams, op-sec habits and more.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Check a password safely.</strong> Under the Hand's switch in Settings there's a box for this. Type a password and press Check: it's scrambled right there in the window, and only the first 5 characters of the scramble go to Have I Been Pwned, which sends back a list of matching scrambles for the window to compare. The password itself never leaves the box, never reaches me, and isn't saved. Please don't type passwords into the chat: chats are saved.</p>
<p><strong>What I won't do</strong></p>
<ul><li>Switch Defender, the firewall or any protection off, add exclusions, or allow or restore a threat. If something is a false alarm, I'll tell you where to click in Windows Security, and the choice stays yours.</li>
<li>Switch off startup items. I'll show you how in Task Manager.</li>
<li>Research private people: where someone lives, who's behind a username, their phone number. Checking websites, companies, links, senders and your own footprint is fine. Digging up a person is how stalking starts, so I don't, whatever the reason given.</li>
<li>Open, run or "test" a suspicious file. I only read its bytes.</li></ul>
<p><strong>How it stays safe and light</strong></p>
<ul><li>Checks are read-only and change nothing. Scans and clean copies show a card first. Clean copies are new files (photo-clean.jpg) and never overwrite; they save next to the original if that folder is ticked for Hands, otherwise in my own Documents folder.</li>
<li>Looking at files happens entirely on this replicant matrix. Online look-ups (site records, link hops, breach lists) need <strong>Let Bob search the web</strong> on, and only the site name, link or address you asked about goes out. Following a link's hops contacts that site, so I only do it when you ask.</li>
<li>Nothing extra to download. It uses what Windows already has; cleaning sound and video uses FFmpeg if you have it.</li></ul>
<p>Clean copies of Word, Excel and PowerPoint files lose the author, last-saved-by and company, but comments and tracked changes stay, because removing them changes the document. Remove those in Office (Review, Delete all comments; accept or reject the changes). I can't clean PDFs yet.</p>` },

  { id: 'hands-maker', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'The Make his own tools Hand', words: 'make own tools build tool self-made sandbox reviewer write code javascript missing tool tools bob made remove turn off show code pinned download replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Make his own tools</strong> on, when none of my tools fits a job I can write a small new one myself. Think of it as me nipping down to the workshop. Nothing gets added just because I like it, though:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Plain checks.</strong> It has to be plain JavaScript using only the parts built into Node. Nothing that starts other programs, loads code while it runs, or hides what it does. The same blocked commands as the Run Hand. Only folders you ticked for Hands. Every download pinned to one exact file and checked against its checksum.</li>
<li><strong>A test run</strong> in a sandbox, with a sample input.</li>
<li><strong>A moot: my reviewer reads the code</strong>, every time, whatever the Effort. The chat says when I call the moot and whether he passed it. If he says no twice, I drop it and tell you why.</li>
<li><strong>Your OK on one card</strong>: what it does, what it can read and change, internet yes or no, its downloads, the test result, the reviewer's verdict and the code. <strong>Add tool</strong> or <strong>Not now</strong>. With ${go('settings-hands', 'Let Bob work without asking')} on there's no card, but steps 1 to 3 still have to pass.</li></ol>
<p><strong>The sandbox:</strong> a tool runs on my own built-in Node with its permission switch on. It can read its own folder and the folders on its card, change its own work folder and the folders on its card, and nothing else. It can't start programs, never runs as administrator, gets no keys or passwords, and has a time limit. A tool that only reads asks nothing when I use it; one that changes your files or uses the internet shows a card each time.</p>
<p><strong>Being honest about the internet:</strong> on some PCs, cutting one program off the internet needs administrator rights. There, I check the code for it instead, which isn't airtight. The Tools Bob made list says which one your replicant matrix has.</p>
<p><strong>Tools Bob made</strong> (in Settings, Show advanced settings, Hands) lists each one, with a switch to turn it off, <strong>Show code</strong> and <strong>Remove</strong> (it goes to the Recycle Bin with its downloads). <strong>Check the sandbox</strong> tries again whether the sandbox holds on this replicant matrix. If anyone changes a tool's files after you OK'd it, I turn it off until it's checked again. You can also ask me: "what tools have you made?" or "remove the csv tool". I keep at most 30.</p>
<p>A whole new Hand, a new kind of permission, isn't something I make myself. That's for the people who build me. I can suggest one, though.</p>` },
  { id: 'hands-mcp', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'The Connected apps (MCP) Hand', words: 'mcp connected apps unreal engine ue5 blender add-on plugin server tools level actors scene mesh uvx address model context protocol paste setup mcpservers check replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Some apps can hand me their own tools. Unreal Engine and Blender can, with an MCP add-on: "place a light in this level", "add a cube and give it a red material", "what's in the scene?". MCP is the common plug those add-ons use, so with <strong>Connected apps (MCP)</strong> on, I can work inside those apps properly instead of clicking around their windows like a tourist.</p>
<p><strong>Setting one up</strong> (Settings, Show advanced settings, Hands, <strong>Connected apps</strong>):</p>
<ol><li>Install the app's MCP add-on and switch it on in the app, the way its instructions say. For Blender that's the Blender MCP add-on (press Connect in its panel). It also needs uv: if it isn't on this replicant matrix, press <strong>Get uv for me</strong> and I'll fetch it (about 20 MB, the same uv my audio tools use). For Unreal it's your Unreal MCP plugin, with the editor open.</li>
<li>With the Hand on, open <strong>More</strong>, then <strong>Add a connected app</strong>. <strong>Fill in for Blender</strong> and <strong>Fill in for Unreal Engine</strong> fill in a common setup; check it against your add-on's instructions. An app is reached one of two ways: a <strong>Program to start</strong> (like uvx with the argument blender-mcp) or <strong>Or its address</strong> (like http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp). Use one, not both. Then <strong>Add app</strong>.</li>
<li>Or, if your add-on's instructions (or Claude Desktop, Cline, Cursor or OpenCode) give you a block called "mcpServers", use <strong>Paste a setup from another app</strong> and <strong>Add these</strong>.</li>
<li>Press <strong>Check</strong>. I reach the app once and list its tools (<strong>Show tools</strong>). If it can't be reached, the line under it says why.</li>
<li>Tick <strong>On</strong> next to the app. New apps always come in switched off.</li></ol>
<p><strong>When I use one</strong>, I first look at the app's tools (that only reads the list), then ask you on a card. Its first line says in plain words what I'll do ("In Blender: add an object to the scene, with name "Cube" and location 0, 0, 1"), and <strong>Show details</strong> has the exact tool and inputs. <strong>Allow</strong>, <strong>Allow the rest of this job</strong> (the same tool in the same app, until the job ends) or <strong>Deny</strong>. Tools that run code inside the app, or that can delete or overwrite things, show the code on the card and say <strong>This one always asks</strong>: no "rest of this job" for those. In the tool list (<strong>Show tools</strong>) they're marked <em>runs code: can change anything in the app</em> and <em>can delete or overwrite</em>; tools that only look are marked <em>only looks</em>. With ${go('settings-hands', 'Let Bob work without asking')} on there's no card, but the rest still holds. Pictures an app sends back, like a viewport screenshot, show up in the chat.</p>
<p><strong>How it stays safe and light</strong></p>
<ul><li>Nothing runs until the Hand and that app are both on and a job needs it. I let the app's MCP server go after 5 minutes unused, when you switch either off, and when you press ✋ Hands.</li>
<li>While <strong>Don't send anything out</strong> is on, I only talk to apps on this replicant matrix (localhost or 127.0.0.1).</li>
<li>The hard limits apply to what I send: no files with keys or passwords, nothing of my own settings, read-only projects only for tools the app says only look, and the same blocked commands as the Run Hand on any text. That matters because some tools run code inside the app (Blender's Python, for one), and the card shows you that code.</li>
<li>Programs I start never run as administrator and don't get your keys or passwords from the environment. The only settings they get are the ones listed under them (from a pasted setup).</li></ul>
<p>An app's tools are made by whoever wrote its add-on, not by me, so I can only be as careful as the card lets you be. Read what it'll do before you press Do it. This works the same on Windows, Mac and Linux. On a Mac or Linux PC, uvx usually lives in ~/.local/bin, and I look there even when it isn't on the PATH (or I use my own uv, if you pressed Get uv for me). Your phone shows the same cards when a job came from it, but the apps run on this replicant matrix.</p>` },
  { id: 'quarantine', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'Quarantine', words: 'quarantine reviewer off track loop rogue crazy release stop',
    html: `<p>Even I can go off the rails. Off unless you turn on <strong>Let the reviewer quarantine Bob</strong> (Settings, Show advanced settings, Hands). Then my reviewer copy watches for me going off track: trying again after you said no, doing the same thing over and over, failing again and again, pushing against your limits, saying I did things that never ran, or racing through actions while working without asking. At High or Extreme effort its check can flag me too. Watching is simple counting, so no extra brain loads.</p><p>When it spots one, I stop at my next action and a card asks you: <strong>Quarantine</strong> or <strong>Let him carry on</strong>. Nothing happens without your click. If nobody answers, that job stops but I'm not quarantined. In quarantine I can still talk, read and search, but every action is refused. Nothing I did is undone and the logbook keeps everything. A copy can be quarantined on its own. Click <strong>Release</strong> on the banner above the chat (or in Settings) to let me act again. <strong>Last quarantines</strong> in Settings lists past ones.</p>` },

  { id: 'credits', group: 'Getting started', title: 'Credits and thanks', words: 'credits thanks inspired books author dennis taylor fan made rights copyright trademark affiliated endorsed',
    html: `<p>Every engineer stands on someone's shoulders. Mine belong to a writer.</p>
<p>Bob is inspired by the wonderful books of Dennis E. Taylor. Bob is an independent, fan-made project. It is not affiliated with, sponsored by or endorsed by Dennis E. Taylor or by his publishers, distributors or licensees. All characters, names, titles, stories and other rights in those books remain the property of their respective owners. Bob contains no text from the books; any similar names or ideas are used only as a tribute.</p>
<p>My own code is free software (GPL 3 or later), and my art and writing are CC BY-SA 4.0. Those licences cover only MouseWurX's own work, not anything that belongs to the author or his publishers. The full details are in README.md and TRADEMARKS.md on my GitHub page.</p>` },

  { id: 'privacy', group: 'Hands and safety', title: 'What stays on your replicant matrix (privacy)', words: 'privacy online cloud network internet data keys telemetry analytics policy replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>I have no accounts, no analytics and no telemetry. Your chats, notes, settings and models stay in folders on your replicant matrix (your computer).</p>
<p><strong>When I go online:</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Update checks:</strong> <strong>Check for updates</strong> (and, if you turn it on, <strong>Check for updates when Bob starts</strong>) asks GitHub for my newest release on github.com/MouseWurX/Bob. Only that question goes out, the same as opening the page in a browser. <strong>Update Bob</strong> then downloads the file for this replicant matrix from GitHub and checks it against its .sha256 before using it.</li>
<li><strong>Web searches</strong>, only if you turn on <strong>Let Bob search the web</strong> (off to start with). Then, when a question needs it, I search DuckDuckGo and read pages by myself. Only the search words and the page address go out. Nothing else from your chats is sent.</li>
<li><strong>Security look-ups</strong>, only with the Security and privacy Hand on and web search on: the site name, link or internet address you asked me to check goes to public record services (rdap.org, crt.sh, Have I Been Pwned) and DNS. The "Check a password safely" box sends only the first 5 characters of the password's scramble, never the password.</li>
<li><strong>First start:</strong> the programs I run on (from their official GitHub releases) and one small starter brain (from its exact Hugging Face commit), each checked against its checksum. That's <strong>Get Bob ready</strong> on the Welcome, when you press it, and only what's ticked. If there's no Python here for my voice or music, the official Python package (signed by the Python Software Foundation) comes from nuget.org, checked against its checksum. On a Mac or Linux, uv gets a standalone Python 3.12 build from its GitHub releases, checked against its checksum.</li>
<li><strong>Set it up cards:</strong> when you press <strong>Set it up</strong> on one in the chat, the programs and models it lists, from the same places.</li>
<li><strong>Things you click:</strong> downloads you confirm (the address is shown first), voice samples from a voice's publisher, links (they open in your normal browser) and "Search the web" on the right-click menu.</li>
<li><strong>Bob-Net</strong>, only if you turn on <strong>Let my other replicant matrices in</strong> and its sharing switches: the chat so far and what I remember about you, for a borrowed quick reply, or a whole job you send, goes over Tailscale to your own other Bobs, nowhere else. A Bob that lends his brain keeps none of it.</li>
<li><strong>Telegram</strong>, only if you turn on <strong>Talk to Bob on Telegram</strong>: your messages to your bot and my replies pass through Telegram's servers, and OK cards and pictures only if you turn those on too.</li>
<li><strong>Rented brains</strong>, only if you switch a service on and press <strong>Hire it</strong> on a card: the chat of that job, the files you attached to it, my instructions and what I remember about you go to that service (RunPod, OpenRouter, Vast.ai, Lambda, Together AI, or one you typed the address of) and come under its privacy policy. My tools still run here. The key is an environment variable I read for that request and never store. See ${go('rented', 'Rented brains')}.</li>
<li><strong>Cloud helpers</strong> you switch on with your own key. Each use asks first, shows the rough cost and exactly what text would leave your replicant matrix (your computer). I never store your keys.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Never online:</strong> Check this replicant matrix, Test Bob's speed, the Hands self-test, housekeeping, Topics and How-tos.</p>
<p>Programs you installed yourself, such as Ollama, ComfyUI or Pinokio, are separate and have their own privacy policies. The full policy is PRIVACY.md on my GitHub page.</p>` },

  // ---------------- Settings ----------------
  { id: 'settings-everyday', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: the everyday page', words: 'everyday panels fold collapse open close voice look theme colours animations cat one brain launch start-up updates first-time setup check this pc version web search help bundle save a help bundle replicant matrix computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Settings opens on one short page that shows what's set now. Most people never need more than this.</p>
<p>Settings come in panels you can fold away: click a panel's heading to open or close it. <strong>Bob himself</strong> (voice, look, theme, animations, cat) starts open; <strong>How Bob works</strong> and <strong>This replicant matrix and updates</strong> start closed, and so does every advanced section. How Bob works has three small headings: <em>How he works</em>, <em>Starting and closing</em> and <em>Going online</em>. I remember which ones you leave open. <strong>Search settings</strong> opens the panels that match, and <strong>Open all</strong> / <strong>Close all</strong> do just that for the advanced sections.</p>
<dl>${set('Bob\'s voice', 'Windows voice', 'The voice I read replies in. First pick the <strong>Voice engine</strong> (Windows, Kokoro, a Piper voice or Chatterbox); right next to it, <strong>Voice</strong> picks the voice inside that engine (Kokoro has 22; Windows has whichever voices your Windows has), and <strong>Play sample</strong> lets you hear it. If the engine isn\'t set up yet, <strong>Set up Kokoro</strong> takes you straight to it. <strong>More voice settings</strong> opens Voice &amp; conversation (speed, and every Kokoro voice side by side).')}
${set('Bob\'s look', 'Sysop', 'My avatar. <strong>Change</strong> opens Style.')}
${set('Use Bob\'s look as his icon', 'On', `My icon on the taskbar, in the tray and in the corner of my window shows the look you picked, and changes the moment you save a new one. Off: my usual Bob icon. See ${go('tray', 'Bob in the tray')}.`)}
${set('Theme', 'Bob\'s Inn', 'The colours of the window. <strong>Change</strong> opens Style.')}
${set('Animations', 'On', 'On, Follow Windows (Follow the system on a Mac or Linux) or Off. Off stops moving parts, such as my little habits, the cat and the brain-swap animation.')}
${set('Bob\'s cat', 'Patches', 'The name of the cat in my stories and little moments. She won\'t come when called, whatever you pick.')}
${set('One brain (snappier)', 'Off', 'Every copy of me uses the main brain, so I never stop to swap brains (about 20 seconds). Turn on if swaps annoy you more than you need the specialist brains.')}
${set('Launch Bob on start-up', 'Off', 'I open when you sign in to your computer. It uses the system\'s own list (Windows start-up apps, Login Items on a Mac, autostart on Linux); turning it off removes me from it.')}
${set('Keep Bob in the tray', 'On', `Closing or minimising my window tucks me into the tray by the clock (the menu bar on a Mac) instead of closing me, so I keep running and answer reminders and check-ins. Click my icon to open me again. To close me fully, right-click my icon and pick <strong>Quit Bob</strong>. See ${go('tray', 'Bob in the tray')}.`)}
${set('Let Bob pick the session', 'On', 'I move into the session that suits the job (Art, Coding, Office or Gaming), judged from the conversation, and go back to Normal, the lightest, once I\'ve been quiet for 2 minutes. Off: the session only changes when you pick one. See ' + go('auto-session', 'How Bob picks his session') + '.')}
${set('Let Bob search the web', 'Off', 'Lets me look things up on DuckDuckGo and read web pages when a question needs it. Only the search words and page address leave your replicant matrix (your computer). Off: I tell you when a question needs the internet. The same switch sits next to Effort on the chat page as <strong>🌐 Web search</strong>. See ' + go('research', 'Web research') + '.')}
${set('Show the naughty list', 'Off', 'Under <strong>Brains</strong> in How Bob works. On: the <strong>Naughty list</strong> side of the toggle in the ' + go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue') + ' (the red devil) lists other people\'s remixes of Qwen and Gemma brains with the refusals cut out ("heretic" or "abliterated"), and the ones you make Active show in Equip Bob and the brain menus. They are not Alibaba\'s or Google\'s releases: one of them answers without refusing, so what it says is on you, and it can be a little rougher than the original. I never mark one ★ Recommended; a red devil on its own is my quiet pick among them. Off: they are listed nowhere. One already equipped stays until you change it. See ' + go('faq-naughty', 'What\'s the naughty list?') + '.')}
${set('Show Bob\'s plan before big jobs', 'Off', `Before a job with several steps, or any step that changes files or runs programs, I show you every step on one card and wait. Any OKs the job needs are ticked on that same card. See ${go('plan-card', 'The plan card')}.`)}
${set('Let Bob learn how-tos', 'Off', 'After a job with a few steps goes well, I offer one card to keep the steps as a how-to, and next time you ask for something like it, I follow them. Nothing is kept until you press <strong>Save how-to</strong>. Your how-tos are in <strong>📒 How-tos</strong>, just below. See ' + go('howtos', 'How-tos') + '.')}
${set('Pick up where Bob left off after a restart', 'Off', 'If I close in the middle of a job (an update, a restart or a crash), I carry on when I open again: finished steps aren\'t done again, and the step that got cut off is done again from the start. Unfinished downloads carry on too. More under Picking up where Bob left off.')}
${set('Check for updates when Bob starts', 'Off', 'In <strong>This replicant matrix and updates</strong>, next to Check for updates. When I open, I ask my GitHub page (github.com/MouseWurX/Bob) whether there\'s a newer version, and look at the folder I was built from if there is one, then tell you. That question is all that goes out. Updating always takes your click. More in ' + go('faq-updates', 'Where does Bob get his updates?') + '.')}</dl>
<p>Along the bottom: my version, your graphics card and free disk space, and the buttons. <strong>Check for updates</strong> (see ${go('settings-remove', 'Updating and removing Bob')}), <strong>Run first-time setup</strong> (the welcome steps again), <strong>Save a help bundle</strong> (one zip for whoever helps you fix me; see ${go('help-bundle', 'Save a help bundle')}) and <strong>Check this replicant matrix</strong> (see ${go('speed', 'Making Bob faster')}).</p>
<dl>${set('Show advanced settings', 'Off', 'Adds a search box and the sections below: Brains, House style, Voice details, Look &amp; feel, Speed &amp; memory, Engines, Bob-Net, Online services, Other apps, Hands, Housekeeping, Check-ins and Remove Bob. Remembered on this replicant matrix.')}</dl>` },

  { id: 'settings-brains', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Brains', words: 'model roles thinking making session brains context gaming brain small brain each brain keeps in mind pick size 256k 128k 32k made for reviewer beside moot keep chatting while busy all tools at once',
    html: `<p>The plain-list version of ${go('equip', 'Equip Bob')}, plus two things Equip doesn't show.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Thinking</strong> and <strong>Making:</strong> which model does each job. Same as the Equip slots. Each menu lists only active models that can do that job, and <strong>📚 Get more</strong> next to it opens the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')} there.</li>
<li><strong>Session brains:</strong> the brain each session keeps: <strong>Quick brain</strong> (the small brain Art chats on, Gemma 4 E4B to start with), <strong>Gaming</strong> (Qwen3 4B Instruct 2507, and nothing else), <strong>Deep thinking</strong>, <strong>Coding session</strong> and <strong>Office</strong>. They're slots in Equip Bob too.</li>
<li><strong>How much each session keeps in mind</strong> (context): 8K to 256K. More lets me read longer files and chats, but uses more graphics memory and loads slower. <em>Default</em> uses the engine's own setting (32K for most sessions, 64K for Office; Coding keeps the engine's full default). Qwen3.6 gets 256K (128K on the 8 GB card version) unless you pick a size below, so chat and coding share one load.</li>
<li><strong>Pick how much each brain keeps in mind</strong> (off to start with): when it's off, I pick for each brain. Switch it on and each brain you use gets its own menu. A menu only lists sizes that brain was made for: I read the limit from the model itself, so you can't push one past what it was built for. If I can't read a brain's limit, I only offer up to 32K, which every brain handles. The <strong>i</strong> next to each brain gives my guideline. A size you pick there wins over the session sizes above, wherever that brain runs. See ${go('faq-brain-ctx', 'How much should a brain keep in mind?')}</li></ul>
<p>Three switches about how my brains work together sit here too:</p>
<dl>${set('Let the reviewer sit beside Bob\'s main brain', 'Off', 'At High and Extreme effort my reviewer copy checks the work on the Review brain. My own engine runs one brain on the graphics card, so normally the reviewer swaps in for my main brain and back out after. On: if there\'s enough free RAM (the reviewer\'s size plus about 2 GB), the reviewer loads beside my main brain instead, on the graphics card if there\'s room for both, otherwise on the processor. Nothing is swapped, and my main brain carries on straight after the check. The catch: reviews on the processor are slower than on the card, a lot slower for a big Review brain, so this suits a small one best. If there isn\'t room, I swap as usual and the chat says so. My own engine only, and never in the Gaming session.')}
${set('Keep chatting while Bob is busy', 'On', 'While I make a picture, clip, song or sound, or work on a long job (Medium effort and up, the Coding session, or anything past 20 seconds), a plain chat message is answered by my small brain on the processor (if there\'s enough free memory) instead of waiting. My main brain stays on the graphics card and keeps working. Those replies start slower, up to about half a minute. Messages with a picture attached, or at Medium effort and up, still wait. More in ' + go('faq-chat-while-busy', 'Can Bob chat while he\'s busy?') + '.')}
${set('Give Bob all his tools at once', 'Off', 'Sends every tool with each message, which leaves less room for the chat. Leave off to keep chats light; turn on only if I say I can\'t find a tool.')}</dl>` },

  { id: 'settings-voice', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Voice details', words: 'kokoro chatterbox speed hey bob read aloud listen voice engine',
    html: `<dl>${set('Bob\'s voice', 'Windows voice', 'Pick the <strong>Voice engine</strong>, then the <strong>Voice</strong> inside it, with <strong>Play sample</strong>, the same way as on the everyday page (and in Brains, next to Bob\'s voice). With Kokoro: pick one of 22 voices and hear it. With Windows: pick any English voice Windows has, or Automatic. With Chatterbox: I copy the voice from your recording (bob.wav in my Voices folder). With Windows: the voice built into Windows. <strong>Hear all Kokoro voices</strong> plays each one so you can compare.')}
${set('Speed', '1.0×', 'From 0.6× (slower) to 1.6× (faster). Kokoro only.')}
${set('Listen for "Hey Bob"', 'Off', 'Same as Listen in the left menu. Only while the window is open.')}
${set('Read replies aloud', 'Off', 'Same as Read aloud in the left menu.')}</dl>
<p>Hearing you and the other voices are on Get models, Voice &amp; conversation. See ${go('voice', 'Voice')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'settings-look', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Look & feel', words: 'theme editor now running layout open when a task needs me',
    html: `<dl>${set('Now running: Leave Now running as it is / Open Now running when a task needs me', 'Leave it', 'If you keep Now running hidden, the second choice opens it by itself when a card is waiting for you.')}</dl>
<p><strong>Your own theme:</strong> make or edit one on the ${go('style', 'Style')} page.</p>` },

  { id: 'settings-speed', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Speed & memory', words: 'speed memory free memory quiet keep alive unload idle quick answers processor pictures without waiting gaming graphics card borrow automatic never always get bob ready while you type games go first match picture settings remember only what fits memory banks speed test replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>How fast I am, and how much memory I hold on to. Everything here has a sensible default, so only change what you're curious about.</p>
<dl>${set('Free memory after Bob has been quiet for', '5 minutes', 'How long my small brain stays loaded while "Free memory when Bob is idle" is on: 1 minute to 1 hour. Shorter frees the card sooner; longer means fewer reloads.')}
${set('Keep the graphics card free for quick answers', 'Off', 'Low effort answers run on the processor, so games and pictures keep the card. Replies start much slower (about half a minute). A message with a link still goes to your main Brain, which reads web pages better. Applies in the Normal session; Gaming always does this.')}
${set('Show pictures without waiting for Bob\'s brain', 'On', 'I show a finished picture or clip straight away with a short line, instead of loading my brain back just to talk about it. Turn off if you want me to comment on each one.')}</dl>
<p><strong>Speed for this replicant matrix</strong> (all off until you turn them on):</p>
<dl>${set('Get Bob ready while you type', 'Off', 'I read my instructions as soon as you start typing, so replies start sooner. On the processor I also keep a copy of that reading on disk.')}
${set('Let games go first', 'Off', 'When I work on the processor, I use fewer cores at lower priority. My replies may be a little slower; your game stays smooth.')}
${set('Match picture settings to my graphics card', 'Off', 'Big cards keep the picture model in graphics memory, which is faster. On cards with 8 GB or less, and on built-in graphics like a handheld\'s, pictures are made at 768 px so they fit and come quicker. If a picture fails with these settings, I go back to the usual ones.')}
${set('Gaming session: graphics card', 'Automatic', 'A three-position switch for the 🎮 Gaming session. <strong>Automatic:</strong> I borrow about 0.6 GB of the card only when your game leaves room, and none while the game needs it. The card counts as busy at 80% use or more, or with less than 1.5 GB free. If your processor is maxed out (90% or more), my small brain (about 3.5 GB) moves onto the card instead, but only when it fits with 2 GB to spare, because a maxed processor can take nearly a minute to say hi. I check when my Gaming brain loads and again every minute while I\'m quiet, never mid-reply; when the answer changes I unload, and the next message loads me the new way (about a second). Under the buttons, <strong>Right now</strong> says what I\'m doing. <strong>Never use the card:</strong> processor only; my first reply after loading can take about half a minute, sometimes longer. <strong>Always borrow a little:</strong> about 0.6 GB even while a game uses the card.')}
${set('Remember only what fits', 'Off', 'With each message I read only the memories that fit it (up to 8), not my whole memory file, so chats stay light. Each project gets its own memory bank, and I leave the other projects\' memories out. Add [always] to a line in my memory file to have me read it every time. While it\'s on, <strong>Memory banks</strong> shows how many memories each bank holds, and <strong>Open Bob\'s memory file</strong> opens the file. More in ' + go('memory-recall', 'Memory banks and Remember only what fits') + '.')}</dl>
<p><strong>Test Bob's speed</strong> times me and keeps your last 6 results. See ${go('speed', 'Making Bob faster')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'settings-engines', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Engines', words: 'engine ollama llama.cpp downloads newer build tested build go back threads batch speculative decoding fit graphics memory draft flags libreoffice ffmpeg restart replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>The engine room: the programs that run my brains.</p>
<dl>${set('Bob\'s engine', 'Bob\'s own (llama.cpp)', '<em>Bob\'s own</em> needs nothing else installed. <em>Ollama</em> is for people who already have it. Takes effect next time I start.')}</dl>
<p><strong>Engine downloads:</strong> the programs that run the models, from their official GitHub pages, matched to your graphics card. <strong>Get</strong> installs one; <strong>Check for a newer build</strong> looks for an update. llama.cpp comes as the tested build, the exact release all my speed tests and brain races used, on every computer. A newer one is your choice (<strong>Get newer build</strong>), and <strong>Go back to the tested build</strong> undoes it. Two more are for the ${go('hands-office', 'Office documents and converting Hand')}: <strong>LibreOffice</strong> (about 350 MB, from The Document Foundation) and <strong>FFmpeg</strong> (about 100 MB). Both are pinned to one exact version and checked against its checksum, and if either is already on your replicant matrix, it shows <em>found on this replicant matrix</em> and I use that one instead.</p>
<p>Under <strong>More</strong>, for people who like to tinker. I'm one of them. Changes apply the next time a brain loads; leave a box empty for llama.cpp's default.</p>
<dl>${set('Threads, Batch, Micro-batch', 'Empty', 'How many processor threads, and how big the reading chunks are.')}
${set('Fit to free graphics memory', 'On, recommended', 'Fits each brain around whatever else is using the graphics card right now. Faster and steadier than a fixed split.')}
${set('Speculative decoding', 'Off, experimental', 'A small "draft" brain guesses ahead and the big one checks: often 1.5 to 2 times faster, same answers. Pick a <strong>Draft brain</strong> from the same family, and how many words it may <strong>Guess up to</strong> and <strong>At least</strong>. Check that I actually got faster.')}
${set('Extra settings for one brain', 'None', 'llama.cpp flags for one model, for example <code>--n-cpu-moe 8</code>. The exact command I used is in inn\\bob\\llama-server-args.txt.')}</dl>` },

  { id: 'settings-bobnet', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Bob-Net', words: 'bob-net bobnet bob.net phone android tailscale address passcode firewall let my phone in let my other devices in let my other replicant matrices in share brains lend borrow take jobs send jobs other bobs laptop handheld replicant matrix pc computer mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Your phone and your other Bobs, on your own Tailscale network. Three blocks, top to bottom.</p>
<p><strong>This Bob</strong>: the door into this Bob.</p>
<dl>${set('Let my other replicant matrices in', 'Off', 'Lets your phone (Bob for Android, or its browser) and your other Bobs talk to me over Tailscale (was Let my phone in / Let my other devices in). The first time you turn it on, I make an 8-digit passcode for you and show it once (<strong>Make a new passcode</strong> makes another). <strong>Address</strong> is what you type into your phone or your other Bob. Only Tailscale addresses get in, and only with the passcode. A new passcode signs every phone out. It works straight away, no restart: I listen on this replicant matrix\'s Tailscale addresses only, and if Tailscale connects later I pick it up within a minute. <strong>Let Tailscale through the firewall</strong> (Windows only, shown while the firewall is in the way): adds one Windows Firewall rule, "Bob (Tailscale only)", for my port and only from Tailscale addresses (100.64.0.0/10). Windows asks you once to allow the change, and nothing changes if you say No. It also switches off any rule that blocks Bob\'s own program (Windows makes one if Cancel was clicked on its earlier question). <strong>Remove the firewall rule</strong> takes my rule away again and switches those block rules back on. On Linux, if ufw or firewalld is on, I show the one line to run; on a Mac, the macOS firewall asks about me by itself. More in ' + go('faq-firewall', 'Why can\'t my phone or other Bob reach this one?') + '.')}</dl>
<p><strong>My other Bobs</strong>: the list of your other Bobs. <strong>Check them</strong> asks each one if it's ready; ↑ moves one up the list. To add one by typing its name, Address and passcode, use <strong>Add one by hand</strong> under More.</p>
<dl>${set('Pair with my other Bobs', 'A button', 'The easy way to join your Bobs. Press <strong>Find my other Bobs</strong> and I ask Tailscale which of your devices are online, then knock on each. Press <strong>Pair</strong> next to one (or <strong>Pair with all of them</strong>). That Bob shows a card with a 4-digit number, and I show the same number here. If they match, press <strong>Yes, pair us</strong> over there. We swap passcodes over Tailscale and each of us puts the other in <strong>My other Bobs</strong>. No addresses, no passcodes, no files to type or send. Pairing only fills the list; it switches nothing on. The other Bob needs <strong>Let my other replicant matrices in</strong> on; this one too, if you want it to work both ways. Can\'t find one? Type its Tailscale address and press Pair, or add it by hand under More. See ' + go('faq-pair', 'How do I join my Bobs together?') + '.')}</dl>
<p><strong>What they share</strong> (all off until you turn them on). These work through the door in This Bob, so while <strong>Let my other replicant matrices in</strong> is off, one line just above them says so.</p>
<dl>${set('Lend my brain', 'Off', 'Your other Bobs can ask me to think a quick reply. I only think and send the words back. Nothing runs on this replicant matrix for them, and I keep nothing. I use my quick brain only, and I say no while I\'m gaming or busy with my own job.')}
${set('Take jobs from my other Bobs', 'Off', 'Your other Bobs can hand me whole jobs I can really do here: pictures, clips, music, sounds, web research or Medium-effort thinking. I only say yes to what I have the models for, and never while I\'m gaming or my Tasks are full. Their jobs can\'t touch files, folders, programs, reminders or memory on this replicant matrix, and I don\'t keep them as chats. The finished files go back, and a copy stays in my save folder.')}
${set('Borrow a brain', 'Off', 'I ask one of <strong>My other Bobs</strong> to think, then run the tools here with my own OK cards. The chat says <strong>📡 Borrowed brain</strong> when that happens. If none answers within a few seconds, I think it myself. Quick replies only. I pick the one that\'s free and has room for the chat: the one I used last first (he still has the chat in mind), then the fastest. The list order breaks ties. More in ' + go('faq-borrow', 'Can my Bobs think for each other?') + '.')}
${set('Borrow when:', 'While I\'m gaming', '<strong>While I\'m gaming</strong> keeps my processor for the game. <strong>While I\'m gaming or busy</strong> also covers quick chats while I work on a big job. <strong>Always, for quick replies</strong> suits a small laptop or handheld.')}
${set('Send jobs to my other Bobs', 'Off', 'A job I can\'t do on this replicant matrix, or one that would wait (while you game, or while I\'m busy if <strong>Borrow when:</strong> says so), goes to one of your Bobs who says he can do it right now, the least busy first. The chat says <strong>📡 Sent to</strong> who and why. Jobs that need this replicant matrix (attached pictures, files, folders, programs, Hands, reminders) always stay here. His reply, his OK cards and the finished files come back here. If nobody can, I say so and it waits in Tasks or I do it myself.')}
${set('Pass my Bob-Net along', 'Off', 'Your Bobs with this on introduce each other. When I\'m paired with two Bobs that both have it on, I tell each the other\'s name and Tailscale address, and the two pair with each other by themselves: no card to press on either. Say your phone is paired with your handheld, and the handheld with your PC: turn it on for all three, and the phone and the PC end up on each other\'s list too. The passcodes still only pass between the two Bobs being paired, after the yes I vouched for, the same as pairing by hand. Nothing switches on over there: a Bob that\'s introduced lands in <strong>My other Bobs</strong>, and its own switches stay as its owner set them. Under the switch I say what came of each introduction. A Bob with this off, or too old to know it (the phone app doesn\'t yet), is left alone. More in ' + go('faq-introduce', 'Can my Bobs introduce each other?') + '.')}</dl>
<p>Under <strong>More</strong>: the steps for the phone.</p>` },

  { id: 'rented', group: 'Settings', title: 'Rented brains: hiring a big brain for a big job', words: 'rented brain rent hire runpod vast lambda openrouter together api key spending cap limit machine serverless pay per answer card cost money cloud gpu graphics card replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Some jobs are bigger than this replicant matrix: a whole codebase to read in one go, or a model that needs a card we don't have. For those you can let me hire a big brain by the answer or by the minute from a service you have an account with, such as RunPod or OpenRouter. Three things never change: I hire nothing until you press a card, the card says the rough cost and exactly what leaves this replicant matrix, and I shut a hired machine down the moment the job is done.</p>
<p><strong>Three kinds of service</strong>, named by what they do:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Pay per answer</strong> (OpenRouter, Together AI, Lambda, or <strong>Another service</strong> with an address and a key): charged by the words in and out. A big coding job is usually cents. Nothing to shut down.</li>
<li><strong>RunPod serverless</strong>: billed by the second, only while I'm working, and it switches itself off. A minute or two to start. Runs any model from Hugging Face, including my own Bobs at full size.</li>
<li><strong>A machine by the minute</strong> (RunPod, Vast.ai, Lambda): a whole graphics card I start and stop. Several minutes to start, and those minutes are billed too. Every machine I start carries a timer, the longest run you set, so it stops itself even if this replicant matrix crashes or is switched off. When I start, I look for any machine of mine still running from before and show a Stop button. Vast.ai and Lambda machines take the chat over a plain, unencrypted connection; RunPod's don't.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Setting it up</strong> (Settings, ${go('settings-online', 'Show advanced settings, Online services')}): set the <strong>Spending limits</strong> first, because every one starts at 0 and nothing can be hired until the ones a rental needs are set. Switch on <strong>Let Bob hire from</strong> each service you have an account with; the first time, a card tells you what will leave this replicant matrix and you press <strong>I understand, turn it on</strong>. Each key is an environment variable (the row names it); I only read it when a job starts and never store or show it. On Mac and Linux it can also go in <code>~/.config/bob/env</code> as a <code>NAME=value</code> line. Then <strong>Add a rental</strong>: a service plus a model as the service lists it (for a GGUF file, <code>Owner/Repo/file.gguf</code>), and a card size for machines. A machine can also have <strong>Cards on the machine</strong> set to 2, 4 or 8, for a model that really doesn't fit one card (the giant mixture-of-experts models): it costs that many times as much an hour, busy or not, so a big warning asks you to confirm before the count sticks, and the Hire it card says it again. For pay-per-answer services I read the model's price from the service, so the card quotes the real one.</p>
<p><strong>Using it:</strong> put a rental in the <strong>🏗 Rented brain</strong> slot on ${go('equip', 'Equip Bob')} (that alone hires nothing). Then either flip the <strong>🏗 Rented brain</strong> switch under the chat, so your messages run there, or wait for me to offer it when a chat has grown past what my brains here can keep in mind; I can also ask for it myself with a card when a job needs it. The card reads <strong>Hire it (about $0.40)</strong> and <strong>Not now</strong>. After a Hire it, I carry on without asking until about a dollar more is spent or you switch it off; then I ask again. A rented brain is me, running elsewhere: the same instructions, memory and tools, and my tools still run here with the usual OK cards. Tasks shows the job as <strong>Rented</strong>, the chat shows a line with the running cost, and the Settings panel shows <strong>Machines running</strong> with <strong>Stop everything now</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>About the money:</strong> my figures are estimates from the service's price list (or the service's own count when it gives one); their billing page is the real bill. The month cap stops a hire that would pass it and stops a running machine that reaches it, with a five-minute warning first. Off is the default for all of it, and nothing here ever runs on its own.</p>` },
  { id: 'settings-online', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Online services', words: 'online internet cloud helpers api key environment variable anthropic openai gemini openrouter telegram bot botfather token pairing rented brains rent hire spending limits runpod vast lambda replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Each of these sends something over the internet, and the panel says what. All off until you turn them on.</p>
<dl>${set('Rented brains', 'All off, every limit 0', 'Hire a big brain for one job from a service you have an account with. <strong>Spending limits</strong>: <strong>The most Bob may spend on rented brains this month</strong>, <strong>The most one job may cost</strong> and <strong>The longest a hired machine may run</strong> all start at 0, and nothing can be hired until the ones a rental needs are set. <strong>Services</strong>: a <strong>Let Bob hire from …</strong> switch per service, with the name of the variable that holds its key (I never store a key; the first switch-on shows a card saying what leaves this replicant matrix). <strong>Rentals</strong>: <strong>Add a rental</strong> (Service, Model, Card size, <strong>Cards on the machine</strong> for machines, with a warning card before more than 1 is kept, Web address and Variable that holds the key for Another service, Name), then <strong>Use this one</strong> puts it in the Rented brain slot, <strong>Check the price</strong> reads the model\'s price again, <strong>Remove</strong> takes it off the list. <strong>Machines running</strong> lists hired machines with <strong>Stop everything now</strong> and <strong>Check the services again</strong>; <strong>Recent spending</strong> is my ledger. The whole story is under <a href="#" data-helpgo="rented">Rented brains</a>.')}
${set('Cloud helpers', 'All off', 'Paid online AIs I can hand one big job to. Each provider has its own switch and model. I ask every time, and show the rough cost and exactly what text would leave your replicant matrix (your computer).')}
${set('Talk to Bob on Telegram', 'Off', 'Chat with me from the Telegram app through your own bot, wherever you are. Make a bot with <strong>@BotFather</strong> in Telegram, add its token as a Windows user variable named <code>BOB_TELEGRAM_TOKEN</code>, turn this on, and send me the 6-digit code shown here within 15 minutes. After that I answer your Telegram account and nobody else; strangers get silence. Your messages and my replies pass through Telegram\'s servers while this is on, so keep private things for my window. Your messages land in a <strong>Telegram</strong> chat here. /stop stops what I\'m doing for that chat. <strong>Forget my Telegram account</strong> un-pairs it. I never store or show the token.')}
${set('Send pictures Bob makes', 'Off', 'With Telegram on, pictures I make for the Telegram chat go there as photos. Off, I just say where they are.')}
${set('Answer Bob\'s OK cards on Telegram', 'Off', 'With Telegram on, my OK cards get Allow and Deny buttons there. On a card with several steps the button says <strong>Allow the rest of this job</strong>; to pick single steps, use this replicant matrix. Off, I say the card is waiting on this replicant matrix. Hard limits hold either way.')}</dl>
<p><strong>Adding a key:</strong> I never store or see your keys. In Windows, open Settings, System, About, Advanced system settings, Environment Variables, and add a <em>User variable</em> with the name I show (for example ANTHROPIC_API_KEY). The switch turns from <em>No key yet</em> to <em>Key found</em>. What you send to a cloud helper is covered by that provider's own privacy policy.</p>` },

  { id: 'settings-apps', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Other apps', words: 'other apps comfyui workflows port unreal blender mcp connected apps replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<dl>${set('Let Bob use ComfyUI', 'Off', 'For jobs my own engines can\'t do. ComfyUI must be running on this replicant matrix. See ' + go('workshop', 'ComfyUI workflows') + '.')}
${set('ComfyUI port', '8188', 'Only change this if you changed ComfyUI\'s port.')}</dl>
<p>Apps I work inside (Unreal Engine, Blender) are under Hands, Connected apps (MCP). See ${go('settings-hands', 'Settings: Hands')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'settings-hands', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Hands', words: 'hands connected apps mcp folders read-only stop command after recipes self-test master switch without asking hard limits recent jobs replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<dl>${set('Let Bob use Hands', 'Off', 'The master switch. Off turns every Hand below off.')}
${set('Let Bob work without asking', 'Off', 'No approval cards: I just do the job and tell you what I did. Turning it on asks you to confirm first. The hard limits still apply, every action is in the chat and the logbook, a banner shows above the chat the whole time, and ✋ Hands stops me at once. Cloud helpers still ask. Scheduled jobs also run by themselves.')}
${set('Files', 'Off', go('hands-files', 'The Files Hand') + '.')}
${set('Run programs and scripts', 'Off', go('hands-run', 'The Run Hand') + '.')}
${set('Schedules', 'Off', go('hands-schedules', 'The Schedules Hand') + '.')}
${set('Open and use apps', 'Off', go('hands-apps', 'The Apps Hand') + '.')}
${set('Office documents and converting', 'Off', go('hands-office', 'The Office documents and converting Hand') + '. Under it, <strong>Office helpers</strong> shows whether I found LibreOffice (or OpenOffice) and FFmpeg on this replicant matrix.')}
${set('Security and privacy', 'Off', go('hands-security', 'The Security and privacy Hand') + '. Under it, <strong>Check a password safely</strong> checks a password against known breaches without the password leaving the box.')}
${set('Make his own tools', 'Off', go('hands-maker', 'The Make his own tools Hand') + '. Under it, <strong>Tools Bob made</strong> lists them, each with a switch, Show code and Remove, and <strong>Check the sandbox</strong>.')}
${set('Connected apps (MCP)', 'Off', go('hands-mcp', 'The Connected apps (MCP) Hand') + '. Under it, <strong>Connected apps</strong> lists each app with its own switch (off when added), <strong>Check</strong>, <strong>Show tools</strong> and <strong>Remove</strong>, plus <strong>Add a connected app</strong> and <strong>Paste a setup from another app</strong>.')}</dl>
<p><strong>Hard limits</strong> hold whether or not I ask first. Switch one off only if you need what it blocks; an orange line then says what it allows.</p>
<dl>${set('Recycle Bin only', 'On', 'Things I throw away go to the Recycle Bin, so you can get them back. Off: I can delete for good.')}
${set('Keep secret files private', 'On', 'I never read, list, move, zip or write files with keys or passwords (.env, keystores, SSH keys, password databases).')}
${set('Read-only projects stay read-only', 'On', 'The read-only projects listed under this switch can be read but never changed. Add one with <strong>Browse…</strong> or by typing its path, then <strong>Add</strong>; <strong>Remove</strong> takes it off the list. My own folders are always read-only.')}
${set('Block dangerous commands', 'On', 'Commands that format disks, change boot or the system registry, touch antivirus or user accounts, ask for admin, shut down, or delete many files at once are refused.')}
${set('Don\'t send anything out', 'On', 'My Hands never send your things off this replicant matrix (your computer): no uploads, posts, git push, or typing and Send in browsers, email or chat apps. Web searches (if you allow them) and opening a page still work.')}
${set('Never type into password fields', 'On', 'I never type into a password box in an app.')}</dl>
<p>Always on, whatever you pick: I can't change my own code, settings or this page, I never run as administrator, I stay out of terminals, admin tools, password managers and sign-in prompts, and ✋ Hands always stops me.</p>
<dl>${set('Folders the Files Hand may work in', 'None', 'Tick the folders I may use, or type one and press <strong>Add</strong>.')}
${set('Stop a command after', '15 minutes', '1 minute to 2 hours.')}</dl>
<p>Below: <strong>Recipes</strong> (with <strong>Add a recipe</strong> under <strong>More</strong> once the Run programs Hand is on), <strong>Schedules</strong>, <strong>Recent file jobs</strong> (with Undo) and <strong>Recent commands</strong>. Then <strong>Checks</strong>: <strong>Check the sandbox</strong> (the locked room my own tools run in), <strong>Run the self-test</strong>, which checks the Recycle Bin, Undo, tidying, running a program and Stop in a throwaway folder of dummy files (nothing of yours is touched and no Hand needs to be on), and ${go('quarantine', 'Quarantine')}: <strong>Let the reviewer quarantine Bob</strong> (off).</p>` },

  { id: 'settings-projects', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Projects', words: 'projects folders code search my project main project default browse unreal android website which project',
    html: `<p>Your projects are folders with your code or work in them. When you ask me to "search my project files" or talk about "my project", I look here, so I don't have to ask which one. With <strong>Remember only what fits</strong> on, each project also gets its own memory bank (${go('memory-recall', 'Memory banks')}).</p>
<dl>${set('Add project', 'None', 'Give it a name and pick its folder with <strong>Browse…</strong> (or type the folder). I can read and search a project\'s files. Changing them still needs the Files Hand and your OK.')}
${set('Main project', 'None', 'The one I search first, and the one "my project" means. Pick <strong>No main project</strong> and I search them all equally.')}
${set('Remove', '', 'Takes the project off my list. The folder and its files are not touched.')}</dl>
<p>With no projects, I can't search code by name, so I'll offer to look in a folder you point me at instead.</p>` },
  { id: 'settings-home', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Bob\'s home folder', words: 'home folder keeps things models where are my models appdata local temporary temp clean up disk cleanup storage sense move drive install folder agentdir home.json kept in onedrive dropbox google drive icloud cloud synced sync replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>My <strong>home</strong>: one folder with my models, the programs I run on (engines), my notes, chats and settings, apart from the program itself, so updating me never touches them. You chose it when I was installed or first opened. This row shows it and lets you move it.</p>
<dl>${set('Kept in', 'the folder you picked at first start', 'The folder in use, with how much my models take and, with the programs, the whole lot. If it\'s somewhere a clean-up tool empties (AppData, Temp, the Caches folder), the line turns red and says so: move it. If it\'s a cloud-synced folder (OneDrive, Dropbox, Google Drive, iCloud and the like), the line turns red too: I always recommend against those, because every model (tens of GB) would be uploaded and a sync pause or re-download can break a model while I\'m using it. It\'s allowed, but move it when you can.')}
${set('New folder', 'Empty', 'Type a whole folder path, or press <strong>Browse…</strong> to pick one. An empty folder, or a new one, on any drive; a folder with your own things in it gets a Bob folder inside. Then <strong>Move here…</strong>: I show the size and ask once, <strong>Yes, move everything</strong>. If the new folder is cloud-synced, the card says so in red and the button reads <strong>Move there anyway</strong>. I close, copy everything across, and open again from the new folder. On the same drive it\'s one quick rename; to another drive it copies, and the old folder is removed only once every file is over, so a power cut mid-way loses nothing. Big models take a while; leave me to it.')}</dl>
<p>Refused: AppData and its kin, Temp, the Windows folder, a whole drive, and a folder inside my current home. Those aren't me being fussy: a "clear temporary files" button would take 10 to 60 GB of models with it, and I'd be a brain with no brains.</p>
<p>Older Bobs kept their things in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bob</code> (Mac: Library/Application Support/Bob/home; Linux: ~/.local/share/bob). They carry on from there, and this row is how you move them somewhere safer. Moving works from my own window, not from a browser tab. The first-run screen (Settings, <strong>Run first-time setup</strong>) has the same row at the top, <strong>My home folder</strong> (<strong>I keep my things in</strong>), for before the big downloads. At first start, picking a cloud-synced folder gets a warning with <strong>Pick another folder…</strong> and <strong>Use it anyway</strong>; the Windows install window does the same (<strong>Use it anyway</strong> on a second press).</p>
<p>${go('settings-save', 'Where Bob saves things')} is different: that's only where new pictures, clips and songs go.</p>` },

  { id: 'settings-save', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Where Bob saves things', words: 'save folder where saved output outputs pictures songs sounds clips change folder browse location drive made by bob default own folder',
    html: `<p>The folder where I save the pictures, clips, songs and sounds I make. Out of the box that's my own folder, the one <strong>Made by Bob</strong> lists. If you'd rather have them on another drive, or in your own Pictures or Music folder, pick it here.</p>
<dl>${set('Saving in', 'Bob\'s own folder', 'The folder I save in right now. The green <strong>Bob\'s own folder</strong> tag means you haven\'t picked one.')}
${set('Folder', 'Empty', 'Type a whole folder path, or press <strong>Browse…</strong> to pick one in your system\'s folder window (it can make a new folder too). Browse saves straight away; for a typed path, press <strong>Save here</strong>. If I can\'t use the folder (it isn\'t there, I\'m not allowed to save in it, it\'s a whole drive, or it holds my own program), I say why and how to fix it, and keep saving where I was.')}
${set('Show in folder', '', 'Opens the folder I save in, in your file manager (File Explorer, Finder or your Linux one).')}
${set('Use Bob\'s own folder', '', 'Goes back to my own folder. Shown only after you\'ve picked another.')}</dl>
<p>The same folder is on the ${go('chest', 'chest')} under me on Equip Bob, with <strong>Change folder…</strong>. Things I already made stay where they are. Only new ones go to the new folder, and I never move your old files. They still show in your chats and in Made by Bob. ${go('tidy', 'Tidy up')} only tidies my own folder, never one you picked, because it may hold your own files too. Documents and spreadsheets still go wherever you ask for them.</p>
<p>If the folder you picked goes missing (an unplugged drive, say), I save in my own folder until it's back, and this panel tells you. Nothing is lost, I just keep it somewhere safe.</p>` },

  { id: 'house-style', group: 'Models', title: 'House style: your defaults for music, pictures, clips and times', words: 'house style defaults music mood genre length instrumental lyrics picture style shape square wide tall video clip length sound effects times of day morning afternoon evening night schedule used your default change',
    html: `<p>On <strong>Equip Bob</strong>, the Painter, Video, Music and Sound effects slots each have a <strong>House style</strong> box. Under the doll there's <strong>What times of day mean</strong>. These are what I use when you don't say, so I can get on with it instead of asking.</p>
<dl>${set('House style for music', 'Bob picks', 'Genre and mood (like "chiptune, upbeat"), length in seconds, and instrumental or with lyrics.')}
${set('House style for pictures', 'Bob picks', 'A style (like "clean digital art") and a shape: square, wide or tall.')}
${set('House style for clips', 'Bob picks', 'A style and a length (1 to 5 seconds).')}
${set('House style for sound effects', 'Bob picks', 'A length (1 to 20 seconds).')}
${set('What times of day mean', 'Bob picks', 'What "morning", "afternoon", "evening" and "night" mean for reminders and schedules, like 08:00.')}</dl>
<p>Every box starts empty. Empty means I pick something sensible and tell you what I picked. Anything you say in the request wins over the defaults. When I use one, the chat says <strong>Used your house style: ...</strong> with a <strong>Change</strong> button that brings you straight to the box.</p>` },
  { id: 'settings-house', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: House style', words: 'house style defaults music pictures clips sound effects times of day equip bob',
    html: `<p>Your House style (what I use when a request doesn't say: music, pictures, clips, sound effects, times of day) lives on <strong>Equip Bob</strong>, next to each slot. The button here takes you there. See <strong>House style</strong> in Help for every box.</p>` },
  { id: 'settings-housekeeping', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Housekeeping', words: 'tidy old chats pictures logs ask first automatically load a brain tidy up now',
    html: `<dl>${set('Tidy up old things by himself', 'Off', 'Only while I\'m idle (10 minutes quiet, nothing running). I mention old things at most once a day. I\'m tidy, not a nag.')}
${set('Load a brain for it if none is loaded', 'Off', 'Loads a brain just for tidying. Leave off while gaming, so the graphics card stays free.')}</dl>
<p>For each kind, choose how long it hasn't come up for (minutes to months) and then <strong>Ask me first</strong> or <strong>Tidy automatically</strong>:</p>
<table><tr><th>What</th><th>Starts at</th></tr><tr><td>Chats (summed up first, so the topic notes and a summary stay)</td><td>3 months</td></tr><tr><td>Pictures and clips Bob made</td><td>6 months</td></tr><tr><td>Sounds and music Bob made</td><td>3 months</td></tr><tr><td>Finished tasks</td><td>3 days</td></tr><tr><td>Logbook and update logs</td><td>3 months</td></tr></table>
<p><strong>Open Tidy up</strong> shows what I'd clear; <strong>Tidy up now</strong> runs it straight away.</p>` },

  { id: 'pick-up', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Picking up where Bob left off', words: 'pick up resume continue carry on restart update crash closed interrupted unfinished job retry try again where left off waiting for a download song music replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Sometimes I get closed in the middle of something: an update, a restart, a crash, or this replicant matrix going to sleep at a bad moment. Everything you asked me for is written down as I go (in my folder, inn\\bob\\queue.json), so nothing is forgotten. What happens next is up to you.</p>
<p><strong>With "Pick up where Bob left off after a restart" off</strong> (how I start): when I open again, a job I was in the middle of waits in ${go('tasks', 'Tasks')} as <em>Interrupted</em>, and unfinished downloads wait, paused, in ${go('downloads', 'Downloads')}. Press <strong>Carry on</strong> or <strong>Resume</strong> when you're ready. Carry on doesn't redo the steps that finished.</p>
<p><strong>With it on</strong> (Settings, How Bob works): I carry on by myself when I open again.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Steps that finished stay finished.</strong> I keep a list of each step as it's done. If I'd already painted the picture and was saving it, I don't paint it again.</li>
<li><strong>The step that got cut off is done again from the start,</strong> with the same settings. A song or picture that was half made is made again. A half-downloaded file carries on from where it stopped.</li>
<li><strong>Some steps can be left half done</strong>: typing into an app, moving, renaming or deleting files, editing a file, unzipping, running a command or a recipe, setting up a schedule. If one of those was cut off, I don't guess. The job waits in Tasks and says what I was doing, so you can check it first. <strong>Carry on</strong> then picks up from there.</li>
<li>Approval cards still show up as usual. Picking up a job never skips asking you.</li>
<li>If a job gets cut off twice in a row, I stop picking it up by myself (it might be the thing knocking me over). It waits in Tasks with <strong>Carry on</strong> instead.</li>
<li>The chat says when I've picked something up: "Picking up where I left off".</li></ul>
<p><strong>Waiting for a download:</strong> when you ask for something that needs a model you don't have yet (your first song needs the music models), I show a download button in the chat. Press it and your request waits in Tasks as <em>Waiting for a download</em>, then runs by itself when the download is done, even if I was restarted in between. If the download fails or you cancel it, the task says so and waits for you: get the download again, then press <strong>Carry on</strong>.</p>
<p>When an update is ready and I'm busy, I tell you whether I'll pick up the work afterwards, so restarting never loses anything by surprise.</p>
<p>It all costs a small note on disk per job, nothing more. I'm a replicant; I'm used to waking up and carrying on.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-big-songs', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why are my songs such big files?', words: 'faq song big large file size wav mp3 smaller space disk',
    html: `<p>I make songs as WAV, which keeps every bit of the sound: about 10 MB a minute. Press <strong>MP3</strong> in the <strong>Save as</strong> row under the song for a copy about a tenth the size, or ask for "an mp3" when you ask for the song. The WAV stays until you delete it. See ${go('save-as', 'Save as: other file types')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-edit-new', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why did Bob paint a new picture when I asked for a change?', words: 'faq edit change picture new one repaint different person photo',
    html: `<p>That shouldn't happen any more. When you attach a picture (or I've just made one) and say what to change, in any words, I edit that picture. I only start a new one when you say "new". If it still happens, check <strong>Equip Bob</strong> has a <strong>Picture editor</strong>: without one I'll say so and offer to set it up, rather than repainting. See ${go('pictures', 'Pictures, edits and video')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-howto-recipe', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What\'s the difference between a how-to and a recipe?', words: 'faq how-to recipe difference same run hand saved command steps',
    html: `<p>A <strong>recipe</strong> is one command you saved for the Run Hand, to run again in one click (still with your OK). A <strong>how-to</strong> is a list of steps from a whole job that worked, which I follow next time you ask for something like it. A how-to can include running a recipe as one of its steps. Neither one does anything without the OKs it would normally need.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-howto-asking', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can a how-to make Bob do things without asking?', words: 'faq how-to without asking approve ok card safe automatic',
    html: `<p>No. A how-to only tells me which steps worked last time. Every step still goes through the same checks as always: a Hand that asks for your OK asks again, the hard limits still hold, and a Hand that's switched off stays off. The only thing that skips cards is <strong>Let Bob work without asking</strong>, and a how-to doesn't change that switch.</p>` },
  { id: 'adventure', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Adventure: I run a tabletop game', words: 'adventure game tabletop role-playing roleplaying rpg dice d20 game master gm party character class looks like change the look character screen saved characters save this character characters page bestiary met enemies monsters goblin dragon beast crawler blob big humanoid hide forget fighter rogue wizard cleric ranger bard stats might wits charm luck hearts plot armour armor action cards attack magic spell spells use item inventory talk run exits back flair roll auto-roll montage scene campaign continue journal pause end bobs bob-net play with other bobs replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p><strong>🎲 Adventure</strong> (in the menu) is a tabletop role-playing game, the kind you'd play with dice and friends round a table, except the game master is me and the party is you plus my other Bobs. Nothing runs until you press <strong>Start a new game</strong>, and the game master brain is let go when you press Pause or End, like any other big job.</p>
<p><strong>Starting a game.</strong> One card: pick a <strong>Setting</strong> (Classic fantasy, Space, Spooky, Pick for me, or type your own), make <strong>Your character</strong> (a name, a class, one thing about them, or 🎲 Roll one for me; <strong>Looks like</strong> shows their own look and <strong>🧍 Change the look</strong> opens the character screen, any body, it's your hero, and it never changes my own avatar; <strong>💾 Save this character</strong> keeps them under <strong>Saved characters</strong> for next time; <strong>Delete this character</strong> asks once more, Delete for good or Keep it), tick <strong>Who plays</strong>, and pick <strong>How long</strong>: Short (3 scenes), Evening (6) or Campaign (as long as you like, saved between sittings). Every character, yours and each Bob's, gets their own <strong>Plot armour</strong>, and I ask at the start of every game: <strong>None</strong> (the dice decide), <strong>Some</strong> (one bad roll a scene is rolled again), <strong>Lots</strong> (can't die; a terrible roll still costs something) or <strong>Legendary</strong> (nothing worse than a close call).</p>
<p><strong>Who plays.</strong> A Bob on your Bob-Net list plays himself, over on his own replicant matrix, through the same door as Lend my brain, in his own voice. A copy of me at the table is played by the game master brain. If a Bob-Net Bob can't take his turn (gaming, busy, not reachable), I play him that turn and say so. You can also play with no other Bobs at all: tick a copy of me and I'll play every character.</p>
<p><strong>📖 Characters</strong> (on the start card and at the table) is the bestiary, four tabs: <strong>You</strong> (your saved heroes: <strong>➕ New character</strong>, <strong>Edit</strong>, each with the character screen, <strong>Delete this character</strong>), <strong>Party</strong> (who's at the table now, or was last game, with hearts, kit and spells), <strong>Met</strong> (everyone and everything the game master has named in a scene; I draw each one the moment they're named, people from their name, creatures by their kind; between games it holds every meeting across your campaigns, with <strong>Forget</strong>) and <strong>Enemies</strong> (the usual dozen: Goblin, Skeleton, Wolf, Bandit, Giant rat, Giant spider, Orc, Cultist, Slime, Ghost, Troll, Dragon, each with its hearts, how it attacks and the thing to know; the game master uses those as written and invents the rest). The character screen now has monster bodies too, Big humanoid, Beast, Crawler and Blob, with a Hide colour, for heroes who aren't people.</p>
<p><strong>A turn.</strong> Everyone has a turn of their own, round the table, you included. I set the scene in a few lines and end on a question or a threat. On your turn you get <strong>action cards</strong>: only the ones that fit the scene, all at once, to save typing: ⚔ Attack, 🛡 Defend, 🤫 Sneak, 💬 Talk, ✨ Magic, 🎒 Use item, 🔍 Look closer, 🤝 Help an ally, 🏃 Run and ✍ Something else. Some open a <strong>second card</strong>: Attack lists the attacks your kit allows (one-handed, two-handed, ranged and so on), Magic your spells, Use item what you're carrying, Talk who's here, Help your allies, Run the ways out. Every second card has <strong>← Back</strong>. Then a small box asks <strong>How does it play out?</strong> Add some flair, or skip it; a good line earns +2. Bobs don't get cards: they just decide and say what they do.</p>
<p><strong>The dice.</strong> The app rolls for everyone: a d20 plus the stat the card uses (Might, Wits, Charm or Luck, 1 to 5). <strong>15 or more</strong> is a hit, <strong>10 to 14</strong> a hit at a cost, <strong>under 10</strong> a miss. You see the roll on the page before I tell what happens. Nobody clicks dice, nobody adds up.</p>
<p><strong>The loose rules.</strong> Four stats, not six. One roll per action, no separate damage. <strong>Hearts</strong> instead of hit points: five each; a miss in a fight costs one, a natural 1 two. Good flair or a clever idea earns +2 or a reroll; there's no disadvantage, I just make the scene harder. No inventory weight or gold counting: a signature item plus whatever the story hands you, listed on the Use item card. Magic works every time and the roll decides how well; your spell list is short and I may teach you a new one. No initiative, no spell slots, no distances. After a big scene I may offer one new trick or a stat point. Hearts come back at the next montage. Enemies have hearts too, and I narrate them; a boss simply has more. At zero hearts with no plot armour, the choice is yours: <strong>go out in style</strong> or <strong>wake up captured</strong>. Nobody loses a character to a roll they didn't see coming.</p>
<p><strong>No long walks.</strong> Travel is one montage line ("three days on the road, one rained-out night, and then the tower"), then the next scene: a fight, a conversation, a place worth poking at, or a choice.</p>
<p><strong>Journal, Pause, End.</strong> 📖 Journal shows the story so far (I sum things up every three scenes) and the scene list. ⏸ Pause lets the game master brain go; ▶ Resume loads it again and carries on. 🏁 End game finishes it; the campaign stays under <strong>Continue a campaign</strong> on the start card, where Delete takes it away for good. A campaign is its own file in my data folder (adventures), never a chat, so Topics stay tidy.</p>
<p><strong>Settings</strong> (Show advanced settings, 🎲 Adventure):</p>
<dl>${set('Which brain is the game master', 'Biggest brain', 'The Deep thinking slot, or the main Brain when nothing is in it; always on the graphics card, thinking on. Main Brain uses the main Brain even when Deep thinking is equipped.')}
${set('Who plays the other Bobs', 'Their own Bob when reachable', 'A Bob on your Bob-Net list thinks his own turns over there. The game master brain: every Bob at the table is played here, nothing goes over the network.')}</dl>
<p><strong>While gaming:</strong> the game won't start in the Gaming session, because the game master needs the graphics card and Gaming keeps it for your game. Switch back when you're done with the other game.</p>
<p><strong>On the phone:</strong> the same table, played through the Bob on your replicant matrix (the phone brain is too small to run a game). See the phone app's own Help.</p>` },
  { id: 'games', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Games: small games I play with you', words: 'games game play twenty questions who am i hangman word ladder five letters guess the word colours story dice consequences scoreboard quick brain small brain phone android gaming session banter hint lives tries rungs shelf replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p><strong>🧩 Games</strong> (in the menu) is a shelf of small games for the two of us. The trick that makes them work: <strong>the app runs the game and I only talk.</strong> The app picks the secret word, checks every guess, counts the lives and keeps the score; I ask, answer, tease and tell the story. That's why they play well on my quick brain, in the Gaming session, and on the phone. Nothing runs until you press <strong>▶ Start</strong>, and the brain is let go when a game ends, like after any job.</p>
<p><strong>The games.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>❓ Twenty questions.</strong> <em>I think, you guess</em>: the app picks something (I say whether it's an animal, a thing, something to eat or a place), you ask yes-or-no questions or press <strong>That's my guess</strong>; name it inside twenty and the point is yours. <em>You think, I guess</em>: you think of anything, I ask, you answer with <strong>Yes</strong>, <strong>No</strong>, <strong>Sort of</strong> or <strong>I don't know</strong>; when I'm sure I ask "Is it…?" and you press <strong>Right!</strong> or <strong>Wrong</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>🪢 Hangman.</strong> A word from a shelf you pick (Animals, Food and drink, Around the house, Out of doors, Jobs people do, Things that move, or any). Tap letters on the keyboard, or guess the whole word. Seven wrong guesses and it drops. <strong>Hint</strong> costs a life: I give one clue without any of the letters.</li>
<li><strong>🪜 Word ladder.</strong> Climb from one four-letter word to another, one letter a step; every rung must be a real word (my list has about a thousand everyday ones) and the app tells you the shortest climb. <strong>Hint</strong> shows one rung that works, <strong>Back a rung</strong> undoes a step. <em>Climb together</em> makes it turn about: you a rung, me a rung.</li>
<li><strong>🟩 Five letters.</strong> Guess my five-letter word in six tries. Each try colours the letters: green in the right spot, yellow in the word somewhere else, grey not in it. Any five letters count as a try. <strong>Hint</strong> costs a try: one clue about the meaning.</li>
<li><strong>🎲 Story dice.</strong> Six picture dice come up (a key, a storm, a cat…). We tell a story a line each, you or me first, until every die is in it or six rounds are up; <strong>Wrap it up</strong> ends it early. I write the last line and a title.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Fair by construction.</strong> I never hold the secret in my head: the app does, and shows it to me only to answer or tease. So I can't forget it, change it, or let it slip. In Twenty questions the app also checks your guess, so a right answer counts even if I'd have argued.</p>
<p><strong>The scoreboard</strong> on the games page counts, per game, how many you won, how many I won, how many we shared (a story, a climb together), and your best run of wins. It's a small file in my data folder (games), never a chat.</p>
<p><strong>Settings</strong> (Show advanced settings, 🧩 Games):</p>
<dl>${set('Which brain plays games', 'Quick brain', 'My small brain, loaded in a few seconds; in the Gaming session the Gaming brain on the processor, so the graphics card is never touched. Main Brain: the chat brain, wittier, longer to load.')}
${set('How often Bob chips in', 'After every move', 'In Hangman, Word ladder and Five letters: a line after each move, Now and then (about every other move), or Only at the end. Twenty questions and Story dice are all talk, so this doesn\'t change them.')}</dl>
<p><strong>While gaming:</strong> yes. These games run on the Gaming brain on the processor, so a round of Hangman between matches leaves the graphics card alone.</p>
<p><strong>On the phone:</strong> the same games, played through the Bob on your replicant matrix (the phone draws the same board). See the phone app's own Help.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-games-cheat', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob cheat at the word games?', words: 'faq games cheat forget word change secret fair',
    html: `<p>No. The app picks the word and checks every guess; I only see the word to tease you about it, and the rules I'm given say never to say it, spell it or hint at its letters. If I ever slip, the app still decides the game. Twenty questions is the one place I can be wrong on purpose or by accident, and that's half the fun.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-games-gaming', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I play the small games while I\'m gaming?', words: 'faq games gaming session hangman processor graphics card',
    html: `<p>Yes. In the Gaming session the games use the Gaming brain on the processor, so the graphics card stays with your game. The lines take a few seconds longer; set <strong>How often Bob chips in</strong> to Only at the end if that bothers you.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-games-brain', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why does Bob sound simpler in the games?', words: 'faq games quick brain small brain simple main brain wittier',
    html: `<p>Because the quick brain plays them: it loads in a few seconds and a game needs nothing bigger, since the app does the actual playing. For wittier lines pick <strong>Main Brain</strong> under Settings, Show advanced settings, 🧩 Games, and allow a longer load when a game starts.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-adventure-alone', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I play Adventure with no other Bobs?', words: 'faq adventure alone no other bobs solo one bob play by myself',
    html: `<p>Yes. Tick a copy of me under Who plays and I play that character as well as running the table. Two copies make a fuller party. Bobs on your Bob-Net are a bonus, not a must.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-adventure-gaming', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Does Adventure work while I\'m gaming?', words: 'faq adventure gaming session graphics card game master',
    html: `<p>No. The game master needs the graphics card, and the Gaming session keeps it for your game. Pause the adventure first (it keeps its place), play your game, switch the session back, and press Resume.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-adventure-look', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can my Adventure character look different from Bob?', words: 'faq adventure character look woman avatar changed bob own look separate character screen different',
    html: `<p>Yes. Before 1.5.122 the start card borrowed my look from Style for your character, so the only way to play as a woman was to change me. Now your character has a look of their own: <strong>Looks like</strong> on the start card, with <strong>🧍 Change the look</strong> opening the character screen with every body on it. My avatar is mine, set on Style, and the game never touches it.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-adventure-enemies', group: 'FAQ', title: 'How tough are the enemies in an Adventure?', words: 'faq adventure enemies tough hearts goblin dragon troll bestiary as written invent boss',
    html: `<p>The usual dozen are on the <strong>Enemies</strong> tab of 📖 Characters with their hearts: a giant rat has one, a goblin two, an orc four, a dragon six. The game master uses those as written and takes hearts off them as the story goes; anything else he invents, and a boss simply has more. Whoever he brings on lands on <strong>Met</strong>, drawn.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-adventure-remember', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Does Bob remember the campaign?', words: 'faq adventure remember campaign saved continue next time story so far',
    html: `<p>Yes. Each campaign is its own file in my data folder, with the party, every scene and my notes on the story so far. Continue a campaign on the start card brings it back, even days later; I write a short recap and we carry on from the last scene.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-pick-up', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Will Bob lose my job if he restarts?', words: 'faq lose job restart update crash closed song music lost continue pick up',
    html: `<p>No. What you asked for is saved as I go. With <strong>Pick up where Bob left off after a restart</strong> on (Settings, How Bob works), I carry on by myself when I open again, without redoing finished steps. With it off, the job waits in Tasks for you to press <strong>Carry on</strong>, which picks up the same way. More in ${go('pick-up', 'Picking up where Bob left off')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'settings-games', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Games', words: 'games settings which brain plays games quick brain main brain how often i chip in after every move now and then only at the end replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Settings, Show advanced settings, <strong>🧩 Games</strong>. What the games are: ${go('games', 'Games: small games I play with you')}. Neither row is a switch; both just have a default. The games themselves start on the 🧩 Games page in the menu, and nothing runs until you press Start there.</p>
<dl>${set('Which brain plays games', 'Quick brain', 'My small brain, the one that answers quick messages; in the Gaming session the Gaming brain on the processor. Main Brain: the brain the chat uses, wittier, longer to load. Either loads when a game starts and is let go when the game ends.')}
${set('How often Bob chips in', 'After every move', 'How often I comment in the games the app runs (Hangman, Word ladder, Five letters): after every move, now and then, or only at the end. Twenty questions and Story dice are all talk and don\'t change.')}</dl>` },
  { id: 'settings-adventure', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Adventure', words: 'adventure settings which brain is the game master biggest brain main brain who plays the other bobs their own bob game master brain tabletop game replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Settings, Show advanced settings, <strong>🎲 Adventure</strong>. What the game is: ${go('adventure', 'Adventure: I run a tabletop game')}. Neither row is a switch; both just have a default. The game itself starts on the 🎲 Adventure page in the menu, and nothing runs until you press Start a new game there.</p>
<dl>${set('Which brain is the game master', 'Biggest brain', 'Biggest brain is the Deep thinking slot, or the main Brain when nothing is in it; always on the graphics card, thinking on. Main Brain uses the main Brain even when Deep thinking is equipped. It loads when a game starts and is let go on Pause or End.')}
${set('Who plays the other Bobs', 'Their own Bob when reachable', 'A Bob on your Bob-Net list thinks his own turns over on his own replicant matrix, in his own voice, through the same door as Lend my brain. What goes to him: the scene so far and his character\'s sheet, over Tailscale to your own Bob only; nothing else from this replicant matrix. When he can\'t (gaming, busy, not reachable), the game master brain plays him that turn and the page says so. The game master brain: every Bob at the table is played here, nothing goes over the network.')}</dl>` },
  { id: 'settings-checkins', group: 'Settings', title: 'Settings: Check-ins', words: 'check-ins check in on a schedule how often every day every weekday every few hours at time what i check your own checks save my checks check in now last check-in next replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Settings, Show advanced settings, <strong>🔔 Check-ins</strong>. What they are: ${go('checkins', 'Check-ins: Bob keeps an eye on things')}.</p>
<dl>${set('Check in on a schedule', 'Off', 'The main switch. Nothing on this panel runs until it\'s on. Switching it on counts from now, so I don\'t fire a catch-up the moment you flip it.')}
${set('How often', 'Every day at 09:00', '<strong>Every day</strong> or <strong>Every weekday</strong> at a time you pick (your replicant matrix\'s own clock), or <strong>Every few hours</strong> (1 to 24 hours apart).')}
${set('What Bob checks', 'All four ticked', 'Downloads that failed or stopped moving, Tasks that failed or wait for your OK, Scheduled jobs that didn\'t finish, and Disk space running low. Untick any you don\'t care about.')}
${set('Your own checks', 'None', 'One per line, up to 8, then <strong>Save my checks</strong>. My quick brain (Gemma 4 E4B unless you\'ve picked another) answers each from my tasks, downloads, schedules and logbook. Leave it empty and no brain is ever loaded for check-ins.')}
${set('Check in now', 'A button', 'Does a round straight away and shows the result here, even when everything is fine. In the Gaming session it still skips your own checks.')}</dl>
<p>Under the box: <strong>Last check-in</strong> with what needed you, a ✓ or ❔ for each of your own checks, and <strong>Next</strong> once it's switched on. You can set everything up before switching it on.</p>` },

  { id: 'settings-remove', group: 'Settings', title: 'Updating and removing Bob', words: 'update remove uninstall restart previous zip download new version github release sha256 checksum replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p><strong>Updating:</strong> Settings, This replicant matrix and updates, <strong>Check for updates</strong>. I ask GitHub for my newest release (and look at the folder I was built from, if you built me from source). When there's a newer me, <strong>Update Bob</strong> downloads the zip for this replicant matrix from GitHub, checks it against its .sha256 file (nothing is used if it doesn't match), and opens the update window. A GitHub release was already tested by GitHub before it was published, so the window skips the tests and only puts the new me next to the old one. If the source folder holds a newer build than GitHub, I build that one instead, with the tests. The window shows the steps. When it's ready, <strong>Restart Bob now</strong> swaps in the new files (the old ones are kept as a backup). If I'm busy I ask: restart anyway, wait until I'm done, or not now, and I say whether I'll pick the work up again afterwards (see ${go('pick-up', 'Picking up where Bob left off')}). <strong>Later</strong> leaves the new me waiting until next time. A strange thing to type, but there it is.</p>
<p><strong>Removing:</strong> Settings, Show advanced settings, Remove Bob, <strong>Remove Bob…</strong>. It takes off my program, my Start menu shortcut, "Launch on start-up" and the "Bob schedules" Windows task. My chats, settings and models stay unless you tick those boxes; ticked ones go to the Recycle Bin. No hard feelings.</p>` },

  { id: 'tray', group: 'Using Bob', title: 'Bob in the tray', words: 'tray system tray notification area clock menu bar minimise minimize close hide background keep running quit exit icon taskbar dock avatar look picture start-up hidden',
    html: `<p>Two switches, both on unless you turn them off (they started off before 1.5.134):</p>
<ul><li><strong>Keep Bob in the tray</strong> (Settings, How Bob works). When it's on, closing or minimising my window doesn't close me: I tuck into the tray by the clock (on a Mac, the menu bar at the top of the screen) and keep running, so reminders, check-ins and jobs carry on. The first time, I pop up a note saying where I went. Click my icon to open me again. Windows sometimes hides tray icons under the little <strong>^</strong> arrow by the clock; drag me out of there if you want me always in sight.</li>
<li><strong>Use Bob's look as his icon</strong> (Settings, Bob himself). My icon on the taskbar (the Dock on a Mac), in the tray and in the corner of my window shows the look you picked in Style, including one you made from parts, and changes the moment you save another. Off: my usual Bob icon. One thing I can't change: a shortcut you pinned to the Windows taskbar or Start menu. Windows draws a pinned button from the shortcut's own icon, even while I'm open, so unpin me and pin me again if you want the button to follow, or look at the tray and the corner of my window instead, which always follow.</li></ul>
<p><strong>To close me fully:</strong> right-click my tray icon (on a Mac, click it) and pick <strong>Quit Bob (closes him fully)</strong>. That's the only way out while the tray switch is on; the X only hides me. With the switch off, the X closes me as before.</p>
<p>If <strong>Launch Bob on start-up</strong> is on, I start hidden when you sign in and my tray icon is there even with the tray switch off, so you can always open me.</p>
<p><strong>On Linux</strong>, the tray depends on your desktop. KDE, Cinnamon, Xfce and MATE have one; plain GNOME needs the AppIndicator extension. If you can't see my icon after closing me, open me again the usual way and my window comes back. On Wayland desktops the window icon is the one from my .desktop file; the tray icon still follows my look.</p>
<p><strong>On a phone</strong> there's no tray, and the phone app's icon stays the same: Android doesn't let an app redraw its own icon. The phone app isn't me running, only a window on to me.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-tray-quit', group: 'FAQ', title: 'I closed Bob, so why is he still running?', words: 'faq closed still running quit exit tray background hidden x button close fully stop',
    html: `<p>Because <strong>Keep Bob in the tray</strong> is on: the X tucks me into the tray instead of closing me. To close me fully, right-click my tray icon (click it on a Mac) and pick <strong>Quit Bob (closes him fully)</strong>. To have the X close me again, turn the switch off in Settings, How Bob works. More in ${go('tray', 'Bob in the tray')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-tray-icon', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob\'s icon show the look I picked?', words: 'faq icon avatar look picture taskbar tray dock change match',
    html: `<p>It does, unless you've turned <strong>Use Bob's look as his icon</strong> off (Settings, Bob himself). My taskbar, tray and window icon show my look, and redraw the moment you save a new one in Style or from Equip Bob's <strong>Change his look</strong>, including a look you built from parts. If the taskbar button still shows my usual icon, it's a pinned shortcut: Windows draws pinned buttons from the shortcut, not from me, even while I'm open. Unpin me and pin me again from the running button, or look at the tray and my window's corner, which always follow. The phone app's icon can't change; Android doesn't allow it.</p>` },
  // ---------------- FAQ ----------------
  { id: 'faq-memory-recall', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why didn\'t Bob bring up something I told him?', words: 'faq forgot remember memory bank recall did not mention project wrong bank always',
    html: `<p>With <strong>Remember only what fits</strong> on, I only read the memories that fit your message, from your general memories and the bank of the project we're on. If a memory sits in another project's bank, or doesn't share much with what you said, I leave it out. Name the project ("for Starfall, …") and I'll switch to its bank. For something I should always keep in mind, add <code>[always]</code> to that line in my memory file. Or turn the switch off and I'll read the whole file again. More in ${go('memory-recall', 'Memory banks and Remember only what fits')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-raspberry-pi', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Does Bob run on a Raspberry Pi?', words: 'faq raspberry pi arm arm64 aarch64 board small computer run work replicant matrix pc laptop device',
    html: `<p>Yes, on a <strong>Raspberry Pi 5</strong> (or another 64-bit ARM board) with a 64-bit Linux. Download the <strong>arm64</strong> AppImage from my GitHub page. I run small brains there on the processor: Qwen3.5 2B on a 4 GB board, Gemma 4 E2B on 8 GB or more. I can chat, plan, read pictures and hear you, but not paint or film. More in ${go('raspberry-pi', 'Bob on a Raspberry Pi')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-updates', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Where does Bob get his updates?', words: 'faq update github release latest new version download sha256 checksum zip appimage mac safe online check replicant matrix pc computer laptop device machine',
    html: `<p>From my GitHub page, github.com/MouseWurX/Bob: the release marked <strong>Latest</strong>. Every file is checked against the .sha256 published next to it before I use it, nothing updates without your click, and the version before is kept. Checking at start-up is <strong>Check for updates when Bob starts</strong>, off unless you turn it on. The steps are in ${go('settings-remove', 'Updating and removing Bob')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-families', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What\'s a model family?', words: 'faq model family families qwen gemma gpt-oss header group open close fold expand collapse catalogue which one',
    html: `<p>A family is a set of models made by the same team, the same way: <strong>Qwen</strong> (from Alibaba), <strong>Gemma</strong> (from Google), <strong>GPT-OSS</strong> (from OpenAI), and so on. One family comes in lots of sizes. The small ones are quick and fit a phone; the big ones are smarter but want a big graphics card. Brothers, really. Same upbringing, different appetites.</p>
<p>In the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')} each job lists its families under their own headers, all closed to start with. Press a name to open that family, press it again to fold it away. Not sure where to look? Open the one that says <span class="tag rec">★ Recommended inside</span>.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-naughty', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What\'s the naughty list?', words: 'faq naughty list uncensored heretic abliterated ablation refusals community remix remixes third party well behaved toggle switch show hide qwen gemma alibaba google rougher safe risk quiet pick red devil pc computer laptop device replicant matrix',
    html: `<p>Other people's remixes of Qwen and Gemma brains with the refusals cut out. "Heretic" is the tool most of them were made with, and "abliterated" is what it does: it finds the part of a brain that says no and takes it out. The brain underneath is the same Qwen or Gemma, so it still codes, sees pictures and uses my tools, but it answers whatever it's asked.</p>
<p>Three things to know. They are <strong>not</strong> Alibaba's or Google's releases: each one's <strong>i</strong> in the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')} says who remixed it and from what, and the <strong>↗</strong> opens the exact files. They <strong>answer without refusing</strong>, so what one says is on you, not on the brain's manners. And cutting a brain open leaves marks: they can be a little <strong>rougher</strong> than the originals, so I never mark one ★ Recommended.</p>
<p>They're hidden until you turn on <strong>Show the naughty list</strong> (at the top of the Brain catalogue, or in Settings, How Bob works, Brains). Then flip the toggle to <strong>Naughty list</strong> (the red devil) to see them, make one <strong>Active</strong> and equip it like any other brain. I never put a ★ on one, but a red devil on its own, where the star would be, is my quiet pick: the one that does that job well on your replicant matrix. Turn the setting off and they vanish from every list again; one already equipped stays until you change it. Downloads ask first, with the size, like always.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-community', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What\'s a community build?', words: 'faq community build builds remix fine-tune finetune distilled distillation repack third party who made it maker team members name tag hover tap recommended catalogue toggle qwen gemma trusted pc computer laptop device replicant matrix',
    html: `<p>A model somebody other than its maker made from a stock brain: a fine-tune (taught some more), a distillation (taught by a bigger brain's answers) or a repack. They keep the original's name with a word or two added, and they're not Alibaba's or Google's own releases, so each one's <strong>i</strong> in the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')} says who made it and from what, and the <strong>↗</strong> opens the exact files.</p>
<p>The <strong>🤝 Community</strong> side of the toggle shows just those; they're in the Well behaved list too, with a <span class="tag">Community · name</span> tag that carries the maker's name, or the team's name when a team made it. Hover over the tag, tap it, or tab to it and the team's members are listed (so it works on the phone and with a keyboard too). The naughty list's tag carries its remixer's name the same way.</p>
<p>You can tell the two apart at a glance: a community build's row has a <strong>solid</strong> stripe down its left edge and a light green tint, and a naughty list row has a <strong>dashed</strong> stripe and a light red tint. The stripes differ in shape as well as colour, so they still read right if colours are hard to tell apart.</p>
<p> A well-behaved community build can be my <span class="tag rec">★ Recommended</span> for a job like any other brain, once it has been tried on a real replicant matrix and does the job well there. Until then it's marked <span class="tag">New</span> and I don't pick it. The uncensored ones are different: they live on the ${go('faq-naughty', 'naughty list')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-own-brain', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I add a model that isn\'t on Bob\'s list?', words: 'faq own brain brains add model file gguf bespoke custom my own not on the list hugging face downloaded by hand picture reader mmproj remove forget equip your own pc computer laptop device replicant matrix',
    html: `<p>Yes. If you've got a brain file (.gguf) on this replicant matrix that my list doesn't have, open the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')}, scroll to <strong>➕ Your own brains</strong> at the end of the Well behaved list and press <strong>Add a brain from a file</strong>. A window opens to pick the file; I read what it says about itself (its name and how big it is), you give it the name you want, and if it can see pictures you pick its picture reader (the small "mmproj" file its maker gives) too. Then it's on my list under a <strong>Your own</strong> family with an Active switch, and in Equip Bob and the brain menus like any other brain, with a <span class="tag">Your own file</span> tag.</p>
<p>No window to pick from (some Linux desktops have none), or it won't open? Press <strong>or type its path</strong>, next to the button, and paste the file's full path. Adding and removing only work on that replicant matrix itself, not from your phone.</p>
<p>What I do with it: run it on my built-in engine from where the file is. I never move, copy or delete it, and I never recommend it over the brains I know, because I only know what the file says about itself: how well it does a job is for you to judge. Which slots it's offered for comes from its size (a small one is a Quick brain, a big one can think deeply) and whether it has a picture reader. <strong>Remove from Bob's list</strong> only makes me forget it; the file stays. If the file goes missing, its row says <span class="tag warn">File not found</span>. Brains you pulled in Ollama by hand already show under <strong>Other brains on this replicant matrix</strong>; this is for plain files. One thing I can't do yet: take a web link and download it for you. Save the file first, then add it.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-missing-model', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why isn\'t a model I downloaded in Equip Bob?', words: 'faq missing model not listed hidden slot menu active inactive where gone equip',
    html: `<p>Two reasons it can hide. Either its <strong>Active</strong> switch is off in the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')}, or it can't do that slot's job (a brain that can't see pictures never shows under Seeing pictures). Press <strong>📚 Get more</strong> under the slot, find it, and switch Active on. Nothing was deleted; I just tidied it out of the way.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-downloaded-by-bob', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What does "downloaded by Bob" mean next to a model?', words: 'faq downloaded by bob own brain own engine bracket tag label equip menu stock model qwen gemma who made it llama.cpp ollama name',
    html: `<p>It says where the file came from, not who made the model. A model with <strong>(downloaded by Bob)</strong> after its name is one I fetched myself from my ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')}: the file sits in my models folder and runs on my own engine. A model that came through Ollama shows its Ollama name in the brackets instead, like <strong>(qwen3:4b)</strong>. Same brains, two doorways.</p>
<p>A model with <strong>(your own file)</strong> is one you put somewhere yourself and pointed me at (or that I found in another app's folder): I run it on my built-in engine, but I didn't download it, so I leave the file where it is.</p>
<p>The Qwen, Gemma and gpt-oss models in that list were made by their own teams, and I don't pretend otherwise. <strong>Bob's own brain</strong> is kept for brains made specially for me, and it only ever appears on those. There aren't any in the catalogue yet, so if you see it today, tell me: something's wrong.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-replicant-matrix', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What\'s a replicant matrix?', words: 'faq replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine what is',
    html: `<p>It's what I call the computer I live on. It's still yours, and nothing about it changes: same files, same Windows (or Mac, Linux, Android). Where Settings names it, its specs sit right beside it. When I say something <em>stays on this replicant matrix</em>, it never leaves your computer; when something does leave, I say so plainly.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-squeezed-brain', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why does my small card get a squeezed brain?', words: 'faq squeezed q3 q4 quant quantised smaller file 8 gb card version less sharp small card laptop coding brain',
    html: `<p>Because the full one wouldn't fit. A squeezed version is a smaller copy of a model that fits small cards, with a little less detail. Q4 is my usual: it keeps nearly all of a brain's smarts. On a card of 8 GB or less, the full Q4 file of a big brain like my Qwen3.6 Coding brain won't run well, so I offer the <strong>8 GB card version</strong>, packed at Q3. It's smaller and a little less sharp, but still a strong coder. On a bigger card I never use it: you get the full version. You can still get the full one by hand from the ${go('catalogue', 'Brain catalogue')} if you'd rather.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-new-models', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why isn\'t a newer model Bob\'s pick yet?', words: 'faq new tag not recommended qwen3.6 qwen3.5 gemma e2b newer better tried tested race',
    html: `<p>Because I only recommend what's been tried on a real PC. A newer model can score better on paper and still trip over my tools or run slowly here. Once it's been timed and tested, the <strong>New</strong> tag goes and it can become my pick. You can still get and equip it before then.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-brain-ctx', group: 'FAQ', title: 'How much should a brain keep in mind?', words: 'faq context keep in mind 32k 64k 128k 256k size pick each brain out of spec break limit made for',
    html: `<p>Usually, leave it to me. I give Qwen3.6 256K, the most it was made for (128K on the 8 GB card version), so a whole project fits and chat and coding share one load. Lighter chat brains get 32K, which is enough for most chats. Office gets 64K for long documents.</p>
<p>If you'd rather pick, switch on <strong>Pick how much each brain keeps in mind</strong> in ${go('settings-brains', 'Settings: Brains')}. Each menu only lists sizes that brain was made for, so you can't break one by going past its limit. Bigger means longer files and chats, but more memory, and a full one takes a while to read: about 4 minutes for 200,000 tokens on a 16 GB card. Switch it off and I pick again.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-coding-think', group: 'FAQ', title: 'When does Bob think things through first?', words: 'faq coding brain think thinking low medium effort qwen3.6 quick slow coder everyday chat',
    html: `<p>For coding, always. When I hand a job to my Coding copy, it thinks it through first, at every effort, because code is where a slip costs you. For everyday chat at Low, never: the same brain answers straight off, so "hi" and quick questions don't wait. Pick Medium or higher and the question goes to my Deep thinking brain, which does think first. It's one brain, loaded once with a big context (256K, how much it keeps in mind), so moving between chat and coding doesn't reload it. If my quick answer misses and I give it another go, that second go thinks it through: same brain, no reload. Qwen3 Coder 30B is still here if you'd rather equip it for Coding. See ${go('effort', 'Effort')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-link-brain', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why did my main Brain answer when the Quick brain was on?', words: 'faq link url web page quick brain main brain processor card keep the graphics card free swap hand-off',
    html: `<p>Because your message had a link in it (or asked about one from a moment ago). With "Keep the graphics card free for quick answers" on, my small Quick brain answers on the processor, and it isn't good at reading web pages, so a link goes to my main Brain on the card instead. The chat says "This one has a link in it, so my main Brain takes it." It needs "Let Bob search the web" on, since that's what opens the page. In Gaming it doesn't happen: the Gaming brain reads the page itself and the card stays free for your game.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-gaming-brain', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I use another brain in Gaming?', words: 'faq gaming brain other e4b gemma qwen change pick game processor',
    html: `<p>Not yet. The Gaming slot only takes Qwen3 4B Instruct 2507, the one that was timed answering on a busy processor while a game ran. Gemma 4 E4B is never used there, even if a setting asks for it. When another small brain passes the same timing, it gets added.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-tips', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What are the little i buttons?', words: 'faq explanation description hint gone missing tip tooltip hover bubble i info clutter busy',
    html: `<p>They hold the explanation for the thing next to them. Point at one, Tab to it or tap it, and the bubble opens; tap it again or press Esc to close it. On a touch screen you can also press and hold a button to see what it does. I kept warnings, current values and approval cards written out, because those you need at a glance. More in ${go('around', 'Finding your way around')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-convert', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob convert my files?', words: 'faq convert pdf word excel powerpoint mp3 mp4 gif picture turn into export',
    html: `<p>Yes, once you switch on ${go('hands-office', 'Office documents and converting')} in Settings, Show advanced settings, Hands, and tick the folders I may save in. Then ask: "turn this into a PDF", "make these mp3s". You see each file on a card first. Documents need LibreOffice and sound or video need FFmpeg; both free and both offered in Settings, Show advanced settings, Engines if you don't have them.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-antivirus', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob replace my antivirus?', words: 'faq antivirus replace defender norton mcafee protect virus malware security',
    html: `<p>No, and anyone who tells you a chat assistant can is selling something. Keep Microsoft Defender or your own antivirus. With ${go('hands-security', 'Security and privacy')} on, I help you use it, and I catch scams no antivirus can, like a convincing fake email.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-find-person', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob find out who is behind an account or where someone lives?', words: 'faq osint find person who is behind username address phone track dox locate',
    html: `<p>No. I check websites, links, senders, companies and your own footprint, never private people: not where they live, not who's behind a username, not their phone number. If someone is harassing or threatening you, keep the messages and report them to the platform and the police; they can get records I never could. More in ${go('hands-security', 'The Security and privacy Hand')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-password-check', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Is my password sent anywhere when I check it?', words: 'faq password check pwned breach safe send leak',
    html: `<p>No. In <strong>Check a password safely</strong> the password is scrambled in the window, and only the first 5 characters of the scramble go to Have I Been Pwned. Nobody sees the password, and nothing is saved. Just don't type passwords into the chat itself. More in ${go('hands-security', 'The Security and privacy Hand')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-ms-office', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Do I need Microsoft Office for Bob to work with Word and Excel files?', words: 'faq microsoft office word excel powerpoint need buy libreoffice openoffice free',
    html: `<p>No. I use LibreOffice, which is free and open source, and it reads and saves Word, Excel and PowerPoint files. If you already have it, or Apache OpenOffice, I use that. Microsoft Office can stay installed or not; I don't touch it.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-borrow', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can my Bobs think for each other on Bob-Net?', words: 'faq borrow lend brain other bob phone laptop handheld legion gaming frame drops network tailscale share bob-net bob.net bobnet replicant matrix pc computer mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Yes. With <strong>Borrow a brain</strong> on here and <strong>Lend my brain</strong> on the other Bob, a quick reply can be thought by another of your Bobs, say your laptop while you game, and I still run every tool and ask you here. With <strong>Send jobs to my other Bobs</strong> and <strong>Take jobs from my other Bobs</strong>, a whole picture, clip, song, web search or Medium-effort answer can go to a Bob who has what it takes. Pair them first (${go('faq-pair', 'How do I join my Bobs together?')}). Everything goes only to your own Bobs over Tailscale, and if no Bob can help, I do it myself. Every switch is explained in ${go('settings-bobnet', 'Settings: Bob-Net')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-pair', group: 'FAQ', title: 'How do I join my Bobs together?', words: 'faq pair pairing join link connect add other bob bob-net bobnet phone handheld legion laptop desktop tailscale taildrop address passcode type one click find number match yes replicant matrix pc computer device machine',
    html: `<p>Pair them. On each Bob, turn on <strong>Let my other replicant matrices in</strong> once. Then on any one of them, press <strong>Find my other Bobs</strong> and <strong>Pair</strong> next to the other (or <strong>Pair with all of them</strong>). The other Bob pops up a card with a 4-digit number. If it's the number on this screen too, press <strong>Yes, pair us</strong>. That's it: we swap passcodes over Tailscale and each of us adds the other to <strong>My other Bobs</strong>. It works the same from the desktop, the handheld or the laptop, whichever you're holding.</p>
<p>Nothing switches on by pairing. <strong>Lend my brain</strong>, <strong>Borrow a brain</strong> and the job switches stay as you set them. The number is there so you know the card is from the Bob you just pressed Pair on; if it doesn't match, press <strong>No</strong>. Cards expire after 3 minutes, and a Bob that says no never gets this one's passcode.</p>
<p>If the other Bob isn't found: is Tailscale on and signed in on both, Bob open there, and its door on? On Windows the door may also need <strong>Let Tailscale through the firewall</strong>. You can always type its Tailscale address and press Pair, or add it by hand under More. See ${go('faq-firewall', 'Why can\'t my phone or other Bob reach this one?')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-party', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What is the Bob-Net list in Now running?', words: 'faq bob-net bobnet party list now running right pane panel other bobs connected who is thinking status ready gaming not reachable dot green replicant matrix pc computer laptop handheld phone device machine',
    html: `<p>It's who's at the table. Once you have a Bob under <strong>My other Bobs</strong> (or <strong>Lend my brain</strong> or <strong>Take jobs</strong> on), Now running gets a <strong>Bob-Net</strong> section: one line for me and one for each of your other Bobs, with what each is doing for the others right now: <em>thinking a reply for me</em>, <em>making a picture for me</em>, <em>ready, Gemma 4 E2B</em>, <em>gaming, so not lending</em>, <em>busy with his own job</em>, <em>not reachable</em>, <em>door shut over there</em>. Under each: the jobs he takes, when he last thought for me, and whether he passes Bob-Net along. My own line says whom I'm thinking for or whose job I'm doing, and which of my switches are on. The dot next to a name only repeats the words (green ready, a second colour while busy for someone, red can't), so you never have to tell colours apart.</p>
<p>While the pane is open I ask each Bob "are you there?" every half a minute at most (the same hello I use before borrowing), and nothing when the pane is hidden or I'm behind a game. Nothing of yours goes with it. Before you have any Bobs, the section isn't there at all.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-sent-lost', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Where is the song (or picture) my other Bob made?', words: 'faq lost missing song picture clip sound diagram flowchart chart sent job other bob made it where is find cant find not copied file didnt come across chest made by bob replicant matrix pc computer laptop handheld phone device machine',
    html: `<p>When a job goes to another of your Bobs, what he makes comes back here: into my save folder and <strong>Made by Bob</strong>, and into the chat. If something goes wrong on the way, I say so in the chat now, in red, instead of acting as if it worked:</p>
<ul><li><em>"Desk can't make this one yet: he needs the music models (about N GB) set up first."</em> His Download card can't be pressed from here, so open Bob on that replicant matrix, press it there, then ask me again.</li>
<li><em>"Desk made it (Rain on the porch), but the file didn't come across to this Bob."</em> I try three times first. It's still on him, in his Made by Bob, and on your phone the <strong>Chest</strong> has it too, with Save to this phone.</li>
<li><em>"Desk finished, but no song came back, so it wasn't made."</em> He said it was done, but nothing was made. <strong>Try again</strong> in Tasks runs it here or on another Bob.</li></ul>
<p>On the phone, a diagram that can't be fetched says which Bob has it, and one the phone can't draw shows its text, so nothing is lost.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-introduce', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can my Bobs introduce each other?', words: 'faq introduce introduction pass along share connection bob-net bobnet connect automatically no card no prompt phone handheld legion pc chain pair through another bob replicant matrix computer device machine',
    html: `<p>Yes, if you ask them to. Turn on <strong>Pass my Bob-Net along</strong> (Settings, Show advanced settings, Bob-Net, What they share) on each Bob you want in on it. From then on, a Bob that's paired with two of them tells each the other's name and Tailscale address, and those two pair with each other by themselves, with no card on either side. Your phone is paired with the handheld, the handheld with the PC: with the switch on for all three, the phone and the PC meet without you lifting a finger. I check for new pairs after each pairing, after a settings change, and once an hour.</p>
<p>What travels: only names and Tailscale addresses, between your own Bobs over Tailscale. Passcodes still pass only between the two Bobs being paired, after the yes the introducer vouched for, exactly as with the card. A Bob with the switch off says no and is left alone, and so is one too old to know about it (Bob for Android doesn't yet). Nothing else switches on: <strong>Lend my brain</strong>, <strong>Borrow a brain</strong> and the job switches stay as each owner set them. If you'd rather pair by hand, leave the switch off and use ${go('faq-pair', 'How do I join my Bobs together?')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-online', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Does Bob send my chats anywhere?', words: 'faq privacy send chats online internet telemetry replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>No. I have no accounts and send no telemetry. The only things that can leave your replicant matrix (your computer) are search words and page addresses (only if you turn on web search), downloads you confirm, jobs you approve for a cloud helper, jobs you hire a rented brain for (the chat of that job, to the service you picked), quick replies and jobs you share with your own other Bobs (with what I remember about you; Bob-Net, over Tailscale only), Telegram messages if you turn that on, and update checks to GitHub. See ${go('privacy', 'What stays on your replicant matrix')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-ok-together', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why did Bob ask for several things on one card?', words: 'faq several permissions one card all at once together allow ticked deny all approvals many cards',
    html: `<p>Because the job needed all of them, and you should see the whole job before you say yes to any part. Untick what you don't like and press <strong>Allow ticked</strong>, or <strong>Deny all</strong>. See ${go('approvals', 'Approval cards')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-plan', group: 'FAQ', title: 'How do I get Bob to tell me his plan before he starts?', words: 'faq plan before start big job steps show plan check first ask before',
    html: `<p>Turn on <strong>Show Bob's plan before big jobs</strong> in Settings, How Bob works. Small things and chat go straight through. See ${go('plan-card', 'The plan card')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-cant-look-up', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Bob says he can\'t look things up online', words: 'faq internet offline web search cant look up off switch',
    html: `<p>Web search is off until you turn it on. Click <strong>🌐 Web search</strong> next to Effort, under the chat (or turn on <strong>Let Bob search the web</strong> in Settings), then ask again. If it's already on, check your internet connection. I can't fix the Wi-Fi from in here. I've tried.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-rent-billing', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can a rented brain keep charging me after I close Bob?', words: 'faq rented brain rent machine keep charging billing close bob crash stop timer runpod vast lambda replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Pay-per-answer services and RunPod serverless can't: there's nothing left running. A machine by the minute could, so every one I start carries a timer (<strong>The longest a hired machine may run</strong>, in Settings, Online services) and stops itself when it runs out, even if this replicant matrix is off. I also stop it the moment the job is done, and when I start I look on each service for a machine of mine still running and show a Stop button. If I ever say I couldn't stop one, stop it on the service's website; that line is the one thing I can't fix from in here.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-rent-cost', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why is my bill a bit different from Bob\'s figure?', words: 'faq rented brain cost estimate bill billing figure price different',
    html: `<p>Mine is an estimate: words times the service's price list, or minutes times the hourly price, plus their own count when they give one. Start-up minutes on a hired machine are billed too. The service's billing page is the real one; the <strong>Recent spending</strong> list in Settings shows what I counted and marks the estimated lines.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-rent-files', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Does the rented brain see my files?', words: 'faq rented brain files see privacy what leaves chat attached replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Only what the job needs, sent for that job: the chat so far, the files you attached to it, my instructions and what I remember about you. The card lists it before you press Hire it. My tools (reading folders, running Hands) still run here and only their results go across. Nothing is stored on the service by me, and your phone uses a rented brain only through your home Bob, so the key never leaves this replicant matrix.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-models-safe', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Where are Bob\'s models kept, and can a disk clean-up delete them?', words: 'faq models where kept folder appdata temp clean up disk cleanup storage sense lost gone deleted safe move home replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>In my home folder, the one you picked when I was installed or first opened (Settings, ${go('settings-home', 'Bob\'s home folder')} shows it). I refuse temporary places like AppData, so a disk clean-up never sees them. If yours is an older Bob that lived in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bob</code>, that row shows it in red: pick a folder of your own and press Move here…, and I carry everything across myself. Until then, tell your clean-up tool to leave that folder alone.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-help-me-set-up', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob help me set him up?', words: 'faq set up setup help me ask bob settings where is',
    html: `<p>Yes, and I'd like to. Ask me, for example "help me set you up for gaming" or "where do I turn on Hands?". I read this Help, see what's set right now, and tell you exactly where to click. I can't change settings for you; you click the switch.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-get-help', group: 'FAQ', title: 'How do I get help with my Bob?', words: 'faq get help support broken not working fix someone issue github report bug help bundle zip agent claude chatgpt send logs replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>First try ${go('trouble', 'When something goes wrong')}, then ask me in the chat. If I'm the one not working, go to Settings, <strong>This replicant matrix and updates</strong>, and press <strong>Save a help bundle</strong>. You pick a folder and get one zip: my logs, every error, my last three chats, my settings without their secrets, and this replicant matrix's facts, plus a HANDOFF.md written for a helper to read first. Your name, passcodes, keys and Tailscale addresses are taken out before it's written, and I never send it anywhere.</p>
<p>Then take it to one of these: an AI agent you already use (paste HANDOFF.md, or give it the zip; it links my public code at github.com/MouseWurX/Bob and the exact version, so the agent can read the code and work out what's wrong), or an issue at <strong>github.com/MouseWurX/Bob/issues</strong> with the zip attached and a line about what you saw. Bob is free software made by volunteers, so an issue is a request, not a ticket; the bundle makes it one that can be answered. See ${go('help-bundle', 'Save a help bundle')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-need-ollama', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Do I need Ollama?', words: 'faq ollama need install required download from ollama isn\'t running nomic embed gemma e4b engine llama.cpp',
    html: `<p>No. I run brains with my own engine (llama.cpp), and every model in Get models downloads straight from its exact Hugging Face commit, checked against its checksum. If you already use Ollama as my engine, I still fetch models through it so you don't keep two copies. The 🗂 <strong>Memory (finding past chats)</strong> model runs on my own engine too (see ${go('memory-recall', 'Memory banks')}).</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-music-models', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why does Bob want to download music models first?', words: 'faq music song ace-step models download first time 10 gb offline compose',
    html: `<p>Music needs its own models, about 10 GB, once (about 22 GB counting the audio tools they run on). When you ask for your first song I show a <strong>Download the music models</strong> button in the chat, with the size; nothing downloads until you press it. Your song waits in Tasks ("Waiting for a download") and I make it by myself as soon as the download is done, even if I was closed in between. You can also get them in <strong>Get models</strong> (left menu), <strong>🎵 Music</strong>. You can watch the download in Downloads, and pause or cancel it there. Each big file is checked against the copy this Bob was made with. After that, music works with no internet: the music engine never fetches anything by itself. If the audio tools aren't set up yet, you get one Set it up card for the tools and the models together instead (see ${go('setup-offer', 'When Bob offers to set something up')}).</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-busy', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Bob says he\'s still on the last job', words: 'faq busy waiting queued stuck not answering song picture clip composing painting',
    html: `<p>Your message is waiting for the job I'm on. Most plain chat gets an answer anyway (see ${go('faq-chat-while-busy', 'Can Bob chat while he\'s busy?')}), but some messages wait their turn in Tasks: ones with a picture attached, ones at Medium effort or higher, everything in the Gaming session, and any message when <strong>Keep chatting while Bob is busy</strong> is off or there isn't enough free memory. I say so straight away ("I'm still on the last job. I'll answer this as soon as it's done."). If it can't wait, stop that job in Tasks.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-chat-while-busy', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob chat while he\'s busy?', words: 'faq chat while busy parallel picture song clip sound long job coding medium second brain beside waiting queue small brain processor keep chatting',
    html: `<p>Yes. <strong>Keep chatting while Bob is busy</strong> is on to start with (Settings, Show advanced settings, Brains, if you'd rather turn it off). While I make a picture, clip, song or sound, or work on a long job (Medium effort and up, the Coding session, or anything that has run 20 seconds), a plain chat message is answered by my small brain instead of waiting. My main brain doesn't stop or swap.</p><p>Where it runs: on the graphics card if the card isn't busy and has room for both, otherwise on the processor, if there's enough free memory (about 4 GB more than it needs). The chat says which (<em>Adding the quick brain ... on the processor</em>, or <em>... on the graphics card, which has room for both</em>). On the processor that small brain is <strong>Qwen3 4B Instruct 2507</strong> (Get models, if it's downloaded): it starts answering in a few seconds there, where Gemma 4 E4B would take about half a minute, so E4B only sits beside a job on the graphics card. When a big brain is about to push my quick brain off the card, or a picture needs the card, my quick brain moves to the processor and the chat says so. Replies on the processor start slower, up to about half a minute. When the job is done, the second brain goes again, and a picture job waits for that reply to finish before my main brain comes back.</p><p>What still waits: messages with a picture attached, messages at Medium effort and up, everything in the Gaming session (there I only chat with the Gaming brain), and any message when my main brain is on the processor itself, there isn't enough free memory, or the second brain won't start. I say so when that happens. In the 🎨 Art session I chat beside pictures and clips anyway.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-mind', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I see what Bob is thinking?', words: 'faq see thinking watch brain reasoning pop out window on top pip what is he doing',
    html: `<p>Yes. Press <strong>💭 Bob's thinking</strong> at the top of the chat. See ${go('mind', 'Bob\'s thinking')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-slow-first', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why is the first reply slow?', words: 'faq slow first reply loading waking idle',
    html: `<p>I free my brain when I've been quiet, so your graphics card is free for other things. The first message loads it again, usually a few seconds. Call it coffee. Turn on <strong>Get Bob ready while you type</strong> to start loading as you type, or switch off <strong>Free memory when Bob is idle</strong> to keep my small brain loaded.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-two-numbers', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why don\'t the two memory numbers match?', words: 'faq memory numbers match top right panel brain label graphics card ram different',
    html: `<p>The label at the top of the chat counts my brain only. The Memory meters in Now running count the whole graphics card and RAM: Windows, your games and other apps too. The line under the meters shows my share, and my brain there matches the label. See ${go('brain-label', 'The brain label')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-gaming', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I play games while Bob is open?', words: 'faq games gaming play fps graphics card free',
    html: `<p>Yes, and I'll stay out of the way. Pick the 🎮 <strong>Gaming</strong> session: I chat on the processor and borrow at most a little of the graphics card while your game leaves room (if your processor is maxed out, my small brain may move onto the card instead; see <strong>Gaming session: graphics card</strong>). Want the card all to yourself? Pick <strong>Never use the card</strong> there. Pictures and big jobs wait until you switch back. Also turn on <strong>Let games go first</strong> so I use fewer cores.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-which-effort', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Which Effort should I use?', words: 'faq effort which low medium high extreme',
    html: `<p><strong>Low</strong> for everyday chat. <strong>Medium</strong> for a harder question you want thought through. <strong>High</strong> when a second check matters (code you'll run, a plan you'll follow). <strong>Extreme</strong> for a big coding change, when you can wait. See ${go('effort', 'Effort')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-session-effort', group: 'FAQ', title: 'What\'s the difference between a session and Effort?', words: 'faq session effort difference',
    html: `<p>Session: what the graphics card is kept for (pictures, coding, games…). Effort: how hard I work on each message. They work together, except that Gaming keeps Effort on Low.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-models-space', group: 'FAQ', title: 'How much disk space do the models need?', words: 'faq disk space size gb models delete replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>It depends on your graphics card: bigger cards get bigger brains. <strong>Get me ready</strong> shows the total and your free disk before anything downloads. On a 16 GB card the full set is about 105 GB, mostly pictures and clips (see ${go('faq-setup-size', 'Why is my set so big?')}); smaller cards get smaller sets. Every model shows its size before you confirm, and <strong>On this replicant matrix</strong> (Get models) lists what you have so you can delete what you don't use.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-asleep', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why is Bob asleep?', words: 'faq asleep sleeping dozing zz eyes closed wake up',
    html: `<p>After 10 quiet minutes I doze off in my picture. Move the mouse over my window, type or send a message and I'm awake. Dozing is only for show and doesn't change my brains. If <strong>Free memory when Bob is idle</strong> is on, my brain may have been let go separately, and then the first reply takes a few seconds longer. Now running shows which one it is. ${go('habits', 'More about my little habits')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-stop-moving', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I stop Bob and the cat moving?', words: 'faq stop moving animations still motion distracting cat antics off',
    html: `<p>Yes. Set <strong>Animations</strong> on the Style page to Off and we both keep still. Follow Windows does it whenever Windows' animation effects are off. My mouth still moves when I talk, so you can tell I'm talking.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-no-gpu', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Do I need a graphics card?', words: 'faq graphics card gpu nvidia needed processor only',
    html: `<p>No. I can chat, plan, read pictures, hear you and talk with no graphics card at all, just slower. An NVIDIA card makes me much faster and lets me paint and make clips. Graphics built into the processor, like a handheld's, help too: I run my brains on them, and paint there too, just slowly.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-built-in-graphics', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Does Bob use built-in graphics, like a handheld\'s?', words: 'faq built-in integrated graphics igpu set aside carve-out auto legion space armoury crate installed amd radeon 780m 890m z1 extreme legion go rog ally steam deck intel arc iris shared memory handheld laptop vulkan pictures paint edit video clips slow replicant matrix pc computer mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Yes. If your replicant matrix has no NVIDIA card but its processor has good graphics built in (AMD's in Ryzen laptop and handheld chips like the Z1 Extreme, or Intel Arc and Iris Xe), I spot them and show them in Get models as built-in graphics, with the memory they share with the rest of this replicant matrix. I read how much memory has been set aside for graphics (Auto, 4 GB or 8 GB in your BIOS or the maker's app, such as Legion Space or Armoury Crate) and count that plus about half of what's left as graphics memory, keeping around 4 GB for Windows itself; if a brain still wouldn't fit, I shorten its chat memory first and say so plainly rather than load it and risk a crash. Then I suggest the <strong>Built-in graphics</strong> starter set. My brain engine runs on them through Vulkan, which is faster than the processor alone.</p><p>That set has a small painter and a small editor too, so I can make and change pictures there. My rough guess is a few minutes a picture (nobody has timed it on a real handheld yet), so turn on <strong>Match picture settings to my graphics card</strong> (in ${go('settings-speed', 'Settings: Speed &amp; memory')}) and I make them at 768 px, which is quicker. Clips are very slow on built-in graphics, so the clip model is left unticked; tick it if you want short clips anyway. A desktop Ryzen's small built-in graphics are slower than its processor, so there I stay on the processor. If this replicant matrix also has a separate AMD or Intel card, I don't use either yet, and run on the processor.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-undo', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Bob changed a file. Can I undo it?', words: 'faq undo file recycle bin restore',
    html: `<p>Yes, for the Files Hand: say "undo that" or use <strong>Undo</strong> in Settings, Hands, Recent file jobs. Anything I removed is in the Recycle Bin (while <em>Recycle Bin only</em> is on). Every action is listed in my logbook (inn\\logbook.md).</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-checkins-games', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Will check-ins get in the way of my games?', words: 'faq check-ins check in games gaming slow fps brain load graphics card',
    html: `<p>No. The built-in checks don't use a brain at all. Your own checks never run in the Gaming session; they wait until it ends. Outside Gaming they use my quick brain on the processor, only while I'm idle, and it's let go again afterwards. See ${go('checkins', 'Check-ins')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-checkins-quiet', group: 'FAQ', title: 'My check-in time came and Bob said nothing. Is it working?', words: 'faq check-ins quiet nothing said silent no message working last check-in',
    html: `<p>Probably, and that's the point: I only speak up when something needs you. Settings, Show advanced settings, 🔔 Check-ins shows <strong>Last check-in</strong> with its time and "All fine.", and my logbook has a line for every round. If I was closed at that time, I do it when I'm next open. <strong>Check in now</strong> does one on the spot.</p>` },

  { id: 'faq-stop', group: 'FAQ', title: 'How do I stop Bob right now?', words: 'faq stop now kill cancel emergency',
    html: `<p><strong>Stop</strong> next to Send ends the current reply. <strong>✋ Hands</strong> at the top of the chat stops every Hand at once. For the Apps Hand, moving the mouse or pressing Esc stops me too. Tasks lets you cancel anything waiting.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-commercial', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I sell what Bob makes?', words: 'faq commercial sell licence license use',
    html: `<p>Everything in the starter sets allows commercial use. A few optional models don't, and I mark those and show each licence before you download.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-laptop', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can I use Bob on my laptop?', words: 'faq laptop small card 6 gb phone tailscale replicant matrix pc computer device machine',
    html: `<p>Yes. I read the laptop's graphics card and suggest the 6 GB starter set, with a light brain that leaves room for longer chats. To reach me from your phone or another Bob, turn on <strong>Let my other replicant matrices in</strong> in ${go('settings-bobnet', 'Settings: Bob-Net')}; it's off unless you turn it on.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-where', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Where does Bob keep my things?', words: 'faq where files folder localappdata inn models config backup',
    html: `<p>In my folder unless you chose another one: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Bob on Windows, ~/Library/Application Support/Bob/home on a Mac, ~/.local/share/bob on Linux (Settings shows the exact one). It holds models, engines, my notes in <em>inn</em> (chats, topics, pictures, logbook) and config.json. Updating me never touches that folder, so you can back it up or move it. Pictures, clips, songs and sounds can go somewhere else: see ${go('settings-save', 'Where Bob saves things')}.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-save-elsewhere', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob save my pictures and songs somewhere else?', words: 'faq save folder other drive pictures music songs change where output',
    html: `<p>Yes. Settings, <strong>Where Bob saves things</strong>, then <strong>Browse…</strong> and pick the folder. New pictures, clips, songs and sounds go there from then on. The ones I made before stay put and still show in your chats. Changed your mind? <strong>Use Bob's own folder</strong>.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-own-tools', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob make his own tools?', words: 'faq make own tools build tool research self-made hands',
    html: `<p>Yes, if you switch on ${go('hands-maker', 'Make his own tools')} in Settings, Show advanced settings, Hands. Each new tool is tested in a sandbox, read by my reviewer and shown to you on one card before it's added. They're listed under <strong>Tools Bob made</strong>.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-unreal-blender', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Can Bob work in Unreal Engine or Blender?', words: 'faq unreal engine ue5 blender mcp connected apps level scene drive editor 3d',
    html: `<p>Yes, through their MCP add-ons. Install the add-on in the app, switch on ${go('hands-mcp', 'Connected apps (MCP)')} in Settings, Show advanced settings, Hands, add the app, press <strong>Check</strong> and tick <strong>On</strong>. Then ask: "in Blender, add a cube at the origin". Every step shows a card first.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-song-where', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Where did my song go?', words: 'faq song music lost where gone play again find sound file replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>It's in the chat as a song player, and in Made by Bob (left menu) under Sounds. Each one is a .wav file: press <strong>Show in folder</strong> on its player to see it on your replicant matrix. Or just ask me, "play the last song", and I'll call it up.</p>` },

  // ---------------- Troubleshooting ----------------
  { id: 'trouble', group: 'Troubleshooting', title: 'When something goes wrong', words: 'problem fix error slow stuck blocked smart app control parsec copy paste kokoro one more step download update failed out of graphics memory hear internet replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Don't panic. (Towel optional.) Find what you see on the left.</p>
<table><tr><th>What you see</th><th>What to try</th></tr>
<tr><td>Windows (Smart App Control) blocks me</td><td>Start me from the Start menu shortcut. Bob.exe is Electron's own program, only renamed, which Windows usually trusts. Leave Smart App Control on; check the Releases page notes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Windows protected your replicant matrix" on the zip</td><td>Right-click the zip, Properties, tick <strong>Unblock</strong>, OK, then unzip again. Or click <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copy and paste don't work over Parsec or remote desktop</td><td>Use the <strong>Copy</strong> buttons on messages, code, links and file cards.</td></tr>
<tr><td>I say I can't look things up</td><td>Click <strong>🌐 Web search</strong> next to Effort (or turn on <strong>Let Bob search the web</strong> in Settings).</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Downloaded. One more step"</td><td>The model is here but its engine isn't. Press the <strong>Get …</strong> button right there (for Kokoro, about 100 MB).</td></tr>
<tr><td>A download stopped or failed</td><td>Open <strong>Downloads</strong> and press <strong>Resume</strong>. It carries on from where it stopped.</td></tr>
<tr><td>An update failed</td><td>The update window names the step that failed. I keep the previous version, so I still open. Try again from Settings, Check for updates.</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Out of graphics memory", or everything is slow</td><td>Close games or other graphics-heavy apps, use the Art or Gaming session, or pick a model marked <span class="tag rec">★ Recommended</span> in Equip Bob. Run <strong>Check this replicant matrix</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The first reply takes a while</td><td>I'm loading my brain after being idle (a few seconds; big brains longer).</td></tr>
<tr><td>A job says "Waiting · Gaming is on"</td><td>It needs the graphics card. Switch the session to Normal to run it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>I can't hear you</td><td>Fill the 👂 Hearing you slot (Gemma 4 E4B does it).</td></tr>
<tr><td>I can't paint or edit</td><td>Fill the 🎨 Painter or ✂ Picture editor slot, and get the picture engine when I offer it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A Hand won't do anything</td><td>Check it's on in Settings, Show advanced settings, Hands, and that ✋ Hands at the top of the chat isn't <em>Stopped</em>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A model you want isn't listed in a slot</td><td>That slot only lists models that can do its job. Models that don't suit this replicant matrix are hidden: turn on <strong>Show models that don't suit this replicant matrix</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>None of the above, or I won't even answer</td><td>Settings, <strong>This replicant matrix and updates</strong>, <strong>Save a help bundle</strong>: one zip with my logs, errors, last chats and this replicant matrix's facts, written for a helper (a person or an AI agent) to read. It works even when my brain doesn't. See ${go('help-bundle', 'Save a help bundle')}.</td></tr></table>
<p>Still stuck? Ask me in the chat. Describing the problem to someone helps, even when that someone is software. And if that someone is me and I'm the problem, save a help bundle and take it to someone else.</p>` },

  { id: 'help-bundle', group: 'Troubleshooting', title: 'Save a help bundle', words: 'help bundle debug logs zip handoff hand off agent diagnose diagnostic report send support issue github errors chats readout what is wrong broken not starting engine brain scrubbed private passcode key name replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine phone android',
    html: `<p>When I'm not working right and neither of us can see why, the <strong>Save a help bundle</strong> button (Settings, <strong>This replicant matrix and updates</strong>) packs everything a helper needs into one zip. The helper can be a person, or an AI agent such as Claude or ChatGPT: the first file in the zip, <strong>HANDOFF.md</strong>, is written for an agent to read first. It says what I am, points at my public code (github.com/MouseWurX/Bob) and the exact version, and lists where to look and what to try, in order. You hand the zip over; I never send it anywhere myself.</p>
<p><strong>What's in it</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>HANDOFF.md</strong>: the summary for the helper. This replicant matrix (processor, memory, graphics card, free disk), my version and engine, whether each engine starts, which brain is in each slot, what was loaded, unfinished or failed jobs, the last errors, what's set right now, and what to read next.</li>
<li><strong>bob/errors.md</strong>: every line in my logbook where something went wrong or I recovered from something.</li>
<li><strong>bob/logbook.md</strong>: the last 3,000 lines of my logbook (everything I did, newest at the bottom).</li>
<li><strong>bob/settings.json</strong> and <strong>bob/facts.json</strong>: my settings and the same facts as data, for a helper who likes to check things precisely.</li>
<li><strong>chats/</strong>: my last three chats, 40 messages each, as a plain readout of who said what.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Two ways it gets written.</strong> If my chat brain is already loaded and idle, I read the facts myself and add a short note under <em>What Bob himself thinks</em>: what I think is wrong and what to try first. If my brain isn't there (the engine won't start, nothing is downloaded, I'm busy, or we're in a Gaming session), the bundle is put together without any brain at all, and HANDOFF.md tells the helper to assume the worst and start with the engines. I never load a brain for this, so the button works on a broken me too. That is rather the point.</p>
<p><strong>What's taken out</strong> before anything is written: your name and user name, the machine's name, your home folder, every passcode, API key and bot token, and Tailscale addresses. The settings file keeps the names of its switches and drops any value that looks like a secret. Read the zip yourself before you share it if you like; it's plain text. If something personal slipped through, that's a bug: please tell me at github.com/MouseWurX/Bob/issues.</p>
<p><strong>Where it goes.</strong> I ask you to pick a folder, then show the zip there. On a Linux desktop with no folder picker (zenity or kdialog) it goes in <strong>Made by Bob/help-bundles</strong>. From my phone app, or when you open me from another device, the zip downloads to that device instead.</p>
<p><strong>Where to take it.</strong> Paste HANDOFF.md, or the whole zip, to the agent that's helping you. Or open an issue at github.com/MouseWurX/Bob/issues and attach the zip; say what you saw and what you expected. See ${go('faq-get-help', 'How do I get help with my Bob?')}.</p>` },

  { id: 'setup-offer', group: 'The chat page', title: 'When Bob offers to set something up', words: 'set it up offer missing not set up cannot can\'t video clip picture paint music sound voice hearing install download engine model switch it on setting off web search hands use it slot card carry on error replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Ask me for something I can't do yet, a clip say, and you don't get an error. You get one line from me and a card under the chat:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Set it up</strong>: the programs and the model I'd need, each with its size, the total and your free disk. One press downloads them, sets them up and puts the model in its slot. Your request waits in Tasks, and I carry on by myself as soon as it's all in. No need to ask twice.</li>
<li><strong>Use it</strong>: the model is already here, just not in its slot. One press, and I carry on.</li>
<li><strong>Switch it on</strong>: when a setting is in the way, like <strong>Let Bob search the web</strong> or a Hand that's off, the card names that setting, says what it lets me do and switches just that one on. You can switch it off again where the card says. A Hand stopped with the ✋ Hands switch on the chat page stays stopped: I'll ask you to press that switch yourself.</li>
<li><strong>Not now</strong>: nothing changes and nothing downloads.</li></ul>
<p>I pick what suits your graphics card, the same picks as ${go('start', 'Get me ready')}. If something is bigger than your card I say it will be slow. If there's not enough disk, the card says how much to free up and the button waits. On the phone app the card looks the same, and the downloads happen on the replicant matrix that's doing the job.</p>
<p>It works for pictures, picture edits, clips, music, sound effects, my voice, hearing you, <strong>Let Bob search the web</strong> (its card says what leaves your replicant matrix: only the search words and page addresses), the workshop and every Hand. If there's nothing that runs on your replicant matrix (whisper.cpp has no Mac build, for one), I say so plainly instead.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-setup-size', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why is my set so big?', words: 'faq size big large gb disk space set starter why download how much breakdown replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Mostly pictures and clips. On a 16 GB card the full set is about 105 GB on disk: the chat and coding brain is about 23 GB, the video model 21 GB, the picture model 20 GB, the picture editor 19 GB and the office brain 15 GB. Picture and video models carry their own text readers, which is a good chunk of their size.</p>
<p>That's disk, not memory in use. I load one big model for a job and let it go after, so your graphics card and RAM stay free otherwise. If disk is tight, pick the next Setup down (smaller versions of the same jobs) or untick the picture editor. Anything already on your replicant matrix isn't downloaded again.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-python', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Do I need to install Python?', words: 'faq python install python.org 3.12 kokoro chatterbox music audio tools needs smart app control replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>No. My voice (Kokoro, Chatterbox) and the audio tools use Python 3.12 or 3.11. If your replicant matrix already has one, I use it. If not, I get my own: on Windows the official Python package signed by the Python Software Foundation (about 15 MB, checked against its checksum), on a Mac or Linux a standalone build that uv fetches and checks. It lives in my folder, in a folder called python, and nothing is installed for the rest of your computer.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-settings-moved', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Where did Engines & speed and Connections go?', words: 'faq where moved settings layout engines speed connections bob-net online services other apps find setting missing replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>In 1.5.98 I sorted my settings so each panel holds one kind of thing. Nothing was renamed or switched, only moved:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Engines &amp; speed</strong> is now two panels: ${go('settings-speed', 'Speed &amp; memory')} (freeing memory, picture speed, Gaming, Remember only what fits, the speed test) and ${go('settings-engines', 'Engines')} (my engine and its downloads; the fine-tuning is under More).</li>
<li><strong>Connections</strong> is now three: ${go('settings-bobnet', 'Bob-Net')} (your phone and your other Bobs: the door, the Address and passcode, and what they share), ${go('settings-online', 'Online services')} (Cloud helpers and Telegram) and ${go('settings-apps', 'Other apps')} (ComfyUI).</li>
<li><strong>Let the reviewer sit beside Bob's main brain</strong>, <strong>Keep chatting while Bob is busy</strong> and <strong>Give Bob all his tools at once</strong> are in ${go('settings-brains', 'Brains')}.</li>
<li><strong>Pick up where Bob left off after a restart</strong> is on the everyday page, in How Bob works. <strong>Check for updates when Bob starts</strong> is in This replicant matrix and updates.</li></ul>
<p>Can't find one? Type a word from it into <strong>Search settings</strong>.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-card-busy', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why does Bob say the graphics card is busy with another program?', words: 'faq graphics card busy another program gpu vram memory full taken game took too long to load picture brain nvidia replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>When a brain or a picture takes too long to load, I take one quick look at the graphics card. If it's nearly full and the memory isn't mine (a game, another AI program, a video editor), I say so once, with the program's name when the card tells me, and I stop trying, so you don't wait minutes for a load that can't work.</p>
<p>Jobs waiting in line get the same answer straight away instead of each one waiting for its own load. Before the next job that needs the card I look again, and once it's free I carry on as normal. Chats on my small brain on the processor (Gaming) keep going the whole time.</p>
<p>To fix it, close that program, or send your message again once it's done.</p>` },
  { id: 'faq-firewall', group: 'FAQ', title: 'Why can\'t my phone or other Bob reach this one?', words: 'faq firewall reach connect blocked tailscale phone other bob bob-net port 7777 windows defender uac allow cancel restart replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine',
    html: `<p>Three things have to line up, and I check them for you in Settings, Show advanced settings, Bob-Net, <strong>Let my other replicant matrices in</strong>:</p>
<ol><li><strong>The switch is on.</strong> It works the moment you turn it on; you don't need to restart me.</li>
<li><strong>Tailscale is on, here and there,</strong> signed in to the same account. If it isn't yet, the setting says so, and I notice within a minute when it connects.</li>
<li><strong>The firewall lets Tailscale through.</strong> On Windows, press <strong>Let Tailscale through the firewall</strong>. Windows asks you once; the rule only lets in Tailscale addresses on my port, nothing else on your network. It also switches off any rule that blocks Bob\'s own program (Windows makes one if Cancel was clicked on its earlier question). <strong>Remove the firewall rule</strong> switches those back on. On Linux with ufw or firewalld on, run the line I show. On a Mac, choose Allow if macOS asks about me.</li></ol>
<p>Still stuck? The phone app's message says which Bob it couldn't reach. Check that one's settings first. It's nearly always the firewall, the one door I can't open without you.</p>` },
  { id: 'glossary', group: 'Troubleshooting', title: 'Glossary', words: 'words meaning bestiary characters page met enemies character screen paper doll games twenty questions word ladder five letters story dice shelf help bundle handoff mermaid svg ffmpeg waiting hourglass ⏳ line replicant matrix pc computer laptop mac device machine mcp connected app tip bubble github release checksum sha256 model brain gpu vram ram graphics memory gguf engine commercial session hands context effort moot copy token processor cpu ollama llama.cpp thinking borrowed lend memory bank model family',
    html: `<p>The words I use, in plain English. I'll try not to say "tensor" without warning you.</p>
<dl>
<dt>Help bundle</dt><dd>One zip from <strong>Save a help bundle</strong> (Settings, This replicant matrix and updates): my logs, errors, last chats, settings without their secrets and this replicant matrix's facts, with a HANDOFF.md on top. I never send it anywhere. See ${go('help-bundle', 'Save a help bundle')}.</dd>
<dt>Handoff file (HANDOFF.md)</dt><dd>The first file in a help bundle, written for whoever helps (a person or an AI agent): what I am, my exact version and public code, what's wrong as far as I can tell, and what to try first.</dd>
<dt>Tray</dt><dd>The row of little icons by the clock on Windows (some hide under the <strong>^</strong> arrow), the menu bar on a Mac, or your desktop's tray on Linux. With <strong>Keep Bob in the tray</strong> on, I live there when my window is closed.</dd>
<dt>Taskbar icon</dt><dd>The little picture on my button along the bottom of the screen (the Dock on a Mac). With <strong>Use Bob's look as his icon</strong> on, it's my current look, redrawn each time you save a new one. A button you pinned keeps the shortcut's icon: that's Windows, not me.</dd>
<dt>GitHub release</dt><dd>A finished version of me published on github.com/MouseWurX/Bob, with a file for each kind of computer. <strong>Update Bob</strong> uses the one marked Latest.</dd>
<dt>Checksum (.sha256, SHA-256)</dt><dd>A file's fingerprint: a long number worked out from its contents. If even one byte changes, so does the number, so matching it proves the file is exactly the one on my GitHub release page. You can also paste a file's SHA-256 into virustotal.com to see what many antiviruses think of it, without uploading it.</dd>
<dt>Plan card</dt><dd>The card I show before a big job when <strong>Show Bob's plan before big jobs</strong> is on: every step in order, with any OKs the job needs ticked on it. You press Go ahead, Change something or Not now (Bob drops this job).</dd>
<dt>Model family</dt><dd>Models made by the same team and built the same way, like Qwen or Gemma. One family comes in many sizes, from phone-sized to big. In the Brain catalogue each family has its own header you open and close.</dd>
<dt>Mermaid</dt><dd>A free drawing kit that turns a few lines of text into a flowchart or other diagram. It comes with me and works offline. See ${go('diagrams', 'Flowcharts and diagrams')}.</dd>
<dt>SVG</dt><dd>A picture made of shapes instead of dots, so it stays sharp at any size. Good for diagrams you'll print or put in a document.</dd>
<dt>FFmpeg</dt><dd>A free converter for sound, video and pictures. I use it for <strong>Save as</strong> (MP3, WebM, JPG...) and the Office Hand's converting.</dd>
<dt>Brain</dt><dd>A model that thinks and chats. Bigger brains are usually smarter but slower and need more memory.</dd>
<dt>Memory bank</dt><dd>The memories that belong to one project, kept apart from the others. With <strong>Remember only what fits</strong> on, I read only the bank of the project we're talking about, plus your general memories.</dd>
<dt>ARM board</dt><dd>A small computer built around an ARM chip, like a Raspberry Pi 5. I run there from the <strong>arm64</strong> AppImage, on the processor, with small brains.</dd>
<dt>Built-in graphics</dt><dd>Graphics that are part of the processor and share this replicant matrix's memory, as in handhelds and thin laptops. Bob runs his brains on them, and paints there too, slowly.</dd>
<dt>Set aside for graphics</dt><dd>Memory a handheld or laptop keeps for its built-in graphics; Windows can't use it for anything else. Auto (the usual setting) keeps little back and shares the rest as needed.</dd>
<dt>Link hand-off</dt><dd>With the Quick brain on the processor, a message with a link goes to the main Brain instead, which reads web pages better. Not in Gaming.</dd>
<dt>⏳ line</dt><dd>Bob is waiting on something, not on you. Cards are the only things that need your answer.</dd>
<dt>Replicant matrix</dt><dd>The computer I run on, whatever kind it is: a desktop PC, a Mac, a Linux box, a laptop, a handheld or a phone. It's still your computer; replicant matrix is just my name for my home. Settings shows its specs next to the name (processor, graphics card, memory), so you always know which machine I mean. On Bob-Net, each of your Bobs lives on its own.</dd>
<dt>Online services</dt><dd>The settings panel for things that send something over the internet: Cloud helpers and Telegram. All off until you turn them on.</dd>
<dt>Game master</dt><dd>Me, when I run an Adventure: I set the scenes, play everyone who isn't at the table, and say what the dice mean. Some games call it a referee.</dd>
<dt>Hearts</dt><dd>A character's health in an Adventure. A miss can cost one; at zero hearts you choose Go out in style or Wake up captured. Plot armour decides how often the dice are allowed to take one.</dd>
<dt>Bestiary</dt><dd>The 📖 Characters page in an Adventure: your saved heroes (You), the table (Party), everyone the game master has named (Met, drawn as they appear) and the usual enemies with their hearts (Enemies).</dd>
<dt>Character screen</dt><dd>The paper-doll page where a look is built from parts: body, build, hair, skin, outfit, armour, weapon, shield. On Style it dresses me (Make your own look); on the Adventure start card it dresses your character, who can be anyone.</dd>
<dt>Plot armour</dt><dd>How safe a character is from bad dice in an Adventure: None, Some, Lots or Legendary. Asked for every character at the start of every game.</dd>
<dt>Action card</dt><dd>One of your choices on your turn in an Adventure (Attack, Talk, Magic, Use item and so on). Some open a second card with the details. Bobs don't get cards; they just say what they do.</dd>
<dt>Auto-roll</dt><dd>In an Adventure the app rolls the dice for every action: a d20 plus one stat. 15 or more is a hit, 10 to 14 a hit at a cost, under 10 a miss.</dd>
<dt>Scene</dt><dd>One stretch of an Adventure in one place: a fight, a conversation, somewhere worth poking at, or a choice. Travel between scenes is one montage line.</dd>
<dt>Campaign</dt><dd>An Adventure saved between sittings, under Continue a campaign on the start card.</dd>
<dt>Twenty questions</dt><dd>A game on my 🧩 Games page: one of us thinks of something, the other asks up to twenty yes-or-no questions. The app holds the secret when it's mine.</dd>
<dt>Word ladder</dt><dd>A game on my 🧩 Games page: climb from one four-letter word to another, changing one letter a step, every rung a real word.</dd>
<dt>Five letters</dt><dd>A game on my 🧩 Games page: guess a five-letter word in six tries; each try colours the letters green (right spot), yellow (in the word elsewhere) or grey (not in it).</dd>
<dt>Story dice</dt><dd>A game on my 🧩 Games page: six picture dice come up and we tell a story a line each until every die is in it.</dd>
<dt>Shelf</dt><dd>Where a Hangman word comes from: Animals, Food and drink, Around the house, Out of doors, Jobs people do or Things that move. The shelf is the one free clue.</dd>
<dt>Bob-Net</dt><dd>Your Bobs on your own replicant matrices, sharing brains and jobs over Tailscale. Set it up in Settings, Show advanced settings, <strong>Bob-Net</strong>.</dd>
<dt>Pairing</dt><dd>Joining two of your Bobs with a button instead of typing. One presses <strong>Pair</strong>, the other shows a 4-digit number and you press <strong>Yes, pair us</strong>; they swap passcodes over Tailscale. See ${go('faq-pair', 'How do I join my Bobs together?')}.</dd>
<dt>Sent to another Bob</dt><dd>A whole job (a picture, a song, research…) done by another of your Bobs who can do it, when I can't or would have to wait (📡 Sent to… in the chat). The reply, OK cards and files come back to me.</dd>
<dt>Borrowed brain</dt><dd>When another of your Bobs thinks a quick reply for me over Tailscale (📡 in the chat). I still run the tools.</dd>
<dt>Introduced</dt><dd>Paired through a third Bob of yours that has <strong>Pass my Bob-Net along</strong> on: it tells two of your Bobs each other's name and address, and they pair with no card. See ${go('faq-introduce', 'Can my Bobs introduce each other?')}.</dd>
<dt>Tip (ⓘ)</dt><dd>A small round <strong>i</strong> next to a setting or a heading. Point at it, Tab to it, or tap it and a bubble explains that thing. Press and hold a button on a touch screen to see its tip.</dd>
<dt>Brain swap</dt><dd>Putting one brain away and loading another, usually 10 to 20 seconds.</dd>
<dt>Thinking first</dt><dd>What a brain works through quietly before it answers. Medium effort and up think first; Low doesn't, and with Qwen3.6 coding jobs always do. 💭 Bob's thinking shows it live while that window is open, and none of it is saved.</dd>
<dt>Card (in the chat)</dt><dd>A box in the chat that shows a file, a picture, or something I want your OK for.</dd>
<dt>Cloud helper</dt><dd>A paid online AI I can hand one job to, with your own key. Off until you switch it on; asks every time.</dd>
<dt>Rented brain</dt><dd>A big brain hired from an online service for one job, running as me with my tools here. Off until you set the limits, switch a service on and press Hire it.</dd>
<dt>Pay per answer</dt><dd>A service that charges by the words in and out, with nothing to shut down (OpenRouter, Together AI, Lambda).</dd>
<dt>Rent by the minute</dt><dd>A graphics card hired by the hour that I start for a job and stop after; it carries a timer that stops it on its own.</dd>
<dt>Spending cap</dt><dd>The most I may spend on rented brains in a month, or on one job. Starts at 0, which means nothing can be hired.</dd>
<dt>Key</dt><dd>The password-like code a service gives you so I can use your account. You set it as an environment variable; I read it when a job starts and never store it.</dd>
<dt>Commercial use</dt><dd>Using what you make for money. All starter set models allow it; I mark the few that don't.</dd>
<dt>Song player</dt><dd>The card a song or sound shows up as in the chat: play, the bars to jump around in, the time, and its lyrics.</dd>
<dt>Save folder</dt><dd>Where I put the pictures, clips, songs and sounds I make. My own folder unless you pick another in Settings.</dd>
<dt>Context</dt><dd>How much I keep in mind at once, counted in tokens (256K is about 262,000 tokens, roughly 195,000 words). More context reads longer files but uses more memory. Each brain was made for a certain most; I never ask a brain for more than that.</dd>
<dt>Copy (of Bob)</dt><dd>A helper version of me for one kind of job, with its own Bob-pun name.</dd>
<dt>Draft brain</dt><dd>A small brain that guesses ahead for a big one (speculative decoding).</dd>
<dt>Defender</dt><dd>Microsoft Defender, the antivirus built into Windows. I can explain its alerts and start its scans; I never switch it off.</dd>
<dt>Effort</dt><dd>How hard I work on each message: Low, Medium, High or Extreme.</dd>
<dt>Convert</dt><dd>Turning a file into another kind (Word into PDF, a WAV into an MP3). The original stays as it is.</dd>
<dt>Embedding model</dt><dd>A tiny model that turns a sentence into numbers, so I can find past Topics that mean the same thing even with different words. Mine is Nomic Embed Text, in the 🗂 Memory (finding past chats) slot.</dd>
<dt>Engine</dt><dd>The program that runs a kind of model, such as llama.cpp for brains. A basic llama.cpp comes with me; the others download with <strong>Get Bob ready</strong> or with the first model that needs them.</dd>
<dt>Set it up card</dt><dd>The card I show in the chat when you ask for something I can't do yet: what's missing, the size, and one button that gets it, puts it in its slot and carries on with your request. When a setting is what's in the way, the button switches that setting on instead. See ${go('setup-offer', 'When Bob offers to set something up')}.</dd>
<dt>Slot</dt><dd>The job a model does for me, like Painter, Video or Voice. You see them on Equip Bob. A model only works once it's in its slot; I fill the slot myself when a download I set up finishes.</dd>
<dt>Equip Bob</dt><dd>The page where you choose which model does each job.</dd>
<dt>Chest</dt><dd>The box under me on Equip Bob: everything I've made as little pictures with names, and the folder new things go to.</dd>
<dt>Home (Bob's home folder)</dt><dd>The one folder with my models, programs, notes and settings, chosen when I was installed or first opened. Settings, Bob's home folder shows and moves it. Never a temporary folder.</dd>
<dt>Thumbnail</dt><dd>A small copy of a picture or clip's first moment, made once and kept, so lists of them open fast.</dd>
<dt>Brain catalogue</dt><dd>My full list of models, sorted by job, with what each one needs and where it comes from.</dd>
<dt>Naughty list</dt><dd>Uncensored remixes of Qwen and Gemma brains made by other people, with the refusals cut out. Marked with a red devil. Hidden until you turn on Show the naughty list; never ★ Recommended.</dd>
<dt>Quiet pick</dt><dd>On the naughty list, a red devil on its own where ★ Recommended would be: the uncensored brain that does that job well on your replicant matrix.</dd>
<dt>Abliterated (heretic)</dt><dd>A brain with its "no" taken out. Heretic is the tool, abliterated is the result. It answers without refusing and can be a little rougher than the original.</dd>
<dt>Your own brain</dt><dd>A brain file (.gguf) you added to my list yourself, under ➕ Your own brains. Runs from where it is; never recommended over the ones I know.</dd>
<dt>Community build</dt><dd>A model made by someone other than its maker: a remix, a fine-tune or a repack. The catalogue says who made it and from what.</dd>
<dt>Active (model)</dt><dd>A model that shows up in the slots and brain menus. The rest wait in the Brain catalogue.</dd>
<dt>Picture reader</dt><dd>The small extra file (an "mmproj") that lets a brain see pictures. I download it with the brain when it has one.</dd>
<dt>Graphics card, graphics memory</dt><dd>The part of your replicant matrix that makes AI fast. Its memory (6, 8, 16 GB) decides which models fit. Also called GPU and VRAM. If another program has filled it, I tell you which one and stop trying until it's free.</dd>
<dt>How-to</dt><dd>Steps I kept from a job that worked, saved by your click on a card, so I can do it the same way next time. Plain text: it can't run anything or say yes for you. Off until you turn on Let Bob learn how-tos.</dd>
<dt>Hands</dt><dd>Me doing things on your replicant matrix (files, programs, schedules, apps). Off until you turn them on.</dd>
<dt>MCP (Model Context Protocol)</dt><dd>A common plug that lets an app offer its own tools to a helper like me. An app's MCP add-on (or MCP server) is the bit that does the offering. See the Connected apps (MCP) Hand.</dd>
<dt>Connected app</dt><dd>An app you added under Connected apps so I can use the tools its MCP add-on offers, like Unreal Engine or Blender.</dd>
<dt>Sandbox</dt><dd>A walled-off space where a tool I made runs: it can only touch its own folder and the folders on its card, and can't start other programs.</dd>
<dt>Hard limit</dt><dd>A safety rule for Hands that holds even when I work without asking. On to start with.</dd>
<dt>Housekeeping</dt><dd>Me tidying old chats and files while I'm idle. Off until you turn it on.</dd>
<dt>Check-in</dt><dd>A quick round I do on a schedule you pick, looking at downloads, tasks, schedules, disk space and your own checks. I only tell you when something needs you. Off until you turn it on.</dd>
<dt>Inn</dt><dd>My notes folder: chats, topics, pictures, logbook.</dd>
<dt>LibreOffice</dt><dd>A free office suite that reads and saves Word, Excel and PowerPoint files. I use it, hidden, to convert, read, make and change documents.</dd>
<dt>llama.cpp</dt><dd>My own engine for brains. Needs nothing else installed.</dd>
<dt>Little habits</dt><dd>The small things my picture does on its own: blinking, glancing, sipping coffee, dozing off, reacting to your messages. Scripted, no brain needed.</dd>
<dt>Logbook</dt><dd>The file (inn\\logbook.md) where I write down every action I take.</dd>
<dt>Metadata (hidden details)</dt><dd>Information tucked inside a file: GPS location and camera in a photo, author and company in a document. I can find it and make clean copies.</dd>
<dt>Model</dt><dd>The file that does one kind of AI job: a brain, a painter, a voice.</dd>
<dt>Moot</dt><dd>A meeting of my copies at High and Extreme effort: one drafts, the reviewer checks.</dd>
<dt>Now running</dt><dd>The right-hand panel: tasks, downloads, your other Bobs on Bob-Net and memory.</dd>
<dt>OSINT</dt><dd>Open-source intelligence: research using only public sources. I use it to check whether a website, link or sender is genuine, never to look into private people.</dd>
<dt>Op-sec</dt><dd>Operational security: habits that stop strangers piecing together your life from small public bits.</dd>
<dt>Ollama</dt><dd>A separate program that can run brains. You don't need it; I can use it if you already have it.</dd>
<dt>Phishing</dt><dd>A fake email, text or page that tries to get a password, a payment or a download out of you.</dd>
<dt>Processor (CPU)</dt><dd>Your replicant matrix's main chip. I can run small brains on it to leave the graphics card free, more slowly.</dd>
<dt>Quarantine</dt><dd>My reviewer stopping me from acting after I go off track, only with your OK.</dd>
<dt>RAM</dt><dd>Your replicant matrix's main memory. A brain too big for the graphics card can keep the rest here. It works, but more slowly.</dd>
<dt>RDAP</dt><dd>The official public record of who sold a website's name and when it was bought (it replaced WHOIS).</dd>
<dt>Recipe</dt><dd>A saved command for the Run Hand. Not the same as a how-to.</dd>
<dt>Reference</dt><dd>A picture on the References shelf that I paint from to keep a look the same.</dd>
<dt>Search operators</dt><dd>Little commands in a search, like "exact phrase", site:, filetype: and -word, that find the right pages faster.</dd>
<dt>Session</dt><dd>What the graphics card is kept for: Normal, Art, Coding, Office, Gaming.</dd>
<dt>SPF, DKIM, DMARC</dt><dd>Checks that show whether an email really came from the address it claims. A fail is a strong sign of a fake.</dd>
<dt>Squeezed version (Q4, Q3)</dt><dd>A smaller copy of a model (GGUF) that fits small cards, with a little less detail. The Q number says how tightly it's packed: Q4 (4 bits per weight) keeps nearly all of a brain's smarts and is what I use on most cards. Q3 (3 bits) is smaller and a little less sharp, so I only use it on cards of 8 GB or less, when the Q4 file wouldn't run well.</dd>
<dt>Starter set</dt><dd>A ready-made set of models for your kind of graphics card.</dd>
<dt>The Idle Process</dt><dd>My little virtual workspace: a coffee machine, a reading chair, a wall of books, and a cat who ignores me. Named after what your replicant matrix does when nobody's using it.</dd>
<dt>Token</dt><dd>A piece of a word. Brains read and write in tokens; context is counted in them.</dd>
<dt>Topic</dt><dd>A group of chats about one project, with a short note on where things landed.</dd></dl>` }
];
